HT_DATA.load("detail", {"LrObZ_HZZUc":{"wy":"HN's go-to short explainer for the City of London versus London, cited in many finance and UK threads.","q":{"x":"CGP Grey has a delightful video explaining this","i":40765912,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40765912,"author":"dwheeler","month":"2024-06","story":"The City of London which is not part of London (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Delightful explanation of the City inside London.","quote":"CGP Grey has a delightful video explaining this","text":"CGP Grey has a delightful video explaining this: \"The Secret City inside of London Revealed\" (2012) [VIDEO] Highly recommended."},{"id":13866182,"author":"Hagelin","month":"2017-03","story":"London’s Big Dig Reveals Layers of History","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great videos distinguishing the City of London from London.","quote":"CGP Grey's \"The (Secret) City of London\" are great videos on the topic","text":"CGP Grey's \"The (Secret) City of London\" are great videos on the topic of the City of London (vs. London) Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":8095455,"author":"M2Ys4U","month":"2014-07","story":"Online piracy will no longer be a criminal offence in the UK","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains City of London versus London.","quote":"See CGP Grey's excellent video[1] for more about the City of London vs the city called London","text":"The short answer is \"no\". The longer answer is \"well, kinda, but not really\". There have been a couple of convictions, but these were guilty pleas - people who have either been advised incorrectly by their solicitors (most probably because they were not experts in copyright law) or just really did not want to go through a trial. The copyright industry has tried to completely side-step copyright law entirely and use the offence of \"conspiracy to defraud\" with very dubious legal basis. It's been far from common, though. There have only been a handful of attempted prosecutions, and usually only because the copyright industry (via FACT[0]) has leant on the police. Most recently they've c̶o̶r̶r̶u̶p̶t̶e̶d̶ err, co-opted the City of London Police. This is the force that polices the square-mile City of London in the heart of London (See CGP Grey's excellent video[1] for more about the City of London vs the city called London). [0] The 'Federation Against Copyright Theft' - an absurd name for an absurd organisation - [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40643716,"author":"bloqs","month":"2024-06","story":"British duo arrested for SMS phishing via homemade cell tower","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the ancient financial district and its police.","quote":"Best explanation","text":"This is the police force for the ancient financial district in London called the City of London. Best explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23645830,"author":"hpoe","month":"2020-06","story":"City of London: Tax haven in the heart of Britain (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Short videos on what the City is and how it operates.","quote":"For those interested in learning more about the City of London there is a youtuber, CGP Grey","text":"For those interested in learning more about the City of London there is a youtuber, CGP Grey, who has to great 10 min vids on what it is why it exists and how it operates. Link here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20643327,"author":"rjmunro","month":"2019-08","story":"Approaching Peak Housing Dysfunction in California","stance":"rec","why":"Best explanation of City of London governance.","quote":"The best explanation I've found is","text":"The \"City Of London\" (a small part of central London) has something like this. They have a resident population of <10,000 people, but somewhere between 500,000 and 1 million people commute there for work. Companies get to nominate some of their workforce as voters for the council (called \"The Corporation Of London\"). For historical reasons (\"The City\" is much older than the UK itself), things are much more complicated than simply \"People who work there get to vote\", so I'm not sure it's a very comparable situation. The best explanation I've found is [VIDEO]"},{"id":19163562,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2019-02","story":"Amazon Pulls Out of Planned New York City Campus","stance":"rec","why":"A great video on the topic.","quote":"GCP Grey does a great video on this","text":"GCP Grey does a great video on this: [VIDEO] Jay Foreman does another great video on the very weird situation with the House of Lords: [LINK]"},{"id":19180917,"author":"mstade","month":"2019-02","story":"London's billion-pound guilds","stance":"rec","why":"Fun intro to the weirdness of the City of London.","quote":"For anyone looking for a fun little intro to the weirdness of the city and it’s history, I recommend CGP Grey’s channel","text":"I don’t know whether this is true because I never looked into it really, but a colleague of mine in the ndustry told me I could join the banker’s guild. His words were that because I lived in the city[1] at the time, and also worked for a bank in the city, I’d be eligible for membership if the bank would nominate or sponsor me as a representative. I’m hazy on the details, it was years ago, and I don’t really know whether it’s true since as mentioned I never checked it out. I kind of regret not doing so now though, since moving out of the city I’ve learned much more of it’s (weird and interesting) history and it would’ve been fun to at least try to see some of this from the inside. It probably wouldn’t have worked anyway, but would’ve been fun to try. For anyone looking for a fun little intro to the weirdness of the city and it’s history, I recommend CGP Grey’s channel and particularly his videos on the City of London[2][3]. [1]: for the benefit of people who might not be aware – “the city” is a colloquialism for the City of London, which is a small somewhat autonomous part of London, situated east of what most people (who don’t know this) probably think of when you say the city of London. [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":15202484,"author":"hencq","month":"2017-09","story":"The Equifax breach may be the worst leak of personal info ever","stance":"rec","why":"Found very interesting by the commenter.","quote":"This one I found very interesting [1].","text":"There are a lot. I thought this one was really funny [0]. This one I found very interesting [1]. Somewhat different, is the Hello Internet [2] podcast, which is by Grey and Brady Haran who you might know from the Numberphile youtube channel [3]. It's basically the 2 of them chatting about random stuff, but I find it very entertaining. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":11693064,"author":"Malic","month":"2016-05","story":"Dark money: London’s dirty secret","stance":"rec","why":"Part 1 covers history and privileges of the City.","quote":"CGP Grey did a nice two-parter video describing its history, its privileges and how to become mayor","text":"If anyone cares to know more about the City of London, CGP Grey did a nice two-parter video describing its history, its privileges and how to become mayor if you care to. The (Secret) City of London, Part 1: History [VIDEO] The (Secret) City of London, Part 2: Government [LINK]"},{"id":10707533,"author":"yoshamano","month":"2015-12","story":"The fall of Jersey: how a tax haven goes bust","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of the history of the City of London.","quote":"CGP Grey does a really good job giving an overview of the history of The City of London.","text":"CGP Grey does a really good job giving an overview of the history of The City of London. [VIDEO] Along with another video about the governing structure of The City of London. [LINK]"},{"id":10534371,"author":"Wintamute","month":"2015-11","story":"London garden bridge users to have mobile phone signals tracked","stance":"rec","why":"Informative, fun video on City of London.","quote":"Informative/fun video on the subject","text":"I find your comment somewhat alarmist, and lacking historical context. The City of London (from which the Temple end of the Garden Bridge starts) is many many hundreds of years older than the UK itself, and has always been privately owned and governed by shadowy, corporate and arcane processes. Traditionally even the Queen is not allowed to enter the City of London without the say-so of the Lord Mayor. This \"private place operating as a public space\" has been operating for centuries and stewarded the emergence of London as a leading global financial centre and until recently capital of the largest Empire the world has ever seen. I admit it's reasonable to debate the pros and cons of private ownership of public spaces in city centres, but my point is that in this case The City of London is both historically unique and important, and by many measures been a huge success for the host nation. Informative/fun video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5409778,"author":"hencq","month":"2013-03","story":"A Tale of Two Londons","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the City of London differs from greater London.","quote":"This video does an effective job in explaining how things work","text":"As an aside, the article points out how the City of London is a separate entity from greater London, but doesn't go into a lot of detail about it. This video does an effective job in explaining how things work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27475009,"author":"mcrae","month":"2021-06","story":"London and Switzerland seeking to dodge G7 global minimum tax","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the specific meaning of City of London.","quote":"","text":"Interestingly the \"City of London\" is a specific thing that has surprising meaning [0], but used here I think it is a metonym for the financial / banking sector of the British economy. [0] [LINK] & [VIDEO]"},{"id":26522652,"author":"DyslexicAtheist","month":"2021-03","story":"Police warn students to avoid Sci-Hub","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter paraphrases the video's content to show the City is weird.","quote":"","text":"> City of London is no weirder than NYC ... really, not weird? compared to what? the Vatican? It's arguably the financial (offshore) capital of the world and home to over eleven thousand people. The City of London lives inside of the city named London but the two Londons have separate city halls and elect separate mayors, who collect separate taxes to fund separate police who enforce separate laws. The Mayor of the City of London is called 'The Right Honourable Lord Mayor of London' to match his fancy outfit. He gets to ride in a golden carriage and work in a Guildhall while the mayor of London has to wear a suit, ride a bike and work in an office building. The City of London also has its own flag and its own crest and gets to act more like one of the countries in the UK than just an oddly located city. The corporation that runs the city of London is older than the United Kingdom by several hundred years. It was created by Romans in 43 CE as \"Londonimium\" with their temples, public baths, roads, bridges and a wall to defend their work. It's this wall which is why the current City of London exists - for though the Romans came and the Romans went and kingdoms rose and kingdoms fell, the wall endured protecting the city within. After the Romans William the Conqueror came to Great Britain to begin modern British history he found the City of London, with its sturdy walls more challenging to defeat than farmers on open fields. So he agreed to recognize the rights and privileges Cit"},{"id":16736350,"author":"akerro","month":"2018-04","story":"DNS Performance compared: CloudFlare 1.1.1.1 x Google 8.8.8.8 x Quad9 x OpenDNS","stance":"reference","why":"Supports point that City of London is separate from London.","quote":"","text":"I never said it's London PD, it's City of London, these are two different things, have different mayors and budget etc. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":14406063,"author":"digi_owl","month":"2017-05","story":"Who Owns England?","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that the City predates the English crown.","quote":"","text":"England is old, very old. For example the City of London existed before the crown of England, never mind the union we know as UK... [VIDEO]"},{"id":9406797,"author":"flurdy","month":"2015-04","story":"BBC News meets a Bulgarian IT entrepreneur [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Clarifies City of London versus London in passing.","quote":"","text":"Agree, though I like the Silicon Roundabout name more. The Tech City name to me seems like a government idea, sort of Information Superhighway.... With finding money it is good that Shoreditch is right next to the City (of London, not London the City [VIDEO] ), and there is a good amount of Angels and VCs in Shoreditch itself."},{"id":5187730,"author":"iuguy","month":"2013-02","story":"Political failure modes and the beige dictatorship","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for City of London being a separate legal entity.","quote":"","text":"The City of London cannot pull the eject handle and relocate. The City of London is a separate legal entity[1] with it's own laws and rules, and it's those rules that meant that the trading that took down Lehman Brothers was conducted in the City of London, because it couldn't have really legally taken place elsewhere. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":4491394,"author":"erichocean","month":"2012-09","story":"Former “Seasteaders” Come Ashore To Start Libertarian Utopias In Honduran Jungle","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a special-status city.","quote":"","text":"The City of London is also another example: [VIDEO] Note: \"City of London\" is not the same as \"London\", where the Olympics were held."}],"r":[["z1ROpIKZe-c",5],["ulCdoCfw-bY",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["GiYO1TObNz8",1],["4AivEQmfPpk",1],["LO1mTELoj6o",1],["ORp3q1Oaezw",1],["fKy7ljRr0AA",1]]},"JcJSW7Rprio":{"wy":"HN treats it as a favorite example of silly but educational computer science, recommended often when threads touch odd storage hacks.","q":{"x":"recommend this video to anyone here.","i":43518243,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43518243,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-03","story":"Vramfs: Vram Based Filesystem for Linux","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Silly computer science that teaches a lot.","quote":"recommend this video to anyone here.","text":"> harder drive Here's the direct YouTube link[0] I'd * HIGHLY* recommend this video to anyone here. It is exactly that fun silly computer science stuff where you also learn a shit ton. His channel is full of this stuff. Don't ask why, ask why not Is essentially the motto of his channel, and it is the best. Leads to lots of innovations and I think we all should encourage more of this kind of stuff. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38700912,"author":"mk_stjames","month":"2023-12","story":"NASA sends cat video over laser from 80 lunar distances","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A favorite Tom7 video; worth strapping in for.","quote":"one of my favorite Tom7 videos","text":"This idea is covered during the first part of one of my favorite Tom7 videos. If this comment is the first you are hearing of Tom7... strap in and enjoy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36810817,"author":"alserio","month":"2023-07","story":"GradIEEEnt half decent: The hidden power of imprecise lines [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"everything on his channel is gold","text":"As the other commenter said, everything on his channel is gold. Just the other day I was rewatching \"Automated 3dfication of Nintendo games\" [LINK] and \"Harder Drives\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34872985,"author":"josephg","month":"2023-02","story":"Store files inside of YouTube videos","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Block storage built from ping packets, with great storytelling.","quote":"his style is amazing","text":"Suckerpunch did a video on “harder drives” where he implemented a block storage device by storing data in ping packets. It’s one of my all time favourite technical talks - his style is amazing, and he’s an incredible story teller. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34172502,"author":"shadowgovt","month":"2022-12","story":"I ran the length of every street in Pittsburgh: PAC TOM [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing absurd data storage project with a memorable ending.","quote":"His Harder Drive paper was amazing","text":"His Harder Drive paper was amazing [ [VIDEO] ], most especially the ending that considered a surprisingly common configuration of utterly useless, inefficient mass data storage that solves a problem nobody actually has."},{"id":42903326,"author":"makizar","month":"2025-02","story":"Visualizing all books of the world in ISBN-Space","stance":"rec","why":"Maps IP addresses on a Hilbert curve; rest of video is great.","quote":"the rest of the video is wildly good","text":"That's a really nice view! Made me think of this map of IP addresses arranged as a Hilbert curve i saw in this Tom7 video: (the rest of the video is wildly good if you haven't seen it) [VIDEO]"},{"id":41202886,"author":"alexwasserman","month":"2024-08","story":"Harder Drive: Hard drives we didn't want or need (2022) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Walks through the paper's ideas; commenter found it fascinating.","quote":"fantastic associated YouTube video that talks through these too","text":"There's a fantastic associated YouTube video that talks through these too: [VIDEO] It's a rabbit hole that had be fascinated for quite a while."},{"id":41063131,"author":"anitil","month":"2024-07","story":"Space-filling curves, constructively","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes the pinged IPv4 space as a Hilbert curve.","quote":"though I recommend the whole thing.","text":"They're also useful for representing IPv4 space. Tom7 had a nice video where he does this after pinging the whole (IPv4) internet [0] [0] [VIDEO] at about 6 minutes, though I recommend the whole thing."},{"id":36821507,"author":"nick-of-time","month":"2023-07","story":"GradIEEEnt half decent: The hidden power of imprecise lines [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among the commenter's favorite videos.","quote":"","text":"Mine are \"Harder Drives\"[1], \"ELO World\"[2], and \"Printable x86\"[3] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":36186579,"author":"mtlmtlmtlmtl","month":"2023-06","story":"Anything can be a message queue if you use it wrongly enough","stance":"rec","why":"Best watched unspoiled; a fun hard-drive hack.","quote":"Reminds me of Harder Drive by Tom 7","text":"I love this! Reminds me of Harder Drive by Tom 7: [VIDEO] It's better to go in unspoiled so I won't reveal any details."},{"id":36519713,"author":"cbm-vic-20","month":"2023-06","story":"Happy 50th Birthday, Ethernet","stance":"rec","why":"Whole video is great; a relevant part starts at 2:31.","quote":"The whole thing is great, but the part relevant to the parent comment starts at 2:31.","text":"See also Tom7's \"Harder Drive\" video. The whole thing is great, but the part relevant to the parent comment starts at 2:31. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34871613,"author":"Lt_Riza_Hawkeye","month":"2023-02","story":"Store files inside of YouTube videos","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining esoteric storage methods.","quote":"I found these esoteric storage methods greatly entertaining as well","text":"Maybe it doesn't have a post of its own, but I found these esoteric storage methods greatly entertaining as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46327368,"author":"poly2it","month":"2025-12","story":"Hacker News front page now, but the titles are honest","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tom 7 did something reminiscent of this","text":"Definitely one of the harder drives feasible! Tom 7 did something reminiscent of this if you hadn't seen already: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":44331654,"author":"ge96","month":"2025-06","story":"Ask HN: How can we keep (part of) the web human?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"does remind me of this fun video","text":"I wonder if registrars provide lists of bought domain names vs. trying to map IPV4 which has multiple domains pointing to same ip would be interesting to do mapping yourself though probably pointless with how much effort/time it would take does remind me of this fun video [VIDEO]"},{"id":42035044,"author":"_jholland","month":"2024-11","story":"Create block devices on your computer backed by Redstone Memory","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Another great Harder Drive to add to the list","text":"Another great Harder Drive to add to the list: [VIDEO] Tom7 made a similar one in Tetris as part of the above video"},{"id":41138603,"author":"simpaticoder","month":"2024-08","story":"Ask HN: What is the best software to visualize a graph with a billion nodes?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"a rather silly implementation of a unix block device using ping latency","text":"Hilbert curves (or similar) are often used for graphing billions of nodes[1]. However this will not by default show the relationships between nodes in a graph. Depending on your data you may be able to write a function to map from your edge list to a node index that hints at proximity. Note that visualizations are limited by human perception to ~10000 elements, more usefully 1000 elements. You might try a force directed graph, perhaps a hierarchical variant wherein nodes can contain sub-graphs. Unless you have obvious root nodes, this variant would be interesting in that the user could start from an arbitrary set of nodes, giving different insights depending on their starting point. 1 - An excerpt from \"Harder Drive\", a rather silly implementation of a unix block device using ping latency with any host that will let him. He visualizes the full ipv4 address space in a hilbert curve at this offset: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36550067,"author":"yonatan8070","month":"2023-07","story":"TabDB: Using browser tabs as a database like only a maniac would","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"where the author used ICMP echo requests to store data","text":"This is my favorite kind of small fun projects, reminds me of the video about harder drives [1], where the author used ICMP echo requests to store data [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36178525,"author":"Elzear","month":"2023-06","story":"Show HN: List and demo of 20 costless serverless databases","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"stupidly complex/convoluted storage systems","text":"Funny, I actually tried finding tricks exploiting the cache from Vercel, but it didn't work. Initially I thought I could find a way to use it to share data across users, but nothing worked. Do you have any links to resources that could help getting a datastore from the Vercel cache? I will try adding things you mentioned, and I'd love to find way to also have stupidly complex/convoluted storage systems, inspired by tom7's harder drives: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31053824,"author":"TheGoodBarn","month":"2022-04","story":"A Census of Minecraft Servers","stance":"reference","why":"Similar IP charting and exploration in Tom7's video.","quote":"A lot of the idea feels similar to the recent Tom7 Harder Drive video","text":"This article is wonderful, I love the working knowledge. A lot of the idea feels similar to the recent Tom7 Harder Drive video: [VIDEO] I had never seen some of this IP charting and stuff and in his video he does a lot of similar stuff."},{"id":43438879,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2025-03","story":"Anubis: Proof-of-work proxy to prevent AI crawlers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Maybe suckerpinch can work it into a sequel to Harder Drive","text":"You can turn Anubis into Proof of Storage by appending large amounts of random data to the session cookie and then hashing it on each request. Combined with per-session-cookie limits, you can effectively force the LLM bots to store all the data you want if they want to scrape your site. Maybe suckerpinch can work it into a sequel to Harder Drive: Hard drives we didn't want or need [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LA_DrBwkiJA",2],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["uY-u1qyRM5w",1],["R7EEoWg6Ekk",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["ssZ_8cqfBlE",1],["LruaD7XhQ50",1],["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["-4FXLQoLDBA",1],["JtKAScORevQ",1],["6OPsH8PK7xM",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["HZGCoVF3YvM",1],["EK32jo7i5LQ",1],["HLNhPMQnWu4",1],["qOTYgcdNrXE",1],["aMcjxSThD54",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1]]},"Nb2tebYAaOA":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever chip architecture, Moore's Law or CPU design comes up, as an unusually deep and accessible view from a top designer.","q":{"x":"I was stunned by Jim's total command of Computer Architecture, and his deep insights about the subject.","i":22284052,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":35692202,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2023-04","story":"Second IC – Homemade 1000 transistor array chip","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Keller interview with Lex Fridman","quote":"Jim Keller was on the Lex Fridman podcast twice [1, 2] and it was awesome.","text":"I didn't make the connection until now, but Jim Keller was on the Lex Fridman podcast twice [1, 2] and it was awesome. In terms of founders this is pretty great team. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":32761422,"author":"robocat","month":"2022-09","story":"Korean nuclear fusion reactor achieves 100M°C for 30 seconds","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Keller's impressions of working with Elon","quote":"skip to 1:20:50 of this (effing amazing) interview","text":"Jim Keller talks about his impression working with Elon - skip to 1:20:50 of this (effing amazing) interview: [VIDEO] Edit: Also Jim talks about Elon’s engineering skills at 50 minutes into this interview: [LINK] Disclaimer: I’m not an Elon fan at all."},{"id":25557320,"author":"Pandabob","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Favorite Podcast Episode of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great episode of Lex Fridman's show","quote":"Loved the episodes with Jim Keller [0] and Brian Kernighan [1] as well.","text":"Loved the episodes with Jim Keller [0] and Brian Kernighan [1] as well. Lex really does a great show and has amazing guests. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":22284052,"author":"GeorgeTirebiter","month":"2020-02","story":"How much better was DEC Alpha than contemporary x86?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jim Keller's deep insights on computer architecture","quote":"I was stunned by Jim's total command of Computer Architecture, and his deep insights about the subject.","text":"I highly suggest viewing one of the Alpha's original designers, Jim Keller, recent chat with MIT Prof Lex Fridman [VIDEO] I was stunned by Jim's total command of Computer Architecture, and his deep insights about the subject. I will never again utter the phrase 'modern computer' without requisite awe."},{"id":24812430,"author":"est31","month":"2020-10","story":"ARM chips have an instruction with JavaScript in the name","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with the x86 spec author on instruction sets","quote":"There's a great podcast/interview from the x86 specification author","text":"Yeah that's how RISC vs CISC is taught in class, I've heard that same thing. I think it's an outdated paradigm though, if it's not been wrong all along. A CISC chip can still officially support instructions, but deprecate them and implement them very slowly in microcode, as is done with some x86 instructions. And a RISC chip manufacturer might just have used this phrase as a marketing line because designing RISC chips is easier than starting with a monster that has tons of instructions, and they needed some catchy phrase to market their alternative. They then get into the gradual process of making their customers happier one by one by adding special, mostly cold, silicon targeted for special workflows, like in this instance. Ultimately, the instruction set isn't that relevant anyways, what's more relevant is how the microcode can handle speculative execution for common workflows. There's a great podcast/interview from the x86 specification author: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24535267,"author":"empiricus","month":"2020-09","story":"Huang’s Law Is the New Moore’s Law, and Explains Why Nvidia Wants Arm","stance":"rec","why":"Jim Keller's thoughts on Moore's Law","quote":"For more details about Jim's thoughts I recommend this interview","text":"Carmack was actually mentioning he asked Jim Keller about Moore's Law. For more details about Jim's thoughts I recommend this interview [VIDEO]"},{"id":23813487,"author":"bob1029","month":"2020-07","story":"Apple Silicon: The Passing of Wintel","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what makes modern x86 CPUs powerful","quote":"I found this to be an excellent explanation for what is going on in these x86 CPUs that makes them so powerful","text":"I found this to be an excellent explanation for what is going on in these x86 CPUs that makes them so powerful: [VIDEO] (Starting from ~6:30)"},{"id":22515546,"author":"spectramax","month":"2020-03","story":"New AMD side channel attacks discovered, impacts Zen architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Chip architecture is hard, from legendary designer Keller","quote":"legendary chip designer","text":"I just wanted to say this about Intel vs AMD. Back when Intel was the \"underdog\" it couldn't compete with Athlon/Opeteron CPUs from AMD with its Pentium IV line, fan boys from both sides continually escalated the armchair warfare on Slashdot and Anandtech forums when Intel finally came out with the Conroe architecture that blew the competition away. I've seen this pendulum swing from one side to the other and back. Putting Intel marketing (awful) and AMD's marketing (awful again...), and their fan bases (toxic) aside, can we acknowledge the fact that working on a computer architecture is an extraordinarily complex task and that requires brilliant people all working together? I'll defer this fascinating topic, which most software engineers and tech enthusiasts are completely and utterly unaware of, to this talk by the legendary chip designer (x86 spec coauthor, A4/A5/Zen/Tesla AI ASIC architect) - Jim Keller and his interview with Lex Fridman: [VIDEO] Designing & manufacturing computer chips is hard af, having worked in semiconductor manufacturing for over 12 years (primarily on the backend side of the Fab). Edit: Redacting some specifics"},{"id":30735550,"author":"baryphonic","month":"2022-03","story":"It’s Like GPT-3 but for Code – Fun, Fast, and Full of Flaws","stance":"reference","why":"Keller says Moore's Law results from thousands of distinct innovations","quote":"","text":"> By 2025, either these models will have hit a wall on diminishing returns and it will take a complete pivot to some other approach to continue to see notable gains, or the products will continue to have improved at compounding rates and access will have become business critical in any industry with a modicum of competition. Is there a single example in AI, or even technology as a whole, where simply continuing to apply one technique has led to compounding growth? Even Moore's Law, according to none other than Jim Keller[1], is more a consequence of thousands of individual innovations that are each quite distinct from others but that build on each other to create this compounding growth we see. There is no similar curve for AI. In this case, GPT-3 (released in 2020) uses the same architecture as GPT-2 (released in 2019), expanded to have ~100x more parameters. It's not hard to see that compounding this process will rapidly hit diminishing returns quickly in terms of time, power consumption, cost of hardware, etc. Honestly, if Google, Amazon and Microsoft didn't see increased computational cost as a financial benefit for their cloud services, people might be willing to admit that GPT-3 is a diminishing return itself: for 100x parameters, is GPT-3 over 100x better than GPT-2? It seems that the big quantum leaps in AI come from new architectures applied in just the right way. CNNs and now transformers (based on multi-head attention) are the ways we've found to scale this thing, "},{"id":30218884,"author":"throwaway13337","month":"2022-02","story":"Let's Rewrite Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Keller advocates rewrites as the way to progress in chip design","quote":"Jim Keller advocates rewrites in a similar way as the only way to progress in chip design.","text":"I'm generally against rewrites as they're often shortcuts for new developers that instantly reject a code base as 'too complicated' without understanding it. However, I have had success in rewriting my own code base a number of times. This is a unique situation of a one-man-band that understands the business case, handles support, and every bit that went into every decision of the code base. With this kind of clarity, when you've spent years with the problem domain, you can uniquely rewrite a project to get at the real business case that, now with years of experience, you see what your customers actually wanted you to solve in the first place, or maybe what they evolved to want in the end. Jim Keller advocates rewrites in a similar way as the only way to progress in chip design. I think we could rewrite very basic things in exciting ways with this sort of attitude. For instance, we know a lot more about what we need in a desktop OS, an email client, a search engine, etc. Basic things that have gotten a lot of cruft over the years as they evolved. Taking a fresh look could be rewarding. I guess one could reframe this as 'first principals thinking\" but with the caveat that the problem needs to be truly understood. Here's Jim Keller talking about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28010635,"author":"anonymouse008","month":"2021-07","story":"B-tree Path Hints","stance":"reference","why":"Keller on guessing the same result as last time","quote":"First Interview (where that poorly paraphrased quote resides)","text":"I'm not qualified to truly understand this, but when Lex first interviewed Jim Keller, Jim basically said regarding processor design -- 'yeah, if you just guess the same result as last time, you get a 50% speed increase.' First Interview (where that poorly paraphrased quote resides): [VIDEO] Second Interview: [LINK]"},{"id":27310095,"author":"mncharity","month":"2021-05","story":"Eric Carle has died","stance":"reference","why":"Keller on reading a couple of books a week","quote":"Thought you might like.","text":"Jim Keller (cpu designer): \"I joke, like, I read books. And people think, 'Oh, you read books'. Well, no, I've read a couple of books a week, for [50] years.\"[1] Thought you might like. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25886615,"author":"ksec","month":"2021-01","story":"Chipmaking Is Being Redesigned","stance":"reference","why":"Keller notes transistors could shrink to about 10 atoms","quote":"transistors could reach 10 atoms by 10 atoms by 10 atoms while avoiding quantum effects","text":"> I expect within the next 6-15 years that we'll hit this limit You are talking about fundamental physical limits? Um... No, [1] \"At about 26-28.5 minutes of the Jim Keller: Jim notes that transistors could reach 10 atoms by 10 atoms by 10 atoms while avoiding quantum effects. People are also working on harnessing quantum effects.\" We are so far away from fundamental limits. That even if we assume we could somehow double transistor density every two years, which we dont anymore, there are still at least another 15-20 years before we are close to that limit. And that is not accounting any 3D Transistors. Realistically we already have Roadmap up to 2030 from TSMC. The only limit we will hit is that the node is too expensive and market could no longer afford the premium. Which You could expect to happen within next 10-15 years. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25360378,"author":"andy_ppp","month":"2020-12","story":"EU countries team up for semiconductor push","stance":"reference","why":"Keller on why Intel stopped believing in Moore's law","quote":"Jim Keller talks about this in this in the Lex Fridman podcast","text":"I agree, their problem wasn't lack of resources it was they stopped believing in Moore's law. They started to believe that 5 and 3nm advancements were not coming and didn't make the hundreds/thousands of new inventions needed to get them there. At least that's what I get reading between the lines here... Jim Keller talks about this in this in the Lex Fridman podcast: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23763027,"author":"pflanze","month":"2020-07","story":"Google offers free fabbing for 130nm open-source chips","stance":"reference","why":"Keller explains how designers use the transistor budget","quote":"Jim Keller explains in this interview how CPU designers are making use of the transistor budget","text":"My understanding is that architectural improvements (i.e. new approaches to detect more parts in code that can be evaluated at the same time, and then do so) need more transistors, ergo a smaller process. (Jim Keller explains in this interview how CPU designers are making use of the transistor budget: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":23783746,"author":"abetusk","month":"2020-07","story":"The Bitter Lesson (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited alongside discussion of Moore's law and cost","quote":"","text":"Moore's law might be dead but the deeper law is still alive. Moore's law is technically \"the number of transistors per unit area doubles every 24 months\" [1]. The more important law is that the cost of transistors halves every 18-24 months. That is, Moore's law talks about how many transistors we can pack into a unit area. The deeper issue is how much it costs . If we can only pack in a certain amount transistors per area but the cost drops exponentially, we still see massive gains. There's also Wright's law that comes into play [3] that talks about dropping exponential costs just from institutional knowledge (2x in production leads to (.75-.9)x in cost). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":23786358,"author":"jmole","month":"2020-07","story":"The Bitter Lesson (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"On what to do with extra transistors","quote":"what the hell do we do with all these extra transistors?","text":"Yup - and this year's top CPUs have almost 10x the performance per watt of CPUs from even 2-3 years ago [0] Raw computation is only half the story. The other half is: what the hell do we do with all these extra transistors? [1] 0 - [LINK] 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23818276,"author":"imtringued","month":"2020-07","story":"Apple Silicon: The Passing of Wintel","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Add timestamps to your link instead of messing around with parenthesis. [VIDEO] Right click -> \"Copy video URL at current time\""},{"id":23161791,"author":"fermienrico","month":"2020-05","story":"Slack was down","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy to silicon refactoring every decade","quote":"","text":"If you noticed how insanely Slack freaked out and handled the service outage. The application just got stuck, didn't know what to do, I see a faint 503 error message which looks like a webpage nginx error displayed right in the app. Clearing cache didn't help and the app just quit. Electron apps are a nuisance and should be eradicated from this planet. We've come a full circle -> Bloat the web -> Create javascript frameworks -> Build SPA/PWA apps -> Invade the desktop land -> Gain traction because \"its crossplatform\" and now try to reimplement GUI elements from some fancy_new framework that is developed by people who are entirely unqualified for it. Throw away all the work that's done by OS developers and designers that are getting paid as professionals to develop guidelines like this: [LINK] The modern tech stack needs to be refactored just like they refactor silicon architecture every 10 years: [VIDEO] Edit: People talking about would native app handle this better? Sure. I argue that it wouldn't just get rid of all UI elements and drop the entire DOM on the page, the UI just crashed. And everytime I open slack it seems like its doing something else entirely. There is a massive difference in quality of native UI libraries and Bulma for example, and its not just the library, you gotta rely on React to redraw things and it depends on 4 billion npm dependencies. The web truly sucks. And it is now eating into desktop apps."},{"id":22330281,"author":"reggieband","month":"2020-02","story":"Self-driving cars turned out to be harder than expected","stance":"reference","why":"Keller and Fridman disagree on how hard self-driving is","quote":"Lex is clearly pessimistic and Jim is clearly optimistic.","text":"I've been watching Lex Fridman's youtube podcast and there is a recent interview with Jim Keller [1]. Keller is a chip designer famous for his involvement in multiple chips at Intel, AMD, Apple and he was co-author of the x86-64 instruction set. He also worked for Tesla. There is a point in the conversation where Lex and Jim clearly disagree about how \"easy\" self-driving AI should be. Lex is clearly pessimistic and Jim is clearly optimistic. I have to admit I was more swayed by Lex's points than by Jim's, but it is hard to discount someone so clearly (extraordinarily) expert and working directly in the field. 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["coGpmA4saEk",1],["0qKp-0h9P18",1],["F9cO3-MLHOM",1],["2qv3fcXW1mg",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["wSdV1M7n4gQ",1],["ia7IKW0yuG0",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["plVk4NVIUh8",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1],["Jv7sLrON7QY",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["Mjx3S3UjmnA",1],["O9upVbGSBFo",1],["gbYXBJOFgeI",1],["bnKIVX968PQ",1]]},"msVuCEs8Ydo":{"wy":"HN commenters send people to it whenever a thread confuses the speed of light with a light-specific limit, and several say it deepened their understanding of relativity.","q":{"x":"The video about “the speed of causality” is the one that caused me to level up","i":24650347,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25231631,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-11","story":"Warp Drive News","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Starting episode in a short, varied physics series.","quote":"it is a great thing to watch when you've got a short wait for something","text":"There's an episode of PBS Space Time about this, \"The Speed of Light is NOT About Light\" [1], which comes pretty close to making the same points you are making. It might be useful to watch this earlier episode, \"Are Space and Time an Illusion?\" [2], first. There is a later episode (or maybe a couple of them) that go into how interactions with the Higgs field make it so things with mass travel slowed than the speed of causality. To tie back to the warp drive news, they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives, \"Is The Alcubierre Warp Drive Possible?\" [3]. There's also \"Superluminal Time Travel + Time Warp Challenge Answer\" [4], and \"Will Wormholes Allow Fast Interstellar Travel?\" [5]. A non-warp unknown physics drive that comes up a lot also got an episode, \"The EM Drive: Fact or Fantasy?\" [6]. There is at least one episode covering interstellar travel without using any unknown physics, \"5 REAL Possibilities for Interstellar Travel\" [7]. The scope of the series is basically anything vaguely connected to astronomy, cosmology, and quantum mechanics, and the episodes are all pretty short (5-12 minutes), so it is a great thing to watch when you've got a short wait for something. Here's the series home page [8], and their YouTube channel [9]. Also on the PBS streaming app, for those who like to get their physics on their big screen TVs. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] [8] [LINK] [9] [LINK]"},{"id":24650347,"author":"willis936","month":"2020-10","story":"NIST Pair of Atomic Clocks Reveal Einstein's Relativity at a Personal Scale","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Speed-of-causality episode that deepened the commenter's understanding of relativity.","quote":"The video about “the speed of causality” is the one that caused me to level up","text":"PBS SpaceTime has a treatise on relativity. There are a dozen videos worth watching on this topic alone. The video about “the speed of causality” is the one that caused me to level up: [VIDEO] Here is a 12 video playlist on relativity: [LINK] Here is a six video series on measuring space and time: [LINK]"},{"id":32554113,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-08","story":"“I survived the TCP transition” (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Early PBS Space Time episode on the speed of light.","quote":"You may also like The Geometry of Causality","text":"As an aside, the PBS space time video The Speed of Light is NOT About Light : [VIDEO] As an aside to the aside - as I rewatch it I quickly notice how young he looks (and then note the date is 2015 on there - one of the early ones and the production is less refined). You may also like The Geometry of Causality [LINK]"},{"id":24665107,"author":"cthalupa","month":"2020-10","story":"Faster Than Light in Our Model of Physics: Some Preliminary Thoughts","stance":"rec","why":"Great explanation that c is not about light.","quote":"has a great explanation from the always excellent PBS Space Time","text":"You’re correct. The speed of light is not actually about light. [VIDEO] has a great explanation from the always excellent PBS Space Time, for the curious."},{"id":21731408,"author":"rhinokungfu","month":"2019-12","story":"If the universe is 13.8B years old, how can we see 46B light years away? (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains c as the causality limit and space expansion.","quote":"PBS space time has a good video on this","text":"Some of the confusion is, I think from referring to speed of light as \"speed of light\". When it really is speed limit of causality between 2 points in space. And light (em waves) just happen to be able to hit that limit. Now since expansion of space is itself not going to allow one point in space to cause any effect on another, it (the expansion) can be faster than speed of causality. PBS space time has a good video on this - [VIDEO]"},{"id":21731429,"author":"rhinokungfu","month":"2019-12","story":"If the universe is 13.8B years old, how can we see 46B light years away? (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains c as the causality limit and space expansion.","quote":"PBS space time has a good video on this","text":"Posted this to a point above, but probably more pertinent here, so reposting - Some of the confusion is, I think from referring to speed of light as \"speed of light\". When it really is speed limit of causality between 2 points in space. And light (em waves) just happen to be able to hit that limit. Now since expansion of space is itself not going to allow one point in space to cause any effect on another, it (the expansion) can be faster than speed of causality. PBS space time has a good video on this - [VIDEO]"},{"id":20949885,"author":"dkersten","month":"2019-09","story":"Water found on a potentially life-friendly alien planet","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what speed of causality means.","quote":"Take a look at this video by PBS Space Time on the topic","text":"@mesid asked in a now dead comment: > What does speed of causality mean? And how is light so close to that? Take a look at this video by PBS Space Time on the topic: [VIDEO] You may need to go down the rabbit hole and watch the earlier videos on relativity and related topics."},{"id":20692812,"author":"rhinokungfu","month":"2019-08","story":"Milky Way’s Black Hole Just Flared, Growing 75 Times as Bright for a Few Hours","stance":"rec","why":"Explains c as universal speed limit of causality.","quote":"PBS Space time has a good video on this","text":"Speed of light is not really just speed of light, but universal speed limit of causality. Light (electromagnetic waves just happens to be one of the few things capable of hitting that limit. Even the effect of gravity is limited to speed of light. PBS Space time has a good video on this - [VIDEO] . So to answer your question, no it is not possible for anything to affect us any faster than the universal speed limit of causality (that light happens to be able to hit)."},{"id":16285610,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-02","story":"Speed of light drops to zero at 'exceptional points'","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies speed of light versus maximum speed of causality.","quote":"Something to head off likely confusion: The Speed of Light is not about the Speed of Light.","text":"Something to head off likely confusion: The Speed of Light is not about the Speed of Light. [VIDEO] Don't get these two things mixed up: - The speed of electromagnetic waves in the local medium - The maximum speed our reality allows for causal phenomena"},{"id":15106360,"author":"neom","month":"2017-08","story":"Rumours swell over new kind of gravitational-wave sighting","stance":"rec","why":"One of several PBS Space Time episodes explaining the topic.","quote":"These PBS Spacetime episodes should help","text":"These PBS Spacetime episodes should help: The Speed of Light is NOT About Light - [VIDEO] Is Quantum Tunneling Faster than Light? - [LINK] The Quantum Experiment that Broke Reality - [LINK] Pilot Wave Theory and Quantum Realism - [LINK] The Future of Gravitational Waves - [LINK]"},{"id":38234514,"author":"light_hue_1","month":"2023-11","story":"Getting the Lorentz transformations without requiring an invariant speed","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as already making the same argument as a critiquing video.","quote":"But that video makes pretty much the same argument.","text":"I don't understand why the video needs be so negative. Why dump on other videos? And unfairly so! > Science educators love to make videos about relativity, but surprisingly few of them seem to understand what this stuff really means. The two examples the video gives of science educators that don't \"know what this stuff means\" are Don Lincoln and Matt O'Dowd. Both have PhDs in physics. Lincoln was on the team that discovered the Higgs boson. O'Dowd is a professor at CUNY. They know what they're talking about. Then the video gives as an example of a bad explanation the PBS Space Time on the speed of light. But that video makes pretty much the same argument. That the speed of light/causality falls out logically even if you don't postulate it. The author clearly didn't watch that video carefully [VIDEO] I would have subscribed to this channel, but this tone is a bad look."},{"id":34937699,"author":"willis936","month":"2023-02","story":"Mysterious object is being dragged into the black hole at the Milky Way’s center","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that c is speed of causality.","quote":"c\" isn't \"the speed of light\" so much as \"the speed of causality\".","text":"\"c\" isn't \"the speed of light\" so much as \"the speed of causality\". Light just happens to be massless packets of energy that move at the speed of causality. For all intents and purposes this is happening in real time from our local frame of reference. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24329038,"author":"irjustin","month":"2020-08","story":"Do we really travel through time with the speed of light?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the meaning of c as the speed of causality.","quote":"","text":"downvoted because time traveling through a medium vs traveling through curved space are not anywhere near the same underlying principles[0]. The implication being suggested is - if I was trapped in a block of plastic or... underwater, I would experience time different compared to someone standing on land just because \"c\" is slower. Clearly not the case. To be specific, when \"the speed of light\" is discussed like this, it usually means the \"speed of causality\"[1] which is the underlying meaning of \"c\" in relativity. The speed of causality through a medium (i.e. water) is the same as a vacuum if spacetime curvature is the same. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17983256,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-09","story":"SETI spots dozens of new mysterious signals emanating from distant galaxy","stance":"reference","why":"Points to PBS Space Time episode on the speed of light.","quote":"","text":"Great answer, but do you have more information on this? PBS SpaceTime. The Speed of Light is not about Light: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14723218,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-07","story":"Evolution of Sexual Intimidation: Male Baboons Beat Up Females, Increases Mating","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the speed of light as the speed of causality.","quote":"Physics tells us that the \"speed of light\" is actually the absolute speed of causality for mass-energy.","text":"humanity will eventually create a \"perfect\" version of ourselves, which with then exterminate us because we are inferior Human cultural evolution has already created self-proclaimed \"perfect\" cultures that went on to attempt exactly this. The Nazis were only the most recent notable example. There are many examples in history of groups who considered it their holy duty or moral duty to take over the world. Fortunately, for most of human history, this hasn't been feasible, but technology is changing this equation. then the AI-human race will create a perfect society and branch off to explore the universe. Physics tells us that the \"speed of light\" is actually the absolute speed of causality for mass-energy. [VIDEO] So until consciousness can inhabit a substrate independent of mass-energy and based on space itself, and thus not subject to the light speed limit, any intelligent civilization which expands is doomed to create its own rivals. In Michael Swannick's Vacuum Flowers the Earth is taken over by a hegemonic super-intelligence, but it's forever trapped there, because when attempting to expand, the light-speed lag will cause a sub-part of it to bud off and immediately attack its greatest rival -- which is always the rest of itself. In any case, civilizations are clearly not stable on cosmic time scales. Who in their right mind would think that billions of interacting sentients with their own agendas would produce any stable organization? It's ridiculous on the face of it."}],"r":[["jPhJbKBuNnA",2],["tafGL02EUOA",2],["1YFrISfN7jo",2],["YycAzdtUIko",2],["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1]]},"Ge3aKEmZcqY":{"wy":"HN people recommend it to anyone wanting a practical sense of what CPUs can really do and why abstraction layers cost so much performance.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching it.","i":30586520,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33991844,"author":"Pr0ject217","month":"2022-12","story":"What we can learn from vintage computing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sparked interest in what the CPU actually does","quote":"Casey Muratori's talks[0] on performance and optimization sparked an interest in me to start putting thought into what the CPU actually does","text":"Thank you for the detailed explanation! It sounds like programming computers back then would have been a joy. Casey Muratori's talks[0] on performance and optimization sparked an interest in me to start putting thought into what the CPU actually does (which wasn't taught when I learned web development). Since discovering his talks, I've been on the hunt for more learning materials. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30586520,"author":"mrv_asura","month":"2022-03","story":"Algorithms for Modern Hardware","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Practical, high-level understanding of CPU architecture and SIMD optimization","quote":"I highly recommend watching it.","text":"The timing of this is great! I just watched Casey Muratori's lecture, \"Simple Code, High Performance\"[1] and its follow-up videos[2, 3]. I highly recommend watching it. It is about Software Optimization, and gives practical and high-level understanding of topics like CPU Architecture, SIMD etc. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":29138786,"author":"DrBazza","month":"2021-11","story":"An Epic future for SPJ","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains developers don't realise how fast code could run","quote":"Casey Muratori's (who has been on here a lot lately) talk nails it [1]","text":"> Speak for yourself; most phones I see still take 2+ seconds to open some apps. Why is that though? I've written code since the 8 bit days of BBC micros and ZX Spectrums. Casey Muratori's (who has been on here a lot lately) talk nails it [1] - developers just don't realise how fast their code could and should run. And the fact that we have layers upon layers of frameworks and no one cares. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42033653,"author":"kleiba","month":"2024-11","story":"I Waited 10B Cycles and All I Got Was This Loading Screen","stance":"rec","why":"Educational series on hardware getting faster while user experience stays the same","quote":"Casey Muratori started a whole educational webseries about this","text":"Casey Muratori started a whole educational webseries about this - the fact that hardware is getting faster and faster but the user experience seems to be staying the same. [VIDEO] He also has a \"performance-aware programming\" series: [LINK]"},{"id":39579337,"author":"mtzet","month":"2024-03","story":"Ask HN: How can I learn about performance optimization?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what optimal performance looks like via grass planting optimization","quote":"I'd recommend two talks that come with a very different perspective from what you usually hear","text":"Most software in the industry is slow because it's doing a lot of stuff that it shouldn't. Often times additional \"optimization\" layers adds caching, but makes getting to the root of the issue harder. The biggest win is primarily getting rid of things you don't need and secondarily operating on things in batch. My playbook for optimizing in the real world is something like this: 1. Understand what you're actually trying to compute end-to-end. The bigger the chunk you're trying to optimize, the greater the potential for performance. 2. Sketch out what an optimal process would look like. What data do you need to fetch, what computation do you need to do on this, how often does this need to happen. Don't try to be clever and micro-optimize or cache computations. Just focus on only doing the things you need to do in a simple way. Use arrays a lot. 3. Understand what the current code is actually doing. How close to the sketch above are you? Are you doing a lot of I/O in the middle of the computation? Do you keep coming back to the same data? If you want to understand the limits of how fast computers are, and what optimal performance looks like I'd recommend two talks that come with a very different perspective from what you usually hear: 1. Mike Acton's talk at cppcon 2014 [LINK] 2. Casey Muratori's talk about optimizing a grass planting algorithm [VIDEO]"},{"id":29736775,"author":"ocharles","month":"2021-12","story":"Why can’t you design noise in frequency space?","stance":"rec","why":"Grass planting algorithm showing blue noise in games","quote":"Here Casy explains a grass planting algorithm for games, where white noise wouldn't be suitable.","text":"For one real application of blue noise in particular, see [VIDEO] (22:30 is a good starting point). Here Casy explains a grass planting algorithm for games, where white noise wouldn't be suitable."},{"id":43057317,"author":"3836293648","month":"2025-02","story":"We were wrong about GPUs","stance":"reference","why":"Guest university lecture using C++ with SIMD intrinsics","quote":"The video was a recorded guest lecture for a uni in the Netherlands though.","text":"It's Casey Muratori, he's an American gamedev, not a professor. The video was a recorded guest lecture for a uni in the Netherlands though. And it wasn't redone in assembly, it was C++ with SIMD intrinsics, which might as well just be assembly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36588796,"author":"akasakahakada","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: Resources for High Performance Computing?","stance":"reference","why":"Possibly cited incidentally in an HPC resource list","quote":"","text":"1. Single-thread performance This is hardly related to HPC. The closest thing maybe code optimization and competitive programming. First learn how to do anything fast in single process, then think about scale it up later. 2. Parallel programming Learn MPI. Specifically mpi4py. Once you go MPI, you never use python's multiprocessing module (it sucks). But stuffs are mostly about solving linear algebra problems, which I am not that interested in. 4. Distributed Computing Related to MPI. But nowaday distributed GPU computing become a thing, thing that I can think of is Dask. Don't know how to use that. [LINK] 5. Quantum Computing Completely unrelated to HPC, at least in the near future (10 to 30 years). Areas that is the most active in this field is molecular computation. If you don't have a phy/chem PHD, you are not able to read anything beyond college level stuff in this field. And those are only basics stuff like elementary schools' arithmetics. But write your own program to solve those quantum problems in classical computers is interesting, because people there only think about problems that cause scaling issue, not the others. That's why some may think that quantum computer is a replacement of supercomputers. If you can find ways and invent techniques that can reduce timecomplexity in terms of classical algorithm, you are welcome. And literally there is a lot of room for anyone because every quantum computer emulator is unoptimized and slow as hell. Google, Microsoft, IBM, "},{"id":29736780,"author":"iamwil","month":"2021-12","story":"Why can’t you design noise in frequency space?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of algorithmic grass placement in The Witness","quote":"Here's an example of how to algorithmically place grass in the game \"The Witness\".","text":"Blue noise is useful in games for generating more natural looking scenes. Here's an example of how to algorithmically place grass in the game \"The Witness\". [VIDEO] So if you use white noise, it looks unnaturally clustered with patches. This is the part that discusses the noise. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29127475,"author":"rasz","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How were video games from the 90s so efficient?","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that old games were fast by avoiding abstraction layers","quote":"There are no tricks, you just have to give up mountains of levels of abstraction accumulated over the years.","text":"There are no tricks, you just have to give up mountains of levels of abstraction accumulated over the years. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WMSBRk5WG58",1],["rX0ItVEVjHc",1],["aircAruvnKk",1]]},"wxL4ElboiuA":{"wy":"HN commenters call it mesmerizing and a great look at how things are actually made by a dedicated craftsman.","q":{"x":"The video in the article is just amazing. You don't normally get to see the exact details of how things get made.","i":17402624,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":35380793,"author":"dymk","month":"2023-03","story":"One Man’s Quest to Revive the Great American Vacuum Tube","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Channel on small-scale high-quality manufacturing by a nixie tube expert","quote":"Recommended viewing for anyone who loves home-ish-scale, high-quality manufacturing","text":"I came to comment about Dalibor as well - amazing youtube channel. Recommended viewing for anyone who loves home-ish-scale, high-quality manufacturing: [VIDEO] He's got to be one of the world's experts on nixie tube manufacturing, at this point."},{"id":27778798,"author":"brokenmachine","month":"2021-07","story":"List of Special Elevator Modes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mesmerizing craftsman video on making nixie tubes","quote":"I watched a mesmerizing and beautiful youtube video about making nixie tubes. I love craftsman videos.","text":"Going even more OT, I watched a mesmerizing and beautiful youtube video about making nixie tubes. I love craftsman videos. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17402624,"author":"dustinmoorenet","month":"2018-06","story":"The Nixie Tube Story: The Neon Display Tech That Engineers Can’t Quit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed look at how nixie tubes are made by hand","quote":"The video in the article is just amazing. You don't normally get to see the exact details of how things get made.","text":"The video in the article is just amazing. You don't normally get to see the exact details of how things get made. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29245638,"author":"jkingsman","month":"2021-11","story":"IoT Nixie Tubes","stance":"rec","why":"Beautiful high quality video of artisanal nixie tube manufacturing, text notes only","quote":"this is a beautiful (with no music or narration; text notes only) high quality video of modern artisanal","text":"Tangentially, this is a beautiful (with no music or narration; text notes only) high quality video of modern artisanal (i.e. all by hand) nixie tube manufacturing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23029772,"author":"janekm","month":"2020-04","story":"Casio CQ-1 calculator and clock","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to how nixie tubes are made","quote":"Also a good introduction to how they are made","text":"Getting a custom VFD made would be a very expensive process indeed. If you need a very small number of segments then Dalibor Farny can make a custom Nixie tube, which is similar technology: [LINK] Also a good introduction to how they are made (making a VFD would be similar but more difficult): [VIDEO] Otherwise Noritake itron still make VFD monitors for industrial applications (seems they have high temp advantages over LCDs): [LINK]"},{"id":16910080,"author":"zxcvgm","month":"2018-04","story":"Hand Made Vacuum Tubes by Claude Paillard (2012) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Related handmade electronics-tube making videos","quote":"if you liked watching this, then maybe you'd also like these videos","text":"if you liked watching this, then maybe you'd also like these videos, which i watched some time ago: making a miniature vacuum tube triode [LINK] The Art of Making a Nixie Tube [VIDEO]"},{"id":47233391,"author":"alnwlsn","month":"2026-03","story":"The Cathode Ray Tube site","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the lab needed to make such tubes","quote":"","text":"In some ways it's never been easier to get all the equipment you will need. But if you're wanting to make ones even half as good as the ones on this page, you'd probably want to become a skilled glassblower and outfit a lab that looks something like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18466447,"author":"CaliforniaKarl","month":"2018-11","story":"The Vacuum Tube’s Many Modern-Day Uses","stance":"reference","why":"Shows nixie tube making","quote":"","text":"And of course, there's the Nixie tube. [VIDEO] (Previously posted in [LINK] , the maker's web site via [LINK] , and covered in an IEEE article via [LINK] )"},{"id":13801024,"author":"mmagin","month":"2017-03","story":"What the death of the CRT display technology means for classic arcade machines","stance":"reference","why":"Small-scale Nixie tube manufacturing by a dedicated individual","quote":"This youtube video documents what a rather dedicated individual does to resurrect small-scale Nixie tube manufacturing","text":"CRT production is a much more complex bit of modern manufacturing than making vinyl LPs. This youtube video documents what a rather dedicated individual does to resurrect small-scale Nixie tube manufacturing: [VIDEO] And technologically, color CRTs are way beyond that. [LINK]"}],"r":[["1f6YyH62jFE",1],["NdppYYfQJgg",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["qJEWOTZnFeg",1]]},"GuigptwlVHo":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for hearing Graham himself and for its clear introduction to up-arrow notation and enormous numbers.","q":{"x":"And here is the man himself describing it","i":23766263,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":40396384,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2024-05","story":"A Trial HIV Vaccine Triggered Elusive and Essential Antibodies in Humans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Part of Numberphile series on enormous numbers that inspire awe","quote":"If math can make you feel awe, you've got to watch the Numberphile series on very big numbers.","text":"If math can make you feel awe, you've got to watch the Numberphile series on very big numbers. They're constants used in proofs that are specific, well-defined integers known to be finite, but so huge that the physical universe does not have enough particles (or even Plank-scale microstates) in it to represent the number of digits these numbers have! Absolutely mind-bending stuff. Listed in increasing order: \"Graham's Number\" [LINK] \"How Big is Graham's Number? (feat Ron Graham)\" [VIDEO] \"The Enormous TREE(3)\" [LINK] \"TREE(3) (extra footage)\" [LINK] \"The Daddy of Big Numbers (Rayo's Number)\" [LINK]"},{"id":9054482,"author":"prezjordan","month":"2015-02","story":"Graham's Number","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Numberphile interview with Ron Graham himself","quote":"My brain melts every time I watch this video","text":"My brain melts every time I watch this video [1] from Numberphile on Graham's number. They also had another video [2] with Ron Graham himself :) [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8731812,"author":"prezjordan","month":"2014-12","story":"Prime Gap Grows After Decades-Long Lull","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing videos on arrow notation and Graham's number","quote":"Big big numbers are really, really cool!","text":"Is loglogloglog...log the only beast that can conquer Knuth's arrow notation? (x ↑↑↑↑...↑ n) Every time I watch videos about arrow notation or, more famously, Graham's number, my head explodes :) [VIDEO] Big big numbers are really, really cool!"},{"id":32285115,"author":"ObiWanFrijoles","month":"2022-07","story":"The Fourth Operation: What Comes After Exponentiation","stance":"rec","why":"Numberphile video with Ron Graham himself","quote":"There's an awesome video with Ron Graham himself on Numberphile (YouTube)","text":"Graham's number [0] can be defined using hyperoperations. There's an awesome video with Ron Graham himself on Numberphile (YouTube) [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21662433,"author":"dahart","month":"2019-11","story":"Inventing an Operation to Solve x^x = y (2000)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains up arrow notation well","quote":"This video explains it pretty well","text":"> why not have an operator that applies up arrows a number of times defined with up arrows? That is exactly what up arrows do, isn’t it? Up arrows are iterated up arrows. I learned this in college but still didn’t fully understand up arrows for a long time, they grow unimaginably bigger and faster than I first thought. This video explains it pretty well: [VIDEO] I don’t know about ‘ultimate’ but there are some generalizations of up arrows: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23766263,"author":"iamaaditya","month":"2020-07","story":"Ron Graham has died","stance":"reference","why":"Ron Graham himself describing his number","quote":"And here is the man himself describing it","text":"Graham's Number, as in 3 ↑↑ 3, is named after Ron Graham. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] And here is the man himself describing it [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XTeJ64KD5cg",2],["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1]]},"AyOAjFNPAbA":{"wy":"HN treats Hamilton's annual talks as the best window into how AWS regions and hardware are actually built.","q":{"x":"The keynote by James Hamilton[1] is absolutely awesome.","i":13102878,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13102878,"author":"nodesocket","month":"2016-12","story":"Links to YouTube Recordings of AWS re:Invent Sessions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enthusiastic James Hamilton tour of fiber, networking, power, and scale.","quote":"The keynote by James Hamilton[1] is absolutely awesome.","text":"The keynote by James Hamilton[1] is absolutely awesome. You can genuinely tell that James finds all this technology fascinating. He talks about fiber, networking, power, redundancy, scale all with utter enthusiasm. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38822002,"author":"mackid","month":"2023-12","story":"The art of high performance computing","stance":"rec","why":"Annual Hamilton talk on AWS infrastructure.","quote":"Worth watching multiple years","text":"Mark Russinovich gives a good talk most years on the internals of Azure and the systems that run it. [1] is an example. Look for talks from other years as well. Meta also publishes a number of papers/blogs/OSS projects on their engineering site [2] James Hamilton of AWS gives a talk most years on their infrastructure. Worth watching multiple years [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27994087,"author":"EwanToo","month":"2021-07","story":"Amazon EC2-Classic Is Retiring","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how AWS is built.","quote":"It'll tell you lots about how AWS is built","text":"Watch this video from 2016 by James Hamilton at AWS Re:Invent [VIDEO] It'll tell you lots about how AWS is built"},{"id":22944964,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2020-04","story":"Now Open – AWS Africa (Cape Town) Region","stance":"rec","why":"One of several good overviews of AWS infrastructure.","quote":"have given pretty good overview over the years of the AWS infrastructure.","text":"James Hamilton [0][1] and others [2][3][4] have given pretty good overview over the years of the AWS infrastructure. [0] AWS Innovation at Scale (2014), [LINK] [1] Tuesday Night Live (2016), [VIDEO] [2] Operation of the AWS Global Network (2019), [LINK] [3] Foundations of AWS Infrastructure (2018), [LINK] [4] Exploring the AWS Global Network (2018), [LINK]"},{"id":17552312,"author":"strong_silent_t","month":"2018-07","story":"Backblaze Durability Is Eleven 9s – And Why It Doesn’t Matter","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed overview of how AWS regions are structured.","quote":"More info here if you want to see how regions are structured at a high level of detail","text":"Others have answered, but I think the general principle for a Region is that between the AZs you have less than 1ms latency, physical separation between the data centers, but they may still be in the same floodplain, be able to be hit by the same hurricane, etc. More info here if you want to see how regions are structured at a high level of detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18316578,"author":"rbranson","month":"2018-10","story":"Packets per Second Limitations in EC2","stance":"reference","why":"Hamilton on 25GbE hardware commitment.","quote":"James Hamilton talked about their commitment to 25GbE hardware at Reinvent in 2016.","text":"James Hamilton talked about their commitment to 25GbE hardware at Reinvent in 2016. Fast forward to ~23m. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13125843,"author":"douglasfshearer","month":"2016-12","story":"Liberouter Combo Cards – FPGA boards focused on network data processing","stance":"reference","why":"Amazon's custom silicon for network processing.","quote":"Custom silicon, rather than the FPGA or ASICS they were using when they started the project in 2012.","text":"Amazon announced they were doing network processing in hardware at Re:work last week [1]. Custom silicon, rather than the FPGA or ASICS they were using when they started the project in 2012. They tout not only the freed CPU resources, but lower latencies and increased security of isolating the networking from the CPUs running hosted VMs. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-pKR212H5vQ",1],["swQbA4zub20",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["3qln2u1Vr2E",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1],["SAc0vQCC6UQ",1],["ycPr5-27vSI",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["EWX5B6cD4_4",1]]},"GNO_CYUjMK8":{"wy":"HN treats it as one of the best intros to the math of 3D graphics and a staple answer to how to learn graphics programming.","q":{"x":"It's a fantastic, FANTASTIC tour of 3D graphics fundamentals.","i":11746902,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11746902,"author":"Fr0styMatt88","month":"2016-05","story":"Scratchapixel: Computer Graphics Programming from Scratch","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best presentation on how matrix math relates to graphics","quote":"It's a fantastic, FANTASTIC tour of 3D graphics fundamentals.","text":"In addition to the recommendation by lastofus, I highly highly recommend this presentation: [VIDEO] Despite the title, the talk is NOT WebGL specific. It's a fantastic, FANTASTIC tour of 3D graphics fundamentals. This is, IMHO, quite simply the best presentation I've ever seen on how matrix math relates to graphics programming. Also see Stevens' site: [LINK] Side note - There are some great pages there, be sure to check this one out if you're at all interested in how math is applied to animation: [LINK]"},{"id":26157462,"author":"theandrewbailey","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: How to self-learn graphics programming?","stance":"rec","why":"Illuminating intro to vectors and matrices for 3D graphics","quote":"Matrixes, vectors, and how they are multiplied with each other are the fundamental magic of 3D graphics.","text":"I was sort of at the same place at one time. I found a presentation years ago that would have helped, so here you go: Steven Wittens: Making WebGL Dance [VIDEO] It doesn't actually get into the math until about 12 minutes in, but it might be illuminating. Matrixes, vectors, and how they are multiplied with each other are the fundamental magic of 3D graphics."},{"id":16841190,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Visual crash course in linear algebra despite its title","quote":"the title is deceptive, it's in some ways a visual crash course in linear algebra","text":"#Softwarish - I'm biased a bit more towards interface development: greg wilson - What We Actually Know About Software Development, and Why We Believe It’s True - [LINK] steve wittens - making webgl dance - the title is deceptive, it's in some ways a visual crash course in linear algebra - [VIDEO] glenn vanderburg - software engineering doesn't work - [LINK] chris granger - in search of tomorrow - [LINK] alan kay - tribute to ted nelson at intertwingled fest - [LINK] bret victor - this is already mentioned but if I had to pick one it'd be 'the humane representation of thought' - [LINK] #Hardwarish: saul griffith - soft, not solid: beyond traditional hardware engineering - [LINK] deb chachra - Architectural Biology and Biological Architectures - [LINK] #Getting more meta in technology and history: James Burke - Connections"},{"id":15646777,"author":"johnhenry","month":"2017-11","story":"Making WebGL Dance: How, Where, and What to Draw (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk with cool animations on WebGL drawing","quote":"IT's worth linking directly to the talk","text":"IT's worth linking directly to the talk: [VIDEO] (Also, after the first few slides in the animation, there are some pretty cool animations)."},{"id":6230573,"author":"DanielRibeiro","month":"2013-08","story":"Rotating Images","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of rendering algorithms and 3D graphics","quote":"Another very good overview of rendering algorithms was given by Steven Wittens","text":"Another very good overview of rendering algorithms was given by Steven Wittens on his Making Webgl Dance [1, 2] [1] slides: [LINK] [2] video: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["H9B5mYfBPlY",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["G0R09YzyuCI",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["AxFzf6yIfcc",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["zDZFcDGpL4U",1],["AbgsfeGvg3E",1],["Ox5P7QyL774",1],["7ttG90raCNo",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["sbQhgEJuExY",1]]},"XvDZLjaCJuw":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a demonstration of the Unix philosophy, with small tools composed through pipes.","q":{"x":"both are really excellent demonstrations and eludications of the Unix philosophy and mindset","i":10789419,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10789419,"author":"danso","month":"2015-12","story":"A Spellchecker Used to Be a Major Feat of Software Engineering (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demonstrates the Unix philosophy via pipes and a talking calculator.","quote":"both are really excellent demonstrations and eludications of the Unix philosophy and mindset","text":"That was a great watch...and if you keep on watching for a few more minutes, you can watch Lorinda L. Cherry rewrite his implementation using Unix pipes and then creating a talking calculator. The screencasting technology wasn't quite there yet ;), but both are really excellent demonstrations and eludications of the Unix philosophy and mindset that is pretty much unchanged and invaluable ~34 years later [VIDEO]"},{"id":30361297,"author":"Ambroos","month":"2022-02","story":"Lorinda Cherry, author of dc, bc, eqn has died","stance":"rec","why":"Cherry demonstrates pipes and scripts with an amusing choice of input.","quote":"I love what she does here","text":"I love what she does here: [VIDEO] , also demonstrating pipes and scripts. The input she chooses makes the whole segment pretty damn funny."},{"id":28651363,"author":"ducktective","month":"2021-09","story":"Toybox: All-in-one Linux command line","stance":"rec","why":"Lorinda Cherry demonstrates a talking calculator built from dc and Unix pipelines in 1982.","quote":"Lorinda Cherry herself explaining how to write a \"talking calculator\" (in 1982!) using dc and Unix pipelines","text":"Look what I've found! Lorinda Cherry herself explaining how to write a \"talking calculator\" (in 1982!) using dc and Unix pipelines: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25298592,"author":"abetlen","month":"2020-12","story":"A spellchecker used to be a major feat of software engineering (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Includes Lorinda Cherry building a talking calculator from piped commands.","quote":"Lorinda Cherry writing a talking calculator by piping together 3 unix commands.","text":"They really were. Here's a version of that video that includes Lorinda Cherry writing a talking calculator by piping together 3 unix commands. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21589163,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-11","story":"A Mystery of Unix History","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Also I found these fascinating","text":"Also I found these fascinating: The Unix-Haters Handbook (1994) has a lot of history and takes you back in time.. [LINK] Brian Kernighan 'interviews' Ken Thompson (2019) - Ken telling stories. They're both very funny, a very entertaining video, some stories I (and even Brian) hadn't heard. [LINK] UNIX: Making Computers Easier To Use (1982) [VIDEO] Also the other day I read in the AWK book that it was mainly inspired by SNOBOL4, so looked at some SNOBOL4 books from archive.org, from 1970-2 and just before, slightly earlier than Unix history takes you back. One tells you how to organize your punch cards to feed in the program.. Another had a bibliography with many dozens of SNOBOL books. It was widely used by people in the humanities apparently; like AWK it was at home dealing with words and text."},{"id":10789336,"author":"bithive123","month":"2015-12","story":"A Spellchecker Used to Be a Major Feat of Software Engineering (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Kernighan builds a spell checker from UNIX utilities in 1982.","quote":"Others might also find interesting this video from 1982","text":"Others might also find interesting this video from 1982 where Brian Kernighan demonstrates building a simple spell checker using UNIX command line utilities: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40257013,"author":"gabrielsroka","month":"2024-05","story":"Brian Kernighan explains Unix Pipeline (2015) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Younger Kernighan explains redirects instead of pipes in a 1982 film.","quote":"Similar but using redirects instead of pipes","text":"See also a much younger Brian from 40 years ago explaining pipeline [LINK] Similar but using redirects instead of pipes [VIDEO]"},{"id":25623757,"author":"LeoPanthera","month":"2021-01","story":"Reviving the 1973 Unix text to voice translator","stance":"reference","why":"Spell checker demo built from unix primitives.","quote":"","text":"URL with time: [VIDEO] In the same video, there's a fun moment where they demonstrate constructing a simple spell checker out of unix primitives, and the demonstrator comments that \"unix\" is a word unlikely to ever appear in the dictionary: [VIDEO] Of course, now it's in every dictionary!"},{"id":22859462,"author":"qqii","month":"2020-04","story":"Malleable Systems: Software must be as easy to change as it is to use it","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Unfortunately I think plan9 only remains this way by keeping the people who use it small, being elitist and somewhat unwelcoming[0]. That's not to say I wouldn't also recommend spending a weekend messing around. I was recently having a conversation about the unix philosophy on reddit[1]. As nix and software built for nix became more and more mainstream it becomes harder and harder to keep with the unix philosophy and the philosophy of this post. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["8dhp_20j0Ys",1]]},"GhsZUZmJvaM":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as an excellent explanation of why flying on Mars is hard and how the helicopter was built.","q":{"x":"a really excellent video that answers all your questions","i":26186351,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26186351,"author":"kibwen","month":"2021-02","story":"Perseverance Rover lands on Mars [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why thin Martian air makes the helicopter hard to fly.","quote":"a really excellent video that answers all your questions","text":"Here's a really excellent video that answers all your questions: [VIDEO] TL;DW: Martian atmosphere is so sparse that it's equivalent to flying at 100,000 feet on Earth. (The altitude record on Earth is 85,000 feet, set by the SR-71.) In order to fly at all the blades have to spin at nearly the (Martian) speed of sound. The drone wouldn't fly on Earth because the atmosphere is so dense that the blades would never make it up to speed."},{"id":28465973,"author":"ruchin_k","month":"2021-09","story":"After six months on Mars, NASA's tiny copter is still flying high","stance":"rec","why":"Technical complexity of flying in Mars's thin air.","quote":"the technical complexity required to fly on Mars was shocking to me","text":"Saw this Veritasium youtube video a year ago and the technical complexity required to fly on Mars was shocking to me. Since the air density is super low the blades need to spin at a speed which nears the speed of sound at the blade tips. Crossing the speed of sound causes mini sonic booms which could disrupt the flight entirely. Kudos to NASA scientists for this technological breakthrough! Sharing the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26693106,"author":"jshmrsn","month":"2021-04","story":"NASA's Mars helicopter Ingenuity touches down on the Red Planet","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Mars atmosphere density and helicopter altitude challenges.","quote":"Veritasium has a great video on the helicopter.","text":"Veritasium has a great video on the helicopter. Early on in the video they explain that Mars’ atmosphere is equivalent in density to Earth’s atmosphere at 100,000 feet, and the record altitude for helicopters on earth is 40,000 feet. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26705342,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2021-04","story":"NASA's helicopter survives first freezing night on Mars","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the helicopter power system and cold batteries.","quote":"This video from Veritasium does a good job of covering the helicopter power system","text":"On top of what others have said, we need advancement it batteries that can withstand low temperatures. Lithium ion batteries generally need to be kept above freezing temps. There have been some advances in capacitors that operate down to -100C, but their energy density makes them a non-ideal option for weight limited applications. This video from Veritasium does a good job of covering the helicopter power system: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26707850,"author":"vizzier","month":"2021-04","story":"NASA's helicopter survives first freezing night on Mars","stance":"rec","why":"Team discusses heating energy use, landing, and low-pressure flight.","quote":"Video from Veritasium about the helicopter that talk with the team about this.","text":"Video from Veritasium about the helicopter that talk with the team about this. He mentions that 2/3rds of the daily energy gathered is used for the purpose[1] Other information about the tech; camera & landing, energy use for heating, flight in low pressure atmosphere, the system to actually land the thing, is also really interesting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26924175,"author":"dirkt","month":"2021-04","story":"Mars Helicopter successfully completed its second flight","stance":"rec","why":"Explains rotor control and the counter-rotating rotor design.","quote":"The video also explains why the chose two contra-rotating rotors instead of a quad-copter design.","text":"This video [VIDEO] explains it: they can change the attack angle of rotor blades for different positions, which produces a torque, though you have to compensate for the gyroscopic effect, which lags behind. Humans cannot fly this, but computers can. The video also explains why the chose two contra-rotating rotors instead of a quad-copter design."},{"id":26178740,"author":"manicdee","month":"2021-02","story":"How NASA Designed a Helicopter That Could Fly Autonomously on Mars","stance":"rec","why":"Discussions with the team that built the helicopter.","quote":"containing some interesting discussions with the team that built it","text":"Veritasium did an episode 'Mars Helicopter' containing some interesting discussions with the team that built it. [VIDEO] nm it was already linked elsewhere"},{"id":26186429,"author":"kibwen","month":"2021-02","story":"Perseverance Rover lands on Mars [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Host visits the drone, interviews makers, covers technical aspects.","quote":"A great video where the host visits the drone, interviews its makers, and goes over the cool technical aspects of it and its mission","text":"A great video where the host visits the drone, interviews its makers, and goes over the cool technical aspects of it and its mission: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26295138,"author":"Brajeshwar","month":"2021-02","story":"The Mars Helicopter Is Online and Getting Ready to Fly","stance":"rec","why":"Details of the helicopter while it was being built.","quote":"You should watch Dr. Derek Muller of Veritasium","text":"You should watch Dr. Derek Muller of Veritasium, who did a video (2019) details of the Helicopter while it was being built. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26296054,"author":"kingo55","month":"2021-02","story":"The Mars Helicopter Is Online and Getting Ready to Fly","stance":"rec","why":"Rundown of how the helicopter was built.","quote":"Good rundown of how it was built","text":"It's not just atmosphere and g-forces. The cold temperatures mean the helicopter spends 2/3 of its battery power keeping the batteries and electronics from freezing. Good rundown of how it was built: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23562062,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-06","story":"NASA rushing to complete Mars launch before planet moves out of range","stance":"rec","why":"Veritasium story on the Mars helicopter.","quote":"Derek from Veritasium did a nice little story on it last summer","text":"Totally agree. It's obviously a high risk portion of the mission but I'm glad someone had the guts to propose it. Derek from Veritasium did a nice little story on it last summer - [VIDEO]"},{"id":26298718,"author":"smoe","month":"2021-03","story":"The Mars Helicopter Is Online and Getting Ready to Fly","stance":"reference","why":"Brief discussion of thin Mars atmosphere around the nine minute mark.","quote":"They briefly talk about it in this Veritasium Video at around the 9 minute mark","text":"They briefly talk about it in this Veritasium Video at around the 9 minute mark [VIDEO] He says, unlike in the movies, it is not really an issue. With 1% earth’s atmosphere there is very little matter actually hitting you."},{"id":26160424,"author":"doitLP","month":"2021-02","story":"NASA's Perseverance rover will attempt to land on Mars this week","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium video about the Mars helicopter.","quote":"Veritasium did a cool video about it a couple years ago.","text":"I’ll bet they worked with my friend who is the Deputy Surface Mission Manager for the rover. He gave us a little presentation of what he worked on, including the little helicopter that is attached to the underside of the rover. Veritasium did a cool video about it a couple years ago. [1] Kudos to everyone who worked on the project and got it launched during the pandemic! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26178999,"author":"SubMachineGhost","month":"2021-02","story":"How NASA Designed a Helicopter That Could Fly Autonomously on Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Engineer interview answering questions about Mars atmosphere and flight.","quote":"The answer comes from an interview with the engineers here","text":"Mars has 1% of earth's atmosphere, so it will not affect the vehicle, The answer comes from an interview with the engineers here : [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["wIC-iGDTU40",1],["QUcTsFe1PVs",1]]},"KTHOwgpMIiU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an illuminating, approachable explanation of distributed consensus.","q":{"x":"Heidi Howard is incredible and her work on distributed consensus is illuminating.","i":22999677,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22999677,"author":"jganetsk","month":"2020-04","story":"Paxos vs. Raft: Have we reached consensus on distributed consensus?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Makes distributed consensus easy to understand.","quote":"Heidi Howard is incredible and her work on distributed consensus is illuminating.","text":"Heidi Howard is incredible and her work on distributed consensus is illuminating. I think she has actually successfully cracked the cookie of \"making consensus easy\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":24770262,"author":"poooogles","month":"2020-10","story":"A Review of Consensus Protocols","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful talk on Heidi Howard's consensus work.","quote":"This talk helped me greatly","text":">A more recent development is Heidi Howard's work Glad someone has mentioned her here. This talk helped me greatly [VIDEO] ."},{"id":24285592,"author":"j-pb","month":"2020-08","story":"Keeping CALM: When Distributed Consistency Is Easy","stance":"rec","why":"Talk connecting CALM, monotonicity and consensus algorithms.","quote":"There's a really great talk on this by Heidi Howard","text":"Tbh I think you got it backwards. CALM is actually very much about eventual consistency. The gist is basically, that distributed systems are only eventually consistent iff they are logically monotonic. Coordination as used in this article basically means \"needs to be decided by an authority\". But because of CALM we know that in order to elect an authority/leader, you need a consensus algorithm that actually has monotonicity at it's core, like raft. There's a really great talk on this by Heidi Howard: [VIDEO] I actually don't think that database isolation levels are a good way to talk about this stuff, because they are practical guarantees, not theoretical ones. Snapshot isolation for example also requires a leader. From a theoretical point of view there is no in between (at least none that I can see), either you are eventually consistent or you're not, either you are monotonic or you're not."},{"id":20850128,"author":"richdougherty","month":"2019-09","story":"The Paxos Algorithm, or How to Win a Turing Award","stance":"rec","why":"Heidi Howard's generalisations of Paxos using immutable state.","quote":"Check out Heidi Howard's work for some nice generalisations and variations of Paxos.","text":"Check out Heidi Howard's work for some nice generalisations and variations of Paxos. Her approach using immutable state makes things more tractable. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1]]},"wtV5ev6813I":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a comparison of German rehabilitative prisons with American retributive justice, backed by statistics.","q":{"x":"the whole talk is rather worthwhile to watch","i":25515804,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":32622613,"author":"Lev1a","month":"2022-08","story":"At $249 per day, prison stays leave ex-inmates deep in debt","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compares American prisons with the civilized world","quote":"this is absolutely worth a watch","text":"On the topic of prison systems in America vs. the civilized world, this is absolutely worth a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27168373,"author":"Lev1a","month":"2021-05","story":"Joe Ligon: America's 'longest juvenile lifer' on 68 years in prison","stance":"rec","why":"American visits German prison, with stats comparing prison systems","quote":"A bunch of stats and comparisons to underline the effects of different prison systems","text":"Or this talk about one of Denmarks neighbours (Germany) by an American, Jewish, (grand-)son of Holocaust survivors visiting a German prison with a bunch of other Americans (\"60 Minutes\" reporter, some Governor, a DA, a convicted murderer etc.). A bunch of stats and comparisons to underline the effects of different prison systems: [VIDEO] And the related segment from \"60 Minutes\": [LINK]"},{"id":25515804,"author":"freeflight","month":"2020-12","story":"In 1980s Los Angeles, a bank was robbed every hour (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Stats on crime drop without mass incarceration","quote":"the whole talk is rather worthwhile to watch","text":"It's debatable if the police state was actually responsible for that drop in crime. Afaik Canada saw a comparable drop in crime, over the same time-period, without starting to mass incarcerate people [0] [0] [VIDEO] (Relevant stats and discussion start around 6:45, but the whole talk is rather worthwhile to watch)"},{"id":14392416,"author":"okket","month":"2017-05","story":"Topics in Core Mathematics at Graterford Prison","stance":"rec","why":"German resocialization versus US retributive justice","quote":"I'd recommend to watch this talk about the German prison system from an American perspective.","text":"I'd recommend to watch this talk about the German prison system from an American perspective. The main point is the difference between European resocialization and US retributive justice. Although this may show the bright sides here in Germany a little bit too bright, they do exist. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29399577,"author":"freeflight","month":"2021-12","story":"The American prison system’s war on reading","stance":"reference","why":"Case for fundamental change in US prison policy","quote":"","text":"When you design for the \"edge cases\" you end up with a bunch of Supermaxes were people vegitate 23 hours a day in solitary, which is btw commonly considered torture [0] Maybe the goal shouldn't be to design for \"unchecked edge cases to apply maximum punishment\" but rather a fundamental change in US prison and incarceration policies [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29369933,"author":"freeflight","month":"2021-11","story":"Without parental leave I might be dead","stance":"reference","why":"Canada's crime and incarceration resemble European systems","quote":"","text":"> That social stigma will not work in big, diverse and marred by identity politics countries like the US or even Canada (in my opinion). The social system analogue to the US is not Canada, Canada's policies in that regard are way more resembling of European systems than the US's, that applies not only to welfare systems, but also crime and incarceration [0]. As such, Canada is very much an example of how at least part of these policies can also work in North America, even with its allegedly exceptional scale and diversity. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27190429,"author":"freeflight","month":"2021-05","story":"A police dog who cried drugs at every traffic stop","stance":"reference","why":"Canada's crime drop without mass incarceration","quote":"","text":"> He stated \"modern policing,\" which I would guess is since the 1980s and the drug war, then 9/11. All this is fairly new, and started within my lifetime. This is \"normal\" to the younger generation, but it's horrifies me. Yup, the US incarceration rate started rising to these insane current day levels around the 1980s [0] Interestingly enough, the incarceration rate kept rising even while crime was going down, but crime going down wasn't a US exclusive phenomena, Canada saw the same trend without mass incarcerating people. [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23528555,"author":"freeflight","month":"2020-06","story":"George Floyd Protest – police brutality videos on Twitter","stance":"reference","why":"Canada's crime rates versus US despite less incarceration","quote":"","text":"> I think every society with lower crime has significantly smaller police brutality Crime-rates do not correlate with police brutality and mass incarceration. Canada locks up way fewer people, has way less harsh sentencing than the US, yet Canadian crime rates are not that different from those of the US [0]. It's also really weird to evoke HK in this discussion when the current police response in the US is way worse than anything reported out of HK. Particularly in the context that for the longest time the HK police actually had a rather splendid international reputation [1]. While US police had a \"Dirty Harry\" like reputation for several decades now, something that's reached its current peak with the whole \"blueline\" mentality and the glorification of comic vigilantes like the Punisher as a symbol for law enforcement. As such a whole lot of this is rooted very deeply in policing and incarceration culture and not some countries being inherently \"more criminal\" than others. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":22649351,"author":"jeffreygoesto","month":"2020-03","story":"The Leader of the Free World Gives a Speech, and She Nails It","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure that incarceration (and the circumstances under which it usually happens) in the US is increasing the \"freedom\". See i.e. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gEKSpZPByD0",1],["SrU9YDoXE88",1],["U6qZPx4uD0g",1],["lyqt6u5_sHA",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1],["lKQ0yaEJjok",1],["8LGDM9exlZw",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1]]},"24R8JObNNQ4":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a rare, eye-opening look inside North Korea, though one notes such tours are staged.","q":{"x":"Vice has a three-part 1-hour-total documentary on North Korea with footage from the country.","i":16276842,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":35946000,"author":"MobileVet","month":"2023-05","story":"Vice, Decayed Digital Colossus, Files for Bankruptcy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rare footage from inside North Korea with a compelling host.","quote":"Very eye opening","text":"I ran across their North Korea report when it was posted 11 years ago and was an instant fan. Shane was compelling and of course the topic was fascinating. Very few people had gotten in there and brought film out like that before, that I had seen at least. Very eye opening [VIDEO]"},{"id":19055530,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-02","story":"Vice Media to Reorganize, Lay Off 10 Percent of Staff","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating Vice coverage of North Korea that won the commenter over.","quote":"I was introduced to Vice with their fascinating coverage of North Korea","text":"IIRC, I was introduced to Vice with their fascinating coverage of North Korea: [VIDEO] (first trip - Shane Smith was banned from the country as a result) [LINK] (second trip years later, where they got in under the guise of a Basketball game) Unfortunately it's been increasingly hit or miss ever since and now their process seems to be along these lines: [LINK] Also, how is it that so many of these media companies are having layoffs at almost exactly the same time? You'd think it'd be more scattered..."},{"id":16276842,"author":"gkya","month":"2018-01","story":"What’s North Korea like?","stance":"rec","why":"Footage from inside North Korea.","quote":"Vice has a three-part 1-hour-total documentary on North Korea with footage from the country.","text":"Vice has a three-part 1-hour-total documentary on North Korea with footage from the country. Part one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5087186,"author":"obstacle1","month":"2013-01","story":"Sophie In North Korea","stance":"reference","why":"Vice visit to North Korea with staged hotels and entertainment.","quote":"There is a documentary on YouTube where Shane Smith (of Vice mag) visits North Korea.","text":"There is a documentary on YouTube where Shane Smith (of Vice mag) visits North Korea. He is put up in a fake hotel, given fake ceremonial dinners, fakely entertained in fake karaoke places, and so on. Seems like the same situation as the author of this article, the difference being Smith is explicitly not under any illusions of authenticity. Link to documentary: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27648891,"author":"tablespoon","month":"2021-06","story":"YouTube takes down Xinjiang videos, forcing rights group to seek alternative","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The highly produced tour would be superfluous (and in this case, potentially dangerous).","text":"> Each time I recommend them to watch YT videos from non main-stream media, no one reply back with what they found out... Youtube videos from randos aren't very good sources, period, especially on topics like this. I mean, that guy seems like laowhy86's mirror universe twin. > And apparently everyone is happy to bash the credit of mainstream media, except for China/XInjiang, it's like suddenly a bunch of guys never set foot in Xinjiang, start to trusting another bunch of guys also never set foot in Xinjiang, and claim that they are good friends all along... Even the people who bash the MSM rely on it for pretty much all of their information about the outside world. Also, you put far too much weight on \"setting foot\" somewhere. For instance, would taking a regime-lead guided tour of North Korea (like this [VIDEO] ) really help anyone understand North Korea at all? It's probably the only way to \"set foot\" there as a foreigner. The answer is no. You'd get much better understanding reviewing documents (both officially distributed and otherwise), interviewing defectors, and maybe even looking at satellite photos. The highly produced tour would be superfluous (and in this case, potentially dangerous)."}],"r":[["PhNUjg9X4g8",1],["GKYCA3UsmrU",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["OKY6BGcx37k",1],["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1],["4Z2uzEM0ugY",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["d1EB1zsxW0k",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1]]},"3BR8tK-LuB0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the simplest, most approachable introduction to quaternions, useful for people working in games and robotics.","q":{"x":"Numberphile's explanation of Quaternions is the simplest I have seen so far","i":17938976,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17938976,"author":"codesushi42","month":"2018-09","story":"Quaternions: the Strange Numbers That Birthed Modern Algebra","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simplest explanation of quaternions, useful for games and robotics.","quote":"Numberphile's explanation of Quaternions is the simplest I have seen so far","text":"Numberphile's explanation of Quaternions is the simplest I have seen so far: [VIDEO] Quaternions are very important to know if you are doing any kind of 3D math. For instance, game programming or SLAM for robotics."},{"id":11492267,"author":"skapadia","month":"2016-04","story":"Calculus Learning Guide","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds quaternions up from real and complex numbers.","quote":"I also love the Numberphile explanation of quaternions (building up from real to complex numbers, etc.)","text":"Kalid - also your explanation of complex numbers is absolutely amazing. Sure I could manipulate them by memorizing rules, but I never really knew what they meant or why they were important. I also love the Numberphile explanation of quaternions (building up from real to complex numbers, etc.) - [VIDEO] -Sujan"},{"id":18314949,"author":"Tempest1981","month":"2018-10","story":"Visualizing quaternions: An explorable video series","stance":"rec","why":"Easier-to-grasp introduction to quaternions.","quote":"This video might be easier to grasp","text":"This video might be easier to grasp: Fantastic Quaternions - Numberphile [VIDEO] It also helped me to have a use-case in mind —- a motivation. Like maybe using the accelerometer/compasss in your phone to perform image stabilization or AR."}],"r":[["tP-Ipsat90c",1],["sLCHg9mUBag",1]]},"3ka4KY7TMTU":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best account of what Self made possible and what its team went on to do.","q":{"x":"explains the history, philosophy, successes, and failures of the Self project","i":31555457,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":1957908,"author":"sb","month":"2010-12","story":"The status of the Self language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Conveys what the Self language made possible, with a few gems.","quote":"It contains a few gems, and is really worthwhile watching.","text":"To get a feeling for the Self language and what was (is) possible, you should (if you can, of course) take the time to watch the video: [VIDEO] It contains a few gems, and is really worthwhile watching. (Disclaimer, I think Dave Ungar has done tremendous things for PLDI; interested people should definitely check out his UC-Berkeley PhD thesis of 1986, easily one of the best I have ever seen...)"},{"id":1520576,"author":"mark_h","month":"2010-07","story":"Self language still alive - new release for Linux, Mac","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ungar's talk on Self, ending with what the project's people did next.","quote":"David Unger gave a great talk recently on this","text":"David Unger gave a great talk recently on this: [VIDEO] (recorded at Stanford, but it's a reprise of his acceptance speech on receiving the Dahl-Nygaard award from memory) He has a role-call at the end of what the people involved in the self project went on to do afterwards, and it's staggering: some of the engineers of the original Google architecture, what became Hotspot as you say, Lars Bak (of Chrome V8) as someone else has pointed out... just an amazing list."},{"id":31555457,"author":"rileyphone","month":"2022-05","story":"Lisp, Smalltalk, and the power of symmetry (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Ungar explains Self's history, philosophy, successes and failures.","quote":"explains the history, philosophy, successes, and failures of the Self project","text":"What was really interesting about Self was its optimizability - they were able to implement a Smalltalk on Self that was much faster than the contemporary Smalltalk systems. The impression I got from poking around at Self was that there was a class/inheritance system informally approximated in the prototypical relations of the objects and traits, but that doesn't mean you can't formally define one. For anyone curious, I recommend this talk [0] by David Ungar, one of Self's creators, that explains the history, philosophy, successes, and failures of the Self project. It's a shame more didn't get done with it (though V8, arguably the most important language runtime, is very similar). Someone has even been making a Zig version of Self recently [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":45710578,"author":"hayley-patton","month":"2025-10","story":"An Efficient Implementation of SELF (1989) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Ungar recalls Self was thought impossible to run efficiently.","quote":"we thought it was impossible to make this language run efficiently","text":"[0] is Self and Self: Whys and Wherefores < [VIDEO] > > And at the time, we thought it was impossible to make this language run efficiently, because it did all these things that were more abstract than languages of the time ..."},{"id":33527210,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-11","story":"The influence of Self","stance":"reference","why":"Ungar argues the real legacy of ideas is the people.","quote":"the real legacy is the people.","text":"Self and Self: Whys and Wherefores (September 30, 2009) David Unger, from IBM Research, discusses how his experience in computer science has led him to the conclusion that even if your ideas succeed, the real legacy is the people. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5zkC9IMQmYA",1],["ShEez0JkOFw",1],["UGhfB-NICzg",1]]},"E0W1ZZYIV8o":{"wy":"Cited in energy debates for its back-of-envelope scale of the transition and the land and storage limits of wind and solar.","q":{"x":"I found this ted talk on the feasibility of various renewable energy sources quite eye opening","i":19021721,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":24149665,"author":"kinjba1","month":"2020-08","story":"Air pollution is worse than we thought","stance":"rec","why":"Downsides of renewables: land use and storage","quote":"showing the downside of renewable energy - such as the enormous land usage and reliable energy storage","text":"No energy source [that we've discovered so far] is perfect. All have downsides. Sadly, living in denial of that fact is more popular than ever, but that's a separate topic. Here are a couple TED talks showing the downside of renewable energy - such as the enormous land usage and reliable energy storage needed to compete with nuclear or fossil. Why renewables can’t save the planet [LINK] A reality check on renewables [VIDEO]"},{"id":19451968,"author":"neilwilson","month":"2019-03","story":"Australia has enough solar, wind storage in pipeline to go 100% renewables","stance":"rec","why":"Energy density per head limits wind and solar","quote":"Good video by the late David Mackay on that point here.","text":"Not everywhere in the world has the land area to do wind and solar. In fact pretty much everywhere other than Russia, Australia, Canada, the US and Brazil. There the energy density per head of population is too high for renewables. Good video by the late David Mackay on that point here. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19021721,"author":"CuriousSkeptic","month":"2019-01","story":"Study tracks dramatic retreat of Canadian glaciers","stance":"rec","why":"Feasibility of various renewable sources","quote":"I found this ted talk on the feasibility of various renewable energy sources quite eye opening","text":"On that topic, I found this ted talk on the feasibility of various renewable energy sources quite eye opening [VIDEO]"},{"id":18794261,"author":"CuriousSkeptic","month":"2018-12","story":"As oil and gas exports surge, West Texas becomes the world’s “extraction colony”","stance":"rec","why":"Scale of the energy transition needed","quote":"","text":"Well, a bunch of countries have more or less agreed on aiming for no more than 1.5 degrees warmer. IPCC made a report on what that would entail stating basically that even with an overnight abolishment it cannot be reached. But that if we assume it feasible to use afforestation and other techniques to extract CO2 from the atmosphere in the future we might have chance to reach that target for 2100 given we stop using fossil fuels by 2030. Given the scale of the transition necessary, overnight or 11-years is more or less the same time frame Edit: Great TED-talk on the scale of what’s needed [VIDEO]"},{"id":27226538,"author":"kinjba11","month":"2021-05","story":"“Energy in the Future”: a time capsule of energy concerns from 1953","stance":"reference","why":"Solar and wind need space","quote":"Briefly: solar and wind energy requires sun or wind and tons of space relative to fossil/nuclear.","text":"Briefly: solar and wind energy requires sun or wind and tons of space relative to fossil/nuclear. Not every place has those. \"A reality check on renewables\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":26300870,"author":"wiz21c","month":"2021-03","story":"Why Nuclear Must Be Part Of The Energy Solution (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the maths of building renewables","quote":"","text":"What about the resources needed to build the solar and wind infrastructures ? Metal, extraction of that metal, rare earths,... ? I'm all for solar and wind and whatever, but at some point there are some maths to do... [VIDEO]"},{"id":19570220,"author":"ppf","month":"2019-04","story":"The Biggest Saudi Oil Field Is Fading Faster Than Anyone Guessed","stance":"reference","why":"Space limits of renewables","quote":"There's also space limits - see","text":"\"Renewable\" doesn't mean \"free energy\". Constructing renewable energy sources (wind, solar, etc) takes more energy than constructing an equivalent non-renewable energy source - this is the EROEI (energy returned on energy invested). There's also space limits - see [VIDEO] What's apparent to me is that we live in a unique era where energy is abundant and cheap, which may never be repeated (unless fusion power is developed)."},{"id":13994513,"author":"spodek","month":"2017-03","story":"Eigg – A small Scottish isle that runs on 90-95% renewable energy","stance":"reference","why":"MacKay's TED talk alongside his book","quote":"","text":"If you haven't read Sustainable Energy Without the Hot Air , by David Mackay, I recommend it. Free download: [LINK] About the author: David MacKay FRS is the Regius Professor of Engineering at the University of Cambridge. He studied Natural Sciences at Cambridge and then obtained his PhD in Computation and Neural Systems at the California Institute of Technology. He returned to Cambridge as a Royal Society research fellow at Darwin College. He is internationally known for his research in machine learning, information theory, and communication systems, including the invention of Dasher, a software interface that enables efficient communication in any language with any muscle. He was appointed a Lecturer in the Department of Physics at Cambridge in 1995 and was a Professor in the Department of Physics from 2003 to 2013. Since 2005, he has devoted much of his time to public teaching about energy. He is a Fellow of the Royal Society. Nine months after the publication of 'Sustainable Energy - without the hot air', David MacKay was appointed Chief Scientific Advisor to the Department of Energy and Climate Change. TED talk: [VIDEO] Harvard talk: [LINK]"}],"r":[["N-yALPEpV4w",1],["l7TONauJGfc",1],["GvtNyOzGogc",1]]},"G-WO-z-QuWI":{"wy":"Offered as a readable explainer whenever Venus terraforming or alternatives to Mars colonization come up.","q":{"x":"There is a decent kurzgesagt video explaining what transforming a new planet would look like","i":28649894,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":46931495,"author":"chasil","month":"2026-02","story":"Where did all the starships go?","stance":"rec","why":"Kurzgesagt explainer on how terraforming Venus could be done","quote":"The Kurzgesagt video on teraforming Venus is interesting.","text":"The Kurzgesagt video on teraforming Venus is interesting. It only takes a thousand years, assuming perfect execution. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31923158,"author":"headcanon","month":"2022-06","story":"MIT researchers propose Brazil-sized fleet of “space bubbles” to cool the Earth","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on similar technique for terraforming Venus","quote":"There's a good video on this that talks about how a similar technique might be used to terraform Venus","text":"There's a good video on this that talks about how a similar technique might be used to terraform Venus over hundreds of years [0] The main physics problem I see is that Langrange points are naturally unstable [1], so the bubbles would need to be constantly correcting their orbit. This problem would be compounded by solar radiation \"blowing\" the bubbles back to Earth. There are clever ways to mitigate this for other designs which are addressed in the video, but I don't see it addressed in the article. Edit: it looks like it is addressed somewhat in the source article linked in another comment, by placing the bubbles a bit further towards the sun than the L1 point to compensate for solar winds. Also, If a bubble does eventually become unstable, they may be cheap enough to destroy it and send another one up. However, the primary problem I see with this approach is that we are simply not politically ready as a species to attempt a global-scale engineering project like that. Some countries may find that climate change actually benefits them [2], or may even impact a geopolitical rival even worse. These countries may be inclined to send a few missiles toward L1 if thats the case. Edit: Additionally, even if this does succeed, countries may see this as an excuse to keep burning stuff despite the additional negative consequences of carbon emissions [3] [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] ."},{"id":28649894,"author":"Gigachad","month":"2021-09","story":"Engineers are developing new ways to convert greenhouse gases to fuel","stance":"rec","why":"Decent explainer on what terraforming a planet involves","quote":"There is a decent kurzgesagt video explaining what transforming a new planet would look like","text":"There is a decent kurzgesagt video explaining what transforming a new planet would look like [VIDEO] Essentially it would be almost infinitely easier to reverse all climate change on earth than to make another planet livable but it is in theory possible."},{"id":27776330,"author":"nerpderp82","month":"2021-07","story":"A Roadmap to Interstellar Flight (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how to terraform Venus","quote":"You might enjoy this video on terraforming Venus,","text":"You might enjoy this video on terraforming Venus, [VIDEO] I think we should consider the whole solar system our galactic ship while making sure to keep the current habitation quarters in top notch shape."},{"id":43835666,"author":"chasil","month":"2025-04","story":"After 53 years, a failed Soviet Venus spacecraft is crashing back to Earth","stance":"reference","why":"Sci-fi but possibly feasible Venus terraforming","quote":"This Kurzgesagt video is equally sci-fi","text":"This Kurzgesagt video is equally sci-fi, but of the \"potentially possible if we wanted to bankrupt the planet\" variety. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40629893,"author":"avmich","month":"2024-06","story":"Everything about Mars is the worst (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Suggestions that make Venus seem viable","quote":"","text":"You'd think Venus would be much worse in this regard than Mars. Then you get these suggestions - [VIDEO] - and start thinking..."},{"id":36382472,"author":"barbariangrunge","month":"2023-06","story":"Mars Declared Unsafe For Humans: No one can survive for longer than four years","stance":"reference","why":"Alternative to Mars colonization","quote":"How to terraform Venus quickly","text":"A friend showed me this once while we were taking about how radiation sort of makes mars colonization unviable How to terraform Venus quickly: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32037846,"author":"downrightmike","month":"2022-07","story":"Mars Colonization Will Never Happen","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim Venus can become Earth-like in centuries","quote":"It would only take a few hundred years to turn Venus into a pretty close Earth analog.","text":"It would only take a few hundred years to turn Venus into a pretty close Earth analog. It has issues to be dealt with, but would really have much more resources available that Mars. Even solar power would be more viable on Venus than Mars. Plus it would give us a good midway point to start planet harvesting Mercury if we wanted it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30095508,"author":"TrainedMonkey","month":"2022-01","story":"How the Soviets put a lander on Venus","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzgesagt video on terraforming Venus","quote":"Kurzgesagt did a video on terraforming Venus","text":"Kurzgesagt did a video on terraforming Venus [VIDEO] My amateurish take on it - total effort to terraform Venus might be lower, however exploring/colonizing Mars is much easier."}],"r":[["lXfEK8G8CUI",1]]},"WxOp5mBY9IY":{"wy":"HN returns to it as a legendary moment in tech history and as a precedent for strategic deals between rivals, such as in the Nokia and Microsoft story.","q":{"x":"the legendary video of Bill Gates appearing on the big screen at the Macworld Boston Keynote 1997","i":1382007,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":24545599,"author":"tambourine_man","month":"2020-09","story":"New Steve Jobs recordings from the '80s released","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs taking questions, crowd booing Gates","quote":"I've seen this many times, so great.","text":"I've seen this many times, so great. Imagine Cook taking random developer's questions at WWDC today. In that same year, the famous Gates at the big screen speech. The audience was booing. Can you imagine something even close to that today? [VIDEO]"},{"id":1382007,"author":"ugh","month":"2010-05","story":"Microsoft to invest $150 million in Apple (1997)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Legendary Macworld 1997 moment with Bill Gates on the big screen","quote":"the legendary video of Bill Gates appearing on the big screen at the Macworld Boston Keynote 1997","text":"What? A link to this story and no one linked to the legendary video of Bill Gates appearing on the big screen at the Macworld Boston Keynote 1997, looming large over Steve Jobs and the crowd booing? Here, I will do that: [VIDEO] (skip to 3:30 if you want to see how the investment is received, skip to 4:40 if you want to see Bill Gates)"},{"id":2213809,"author":"charleso","month":"2011-02","story":"Mike Elgan: Why Nokia is toast","stance":"rec","why":"Apple's 1997 Microsoft deal as precedent for Nokia","quote":"If you think Nokia is doomed, you might want to re-watch that video.","text":"All of this 'Nokia is doomed because of their Microsoft partnership' talk reminds me of when Apple announced something similar back in 1997. Anyone remember this Steve Jobs talk from when Apple was on the financial ropes? [VIDEO] If you think Nokia is doomed, you might want to re-watch that video. Things didn't turn out quite so bad for Apple."},{"id":2213863,"author":"charleso","month":"2011-02","story":"Nokia to get 'huge' payments from Microsoft  ","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs's 1997 presentation of the Microsoft partnership","quote":"you may want to watch this Steve Jobs Macworld 1997 presentation discussing their joint venture","text":"How much in the minority am I for thinking this is a good plan for Nokia? Echoing my post in another thread, Apple received $150 million in financing from Microsoft back in 1997 in exchange for including IE on the Mac (among other partnership goodies). If you don't recall that period of computer history (when Apple almost went bust), you may want to watch this Steve Jobs Macworld 1997 presentation discussing their joint venture: [VIDEO] \"Kneeling before Zod\" didn't turn out too badly for Apple."},{"id":40706443,"author":"adolph","month":"2024-06","story":"First look at the upcoming Starlink Mini","stance":"reference","why":"Apple/Microsoft 1997 deal as analogy for Starlink competition","quote":"","text":"> a constellation that needs to launch thousands of satellites. If I were the folks at Kuiper or any other would-be LEO RF communication constellation: * Starlink is proving the value prop to investors and consumers * This is an early stage this sector--there will be tech improvements * Improvements in satellites mean competitors can enter at vN+1 * SpaceX/Starlink has some anti-trust risk--they might throw a bone to a weak \"competitor\" a la Apple/Microsoft 1997 [0] 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27841905,"author":"Lammy","month":"2021-07","story":"Microsoft exec: “Summary of meeting with Steve Jobs\"","stance":"reference","why":"Crowd booing at the IE default browser announcement","quote":"Announcing the browser deal is the only time I heard an entire crowd boo","text":"Mac OS 8.x and 9.x shipped with both Netscape and IE4, 4.5, then 5, but IE was the default for the \"Browse the Web\" alias on the Desktop of new installations. Mac OS X 10.0 shipped with a very early Carbonized IE 5.0 that was unstable enough to earn a \"prerelease\" label on the startup screen. At the time I remember a lot of people did actually prefer to use the Classic OS version of IE 5.0 over the IE beta, at least until OmniWeb 4.0 came out around the same time as OS X 10.1 [LINK] Announcing the browser deal is the only time I heard an entire crowd boo Stebe [VIDEO] It's also amusing how Steve was still obviously using Concurrence instead of a Mac to do these early iCEO-era presentations [LINK]"},{"id":26400817,"author":"Lammy","month":"2021-03","story":"Internet Explorer is evil (1996)","stance":"reference","why":"Crowd booing Jobs over IE default browser","quote":"the only time I can remember hearing an entire crowd boo","text":"I always thought tabs were an Opera invention, or maybe one of those IE shells like Maxthon or Neoplanet? IE5:mac was a fantastic browser though and was widely regarded as having the best CSS1 conformance of its time, as well as some cool features like an eBay auction tracker sidebar that seem quaint today but were very useful to me at the time: [LINK] On the flip side, the only time I can remember hearing an entire crowd boo Stebe Jovs was when he announced that IE 4.5 would ship as the default browser in Mac OS 8: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26401694,"author":"veidelis","month":"2021-03","story":"Internet Explorer is evil (1996)","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs booed announcing IE as default Mac browser","quote":"Steve Jobs was loudly BOOED as he announced that IE would be the default browser.","text":"\"And as if forcing IE on all Windows users wasn't bad enough, Microsoft forced Apple to bundle IE for Mac as their default browser instead of Netscape. If Apple refused, Microsoft would discontinue MS-Office for Mac. Steve Jobs was loudly BOOED as he announced that IE would be the default browser.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":24663273,"author":"thedudeabides5","month":"2020-10","story":"Microsoft Excel’s 35th birthday","stance":"reference","why":"Excel on Mac was part of Microsoft's 1997 Apple investment","quote":"","text":"Pour one out for Visicalc, the precursor to Excel/Lotus and the 'killer app' of 1979 for the Apple II! [LINK] Flash forward to 1997, and a key part of the deal for MS investing in Apple is...you guessed it, Excel on the mac. [VIDEO] Funny to think about next time some engineer is telling you how this or that new cloud software will 'totally replace spreadsheets.' Spreadsheets powered the personal computing revolution - single handedly determined the winner of the OS wars (when people fled to first DOS and then windows after MSFT build Lotus into Excel). Like em or hate em, spreadsheets are here to stay."}],"r":[["HUEvRyemKSg",1]]},"lPgZfhnCAdI":{"wy":"Commenters call it a favorite Daily Show clip and link it as a ready answer to climate-skeptic arguments.","q":{"x":"my all time favorite Daily Show clip, Burn Notice","i":12609189,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12609189,"author":"atmosx","month":"2016-09","story":"Atmospheric carbon dioxide passes 400ppm","stance":"strong_rec","why":"All-time favorite Daily Show clip on climate skepticism.","quote":"my all time favorite Daily Show clip, Burn Notice","text":"Given recent events[1], one cannot stress the word deep enough. To be honest though, it's not just the republican candidate who thinks that global warming is a non-issue. It's the entire Republican party. Of course, I can't help but referring my all time favorite Daily Show clip, Burn Notice [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11929433,"author":"atmosx","month":"2016-06","story":"May is Earth's hottest on record","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Personal favorite Daily Show episode.","quote":"It's my favourite episode of the daily show.","text":"> let alone convincing people that a problem actually exists. I love this: [VIDEO] It's my favourite episode of the daily show."},{"id":10341965,"author":"lbenes","month":"2015-10","story":"Gains in Antarctic ice might offset losses","stance":"rec","why":"Jon Stewart answers the climate denier argument.","quote":"Jon Stewart answers your question here","text":"Watch Fox new much? Climate deniers love this argument. Jon Stewart answers your question here: [VIDEO] While looking for the clip I found this gem: [LINK]"},{"id":24504105,"author":"mam2","month":"2020-09","story":"ByteDance won't sell TikTok U.S. operations to Oracle or Microsoft: CGTN","stance":"reference","why":"Example of reframing technique used by Jon Stewart.","quote":"","text":"This is not unsubstantiative. It's called reframing the person's point in a new light, and it's valid debating technique, although indeed it often points out stupid parts in the other persons view points. John steward did it all the time in his show. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44564683,"author":"atmosx","month":"2025-07","story":"Random selection is necessary to create stable meritocratic institutions","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"you can't get much worse than the 2014 Committee for Science, Space and Technology","text":"I believe that it worked somewhat fine in Athens (500th century BC): [LINK] Plus, you can't get much worse than the 2014 Committee for Science, Space and Technology: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41913604,"author":"rolandog","month":"2024-10","story":"A near impossible literacy test Louisiana used to suppress the black vote","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The fact that literacy and competency testing were misused in the past is no excuse to allow illiterates and incompetents to determine the course of our civilization. I'm glad we both agree that more money should abundantly be allotted to education budgets, and making higher quality education more accessible — without discrimination — to the masses. I do view the literacy and competency tests as a tool that should be pointing in the other direction: at all elected and non-elected officials; exhibit A, the United States House of Representatives Committee on Science, Space and Technology of 2014 [0] (relevant timestamp: 02:47). [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10767158,"author":"atmosx","month":"2015-12","story":"Juniper hack has U.S. fearing foreign infiltration","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> NIST never saw it coming. How so? The NSA is a spy agency, they are the one institution that you clearly should NOT ask. BTW I love the USA Committee on Science, Space and Technology[1], full of geniuses. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cjuGCJJUGsg",1]]},"6ZYLp5uX9Yw":{"wy":"Used as an example of prototyping music and, in a Copilot copyright thread, of a handmade reconstruction that sounds like the original.","q":{"x":"There are legendary Prodigy tracks reconstructions by Jim Pavloff","i":41385909,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41385909,"author":"xpl","month":"2024-08","story":"Judge dismisses majority of GitHub Copilot copyright claims","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Handmade Prodigy reconstruction that sounds like the original","quote":"Those pieces sound exactly the same as the original","text":"There are legendary Prodigy tracks reconstructions by Jim Pavloff, see: 1. \"Smack my bitch up\" [LINK] 2. \"Voodoo people\" [VIDEO] Those pieces sound exactly the same as the original, but it does not violate copyright because they've been produced by hand. Mind blowing!"},{"id":5379618,"author":"mlla","month":"2013-03","story":"I analyzed the chords to 1300 songs for patterns. Part 3 – Interactive Discovery","stance":"rec","why":"Recreates Prodigy tunes from their listed samples","quote":"Jim Pavloff has uploaded to YouTube some cool videos","text":"A bit on a same note (pun intended), Jim Pavloff has uploaded to YouTube some cool videos, where he re-creates some of the tunes by The Prodigy based on the listed samples, e.g. Voodoo People ( [VIDEO] ) and Firestarter ( [LINK] )"},{"id":3090222,"author":"przemoc","month":"2011-10","story":"Ask HN: What are your music prototyping solutions?","stance":"rec","why":"Recreating Voodoo People in Ableton, shown as prototyping example","quote":"Here is some nice stuff.","text":"Thanks for commenting. Ableton Live is undoubtedly a great software. AFAIK it's not suited for rapid prototyping. Here is some nice stuff. Just in case somebody haven't seen it yet. Making of \"The Prodigy - Voodoo People\" in Ableton by Jim Pavloff [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eU5Dn-WaElI",1]]},"C8--cXwuuFQ":{"wy":"HN commenters say it permanently changed how they think about inspecting binary data, and point to it as the origin of visual binary analysis tools.","q":{"x":"after having seen Christopher Domas present \"The Future of RE: Dynamic Binary Visualization\" my mind was forever blown.","i":13303310,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13303310,"author":"LargoLasskhyfv","month":"2017-01","story":"Unexpected Risks Found in Editing Genes to Prevent Inherited Disorders","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dynamic binary visualization that makes hexdump-era tooling seem stone-age","quote":"after having seen Christopher Domas present \"The Future of RE: Dynamic Binary Visualization\" my mind was forever blown.","text":"Maybe it's a matter of tooling. I don't want to appear disrespectful, but after having seen Christopher Domas present \"The Future of RE: Dynamic Binary Visualization\" my mind was forever blown. Working with hexdumps, debuggers, disassemblers etc. seems so stone-agey compared to that. Like so much in IT in general. Anyways, caveman CAN more than crawl, obviously ;-) So, if you have up to an hour of time to waste, maybe you can have your mind blown to cantor dust too? [LINK] [VIDEO] Something very similar seems to be developed here: [LINK] Happy New Cyber! edit: spelling"},{"id":41434445,"author":"hoosieree","month":"2024-09","story":"Show HN: Bin-graph: Visualize binary files","stance":"rec","why":"Demo with 3D pan, tilt and zoom trigraph visualizations","quote":"The 3d pan/tilt/zoom visualizations of the trigraphs are especially nice to look at.","text":"Have you checked out this demo? [VIDEO] The 3d pan/tilt/zoom visualizations of the trigraphs are especially nice to look at."},{"id":38318749,"author":"oldmanludd","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Are There Viewers for Memory Layout?","stance":"rec","why":"Cantor.dust, an interesting visualization tool for binary data","quote":"cantor.dust is an interesting visualization tool for binary data.","text":"I don't think it's quite what you're looking for but cantor.dust is an interesting visualization tool for binary data. Security conference presentation from a decade ago: [VIDEO] Release of open-source Ghidra plugin in 2020: [LINK]"},{"id":10020631,"author":"jsprogrammer","month":"2015-08","story":"X86 rootkit","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by the author vouching for his credibility","quote":"The author is legit. Here's a somewhat recent talk he gave","text":"Yep. I didn't read most of the last 1/2-1/3 of the slides, but it looks like the magic number may be different for each machine (ie. there is no one binary to rule them all[all though you could probably trivially generate all possibilities])? And that there is at least some process involved in determining the number what the number is? The author is legit. Here's a somewhat recent talk he gave: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4bM3Gut1hIk",1]]},"HM1Zb3xmvMc":{"wy":"HN shares it as a fun, cheerful introduction to Lisp culture that people say they rewatch.","q":{"x":"Land of Lisp is fun, and the video is a must!","i":31649040,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31649040,"author":"eigenhombre","month":"2022-06","story":"A Road to Common Lisp (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fun Lisp music video.","quote":"Land of Lisp is fun, and the video is a must!","text":"Land of Lisp is fun, and the video is a must![0]; I also really like Norvig's PAIP (Paradigms of Artificial Intelligence Programming, available for free now on GitHub[1]). PAIP shows ways of programming in Lisp that really are unlike what I've seen in most other languages. Lately I have been working the Exercism exercises and there are over 80 Common Lisp problems, with more problems being added over time (my hope is to finish what's there and then help by adding some more). [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":27141134,"author":"eigenhombre","month":"2021-05","story":"The Lisperati1000 is a cyberdeck terminal dedicated to Lisp programming","stance":"rec","why":"Playful Lisp music video.","quote":"Brightens my day every time I watch it.","text":"Barski's music video[1] might give some more flavor to PG's comment. Brightens my day every time I watch it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15078573,"author":"flavio81","month":"2017-08","story":"Answers from 2017 Common Lisp experts","stance":"rec","why":"Fun introduction to Lisp.","quote":"if you want to have lots of fun","text":"> Compared to Symbolics CL looks like a mess at least from reading the books. Which books, may I know? The Common Lisp Hyperspec is one of the best reference manuals for a programming language, ever. But it is not a tutorial nor a how-to guide! Try \"Pratical Common Lisp\". Or \"Land of Lisp\" if you want to have lots of fun. simple but refined, guaranteed to blow your mind... [VIDEO]"},{"id":42672661,"author":"Naru41","month":"2025-01","story":"Why I Chose Common Lisp","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I remember this song well.","text":"More than decade ago, I didn't understand an actual value of Lisp, but I remember this song well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6759652,"author":"whyenot","month":"2013-11","story":"Groovy, the Python of Java","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Land of Lisp [VIDEO] (but I'd argue it wasn't until InterLISP and Smalltalk that all the pieces were there)"},{"id":33891162,"author":"sshine","month":"2022-12","story":"RMS – EmacsConf Talk","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Does JS have a music video like this?","text":"It depends on what you mean by \"learn\". JS is much easier to become bad at quickly, which is not a bad thing. Lisp is an ever-expanding paradigm, but you can learn it at a steady pace like JS. JS is directly transferrable to similar-syntax languages, yes. But Lisp provides insight into techniques that will expand your knowledge and understanding of all programming languages. Does JS have a music video like this? (The Land of Lisp) [VIDEO]"},{"id":30381048,"author":"nineteen999","month":"2022-02","story":"Lisp Alien 3D Model","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Lisp programming community represent the artistic pinnacle of the computing world. Not only do they produce art like this which really gives Pixar a run for their money, but behold the creative majesty of \"The Land of Lisp\": [VIDEO] This REALLY shows how the Lisp community can put the psychedelic giants of the late 60's to shame. Or consider Julia Ecklar's quintessential \"The Eternal Flame\": [LINK] Susanna Hoffs ain't got nothing on this one. It's no surprise that this incredible programming language has taken the world by storm, become an Enterprise programming juggernaut, dominating both the technical and creative landscapes all at the same time."}],"r":[["7gtog_gOaGQ",1]]},"L_XJ_s5IsQc":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend Snarky Puppy as proof that vibrant, progressive modern jazz is being made today.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend anything by Snarky Puppy.","i":18772405,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18772405,"author":"bevinahally","month":"2018-12","story":"It’s a Great Age for Jazz, but Don’t Call It Golden","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Modern jazz that shows something phenomenal is happening.","quote":"I highly recommend anything by Snarky Puppy.","text":"This was addressed perfectly by Robert Glasper at a workshop that I attended. To paraphrase, he was talking about current day jazz school students talking about jazz like it's a work of Mozart or Beethoven, to be reproduced perfectly. \"Hip-hop musicians are more jazz than jazz musicians today\" I wouldn't write off modern jazz though. I truly think that we're experiencing something phenomenal these days. I highly recommend anything by Snarky Puppy. Case in point - [VIDEO]"},{"id":20682062,"author":"drhodes","month":"2019-08","story":"Where did the melody go? (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Good new music exists if you look.","quote":"It's out there if you look.","text":"It kind of sounds like you're having a hard time finding good new music. It's out there if you look. Snarky Puppy - Lingus (We Like It Here) [VIDEO] Hiatus Kaiyote - By Fire [LINK] Alina Engibaryan - We Are [LINK]"},{"id":12327760,"author":"21echoes","month":"2016-08","story":"Jazz in the 21st century: Playing outside the box","stance":"rec","why":"More progressive modern jazz from Snarky Puppy.","quote":"for stuff in the more progressive vein, check out","text":"different senses of innovation /shrug for stuff in the more progressive vein, check out: Jaga Jazzist : [LINK] or [LINK] Jacob Collier - P.Y.T. : [LINK] more Hiatus Kaiyote : [LINK] or more Snarky Puppy : [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NWSZ4c9yqW8",1]]},"PpG0z-npFIY":{"wy":"HN users recommend it as a moving, heartwarming film whenever Istanbul's cats or cat history come up.","q":{"x":"Recommend it for those who haven't seen it","i":25168886,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":16478298,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2018-02","story":"Why Do We Sleep Under Blankets, Even on the Hottest Nights? (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moving documentary on cats and human relationship in Istanbul.","quote":"Made me cry.","text":"Just watched KEDi on Youtube Red [1]. Made me cry. I love my cats so much but they live the best lives when they have the freedom. And I don't know how to deal with them getting sick or dying. With kids, you usually hope you are the first to go. But with pets that have shorter lives, how to cope? There is a lot of wisdom in the movie with regard to the special relationship and insight cats give us to life itself. I have such a deep respect for Turkey now, I even have new respect for Islam. Allahu akbar. May peace be with you my friend. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33607531,"author":"tareqak","month":"2022-11","story":"The history of the domestic cat in Central Europe","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on stray cats of Istanbul and their human neighbors.","quote":"I watched it, and I really liked it.","text":"There is a documentary called \"Kedi\" about the stray cats of Istanbul and their relationship with the human inhabitants of the city. Here is the trailer on YouTube: [VIDEO] . I watched it, and I really liked it."},{"id":25168886,"author":"mehrdadn","month":"2020-11","story":"Why are there so many cats in Istanbul?","stance":"rec","why":"Cute documentary about Istanbul's stray cats.","quote":"Recommend it for those who haven't seen it","text":"They're not feral cats, they're stray cats. I found them pretty cute when I watched the video. (Recommend it for those who haven't seen it: [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["rHIkrotSwcc",1],["0rCUF-V1esM",1],["97t7Xj_iBv0",1],["QyQvfaW54NU",1],["Nnuq9PXbywA",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1]]},"TVgQ_tgWMyU":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a comprehensive talk on depression, and one says it helped them through a bad period.","q":{"x":"this video helped me immensely at one point in my life","i":37490540,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37490540,"author":"Panini_Jones","month":"2023-09","story":"Breaking Through Depression","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video that helped the commenter through depression","quote":"this video helped me immensely at one point in my life","text":"For anyone experiencing depression right now, this video helped me immensely at one point in my life [0]. CBT has also changed my life; I highly recommend the book 'Feeling Good'[1]. [0] - [VIDEO] [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":28377628,"author":"CretinDesAlpes","month":"2021-09","story":"Robert Sapolsky on Depression (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Multidimensional view of depression beyond biology","quote":"suggests a multidimensional viewpoint rather than purely biological.","text":"This 2018 talk from Dr Yapko, \"Keys to unlock depression: Why skills work better than pills\" - [VIDEO] - suggests a multidimensional viewpoint rather than purely biological. He says the causes are: 1. Biological. He says \"Yes, biology matters, but surprisingly not as much as you would think, that when we look at what’s termed genetic variance, how much do genes play a role in depression? Is there a depression gene? No. Are there genes that make people vulnerable to depression? Definitely. So if we look at biochemistry, yes it’s a factor. If we look at disease processes, there are many diseases where depression is a predictable consequence. If we look at drugs, there are many drugs that have depression as a predictable side effect. Biology matters, but when we look at genetic variance, that figure is between .3 and .4. That represents a mild, moderate at best, genetic influence.\" 2. Psychological. In particular what is known as \"Attributional/Explanatory style\" ( [LINK] ), i.e. the person's habitual or reflexive way of attaching meaning to life events. He says \"It isn't what happens to people that increases their risk for depression so much as how they interpret and respond to what happens to them.\" 3. Social At the end, he says \"the goal is not to cure depression, the goal is to learn how to manage your mood. You don't exercise once and now you're done with the exercise thing.\""},{"id":26203101,"author":"antman","month":"2021-02","story":"How Antidepressants Work, at Last?","stance":"rec","why":"Comprehensive talk on cognitive factors and why medication alone falls short","quote":"a nice comprehensive and dense video I found","text":"For the cognitive aspect of depression a nice comprehensive and dense video I found is [0], key factors in slide at 24:10 and the reasons for this approach are at a slide at 23:00 including why a medication only approach is intractable in the long term. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27364679,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2021-06","story":"A possible link between childhood trauma and Alzheimer disease","stance":"reference","why":"Yapko talk on why some people resist depression","quote":"","text":"This reminds me of a talk by Michael Yapko titled \"Keys to unlocking depression\" [1], where he said: \"My first job was working in a psychiatric facility and I was the admissions person. It was my job to admit new patients to the unit. ... \"I would interview people who had been through the worst experiences that you could imagine. People who had been raped, tortured, mutilated, people who had fled their homelands literally with bullets whizzing past their head, people who lost their entire family in one fell swoop in a plane crash or a car crash, people who had suffered the worst adversities you could possibly imagine. \"When I would interview these people it would be easy to appreciate why they were broken, why they were so despairing, why they had given up on life. ... But what fascinated me clinically ... was when I interviewed people who had been through these horrific experiences, people who probably should have been depressed but they weren't, and I wanted to know why not. What is it about the way these people are coping that somehow serves to insulate them against depression?\"* [1] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["sqEyWLGvvdw",1]]},"ZuXzvjBYW8A":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a clear exposition of intergenerational unfairness in housing and wages, especially from a UK angle.","q":{"x":"One of the best expositions of the problem I've seen is this talk","i":27586801,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27586801,"author":"nickcox","month":"2021-06","story":"Ever-increasing home values are a Ponzi scheme","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Boomer peer's exposition of the intergenerational fairness problem.","quote":"One of the best expositions of the problem I've seen is this talk","text":"There is definitely a serious intergenerational fairness issue here. One of the best expositions of the problem I've seen is this talk by Tory peer (and boomer!) Lord David Willetts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32477085,"author":"zbuf","month":"2022-08","story":"Gen Z have 86% less purchasing power compared to baby boomers in their twenties","stance":"rec","why":"UK perspective comparing boomers with other generations.","quote":"You might find this video interesting from a UK perspective","text":"You might find this video interesting from a UK perspective, I suspect with a mix of overlap and difference to the USA: [VIDEO] David Willets draws comparison of \"boomers\" to other generations, and includes what is paid into healthcare vs. what is consumed in a lifetime."},{"id":32162513,"author":"marcyb5st","month":"2022-07","story":"A brief history of nobody wants to work anymore","stance":"rec","why":"UK-focused talk on stagnant labor rewards and housing.","quote":"I think a good explanation of the phenomenon is detailed here","text":"I think a good explanation of the phenomenon is detailed here: [VIDEO] (it's a 50 min talk and targets the UK, but it's applicable to the Western world IMHO). The summary is: - People are working more and reaping less and less from their labor - Social security is getting both worse and less accessible for people in the working age - Working people in general have a much harder time entering the house market and when they do the bang for the buck is way worse than in the past - Retirement plans are getting worse and worse - Millennials and younger people will likely have to drastically reduce their carbon footprint by as much as a factor of 8 compared to the previous generations if we want to hit climate goals for the century Apart for the carbon one, the others are things I am also witnessing both in Switzerland and Italy. And with these premises I don't find surprising that people consider \"working hard\" or even working not worth it."},{"id":40262618,"author":"Voultapher","month":"2024-05","story":"I joined landlord groups to persuade them to be better people to their tenants","stance":"reference","why":"Covers large-scale societal effects of the system.","quote":"This talk for example while focused on a different topic covers some of the large scale effects","text":"You are looking at specific instances. For me it's about the systemic, large scale effects of the system. People routinely partake in exploitative systems without ill intent. The examples you provide could be applied in spirit to child labour. Is every instance of child labour immoral? I don't think so. But using the example of, how you as a 15 year old mowed some lawns to defend systemic child exploitation seems to miss the mark for me. I believe not all instances of landlordism are bad, but as a whole its effects on society are very negative. And that remains true even if all landlords were friendly and full of good intentions. The systemic effects remain the same. This talk for example while focused on a different topic covers some of the large scale effects [VIDEO] ."},{"id":35964997,"author":"TRiG_Ireland","month":"2023-05","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Older people being richer is actually a relatively modern phenomenon.","text":"Older people being richer is actually a relatively modern phenomenon. Ref: \"Have the Boomers Pinched Their Chiren's Futures\", David Willets, Royal Society lecture. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31536445,"author":"trinovantes","month":"2022-05","story":"Why Gen Z workers are already so burned out","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim of declining wealth and rising tax burden.","quote":"It also follows the conclusion from this talk [1].","text":"Income and wealth after Gen X is trending down for same age groups according to your sources. It also follows the conclusion from this talk [1]. Another thing to keep in mind is with decreasing birth rates, future generations will have a higher tax burden to support the aging costs of the previous generations. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["w3_0x6oaDmI",1],["qLjgXPDzeZo",1]]},"pxBQLFLei70":{"wy":"Recommended in life-advice and motivation threads as worth watching in full, especially for its point about starting small by changing yourself.","q":{"x":"Worth the whole watch","i":22105648,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22105648,"author":"wizardforhire","month":"2020-01","story":"Tricks to start working despite not feeling like it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compelling case for making your bed each morning","quote":"Worth the whole watch","text":"Obligatory UT Admiral William H. McRaven Commencement Address[1] Worth the whole watch, however it’s makes he makes a very compelling argument for making your bed in the morning. I’ll add cleaning as part of the morning routine is also highly effective to get moving. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19651032,"author":"jefflombardjr","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: What are good resources for life advice?","stance":"rec","why":"McRaven on changing the world by starting with yourself","quote":"My takeaway: if you want to change the world, start by changing yourself.","text":"First and foremost, what you are describing is metacognition - thinking about the way you think. Hopefully that will aid you in your search for things! Secondly, here are some of my favorite influential material: - Li Ka-Shing, Tips on Life. My takeaway: invest in yourself. [LINK] - Randy Pausch, The Last Lecture. My takeaway: don't limit yourself [LINK] - Guy Kawasaki, Make Meaning. Aimed for startups, but equally applicable in life. My takeaway: Have the proper motivations for doing things. [LINK] - Admiral William H. McRaven, Make your bed. My takeaway: if you want to change the world, start by changing yourself. [VIDEO] - David Foster Wallace, This is Water. My takeaway: Take a big picture view, and don't sweat the small stuff. [LINK] - “Out beyond ideas of wrongdoing and rightdoing there is a field. I'll meet you there. When the soul lies down in that grass, the world is too full to talk about.”― Rumi - Man's Search for meaning. My takeaway: We make meaning. [LINK] - \"All the livin that you're saving, won't buy your dreams for you\" - Townes Van Zandt - Idiot wind by Bob Dylan My takeaway: Your reaction to things can be just as bad as the actual thing. [LINK] - Stoicism and Marcus Aurelius, Seneca. My takeaway: Focus on what matters. [LINK] (read this one first for a good foundation) [LINK] [LINK] - Buddhism. Our takeaway: Meditation is important, the doctrine of emptiness (not nihilism!) is super interesting. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] - Leonardo Da Vinci My take"},{"id":14086267,"author":"musha68k","month":"2017-04","story":"The relationship between social media use and well-being","stance":"rec","why":"Speech friend recommended as relevant to talking across divides","quote":"Just got a recommendation for this great correlating speech by a friend actually","text":"That's why I call it DeplorablesBook but that's also where the problem lies maybe. Most probably. Maybe certainly. We need to engage in those tiresome discussions collectively. We need to talk more, with more people from more diverse backgrounds if we want to change anything actually. Let's educate everyone so that we get an even better internet than the one we came to love back in the 90s and early 00s! Just got a recommendation for this great correlating speech by a friend actually: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47491434,"author":"neilv","month":"2026-03","story":"Attractive students no longer receive better results as classes moved online","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Later, I heard a similar anecdote in a speech","text":"It could be that height says nothing about competence as a CEO, or it could be that the people who attain CEO and succeed despite height bias need to have an exceptionally strong mix of merit/will/effort. I've heard the latter theory at least a couple times about US Navy SEALs. The first time, it was a retired SEAL I knew (well over 6', and a brick wall) who one day out of the blue said something like, \"You shouldn't feel bad about being short. The best SEAL I knew was a short guy, and he could kick my ass.\" Later, I heard a similar anecdote in a speech: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eJ3RzGoQC4s",2],["fpbOEoRrHyU",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1],["OL6-x0modwY",1],["eDNX65d-FBY",1],["ksb3KD6DfSI",1],["j7zzQpvoYcQ",1]]},"rtR63-ecUNo":{"wy":"HN finds it mesmerizing and uses it to build intuition for waveforms and oscilloscope XY drawing.","q":{"x":"This was amazing to me: How To Draw Mushrooms On An Oscilloscope With Sound","i":31432580,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31432580,"author":"pyinstallwoes","month":"2022-05","story":"Korg NTS-2 DIY oscilloscope and wave generator kit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds intuition on waveforms and oscilloscopes","quote":"This was amazing to me: How To Draw Mushrooms On An Oscilloscope With Sound","text":"Anyone have a some tips on wanting to build intuition on wave-forms, electronics, and having fun with an oscilloscope? This was amazing to me: How To Draw Mushrooms On An Oscilloscope With Sound [VIDEO]"},{"id":23129030,"author":"MayeulC","month":"2020-05","story":"The DooM-chip: no CPU, no opcodes, no instruction counter","stance":"rec","why":"Fenderson has published many good oscilloscope videos since 2014","quote":"I like this guy.","text":"I like this guy. I thought this was going to be a more introductory video: [VIDEO] (holy c *, he published a lot of these since 2014, they're pretty good :) ) This has been done with quake: [LINK]"},{"id":15448172,"author":"chrissoundz","month":"2017-10","story":"My Sudden Synaesthesia","stance":"rec","why":"Drawing objects on an oscilloscope via stereo channels","quote":"I find this video quite mesmerizing","text":"I find this video quite mesmerizing: [VIDEO] Essentially it draws an object on an oscilloscope by splitting the audio channel into x y coordinates."},{"id":20302699,"author":"basq","month":"2019-06","story":"How to Understand the Universe When You’re Stuck Inside of It","stance":"reference","why":"Tutorial drawing and animating a mushroom on an oscilloscope","quote":"A tutorial I watched demonstrates the drawing of a mushroom, then animates it by modulating with a larger wave.","text":"What you've written is extremely interesting. Not sure how much of it I truly understand, but I wanted to add that your explanation for everything as a wave function reminded me of oscilloscope music, ie. depicting images with waveforms. A tutorial I watched demonstrates the drawing of a mushroom, then animates it by modulating with a larger wave. In my laymen's head model, Spacetime is like a very large wave that everything else is being modulated by. Here's the relevant video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15362897,"author":"tomatopotato","month":"2017-09","story":"Visualising sound: Laser and mirror and sound","stance":"reference","why":"Drawing images with stereo sound on oscilloscopes","quote":"Reminds me of Jerobeam Fenderson using the XY mode in ossiloscopes to draw images with stereo sounds","text":"Very cool! Reminds me of Jerobeam Fenderson using the XY mode in ossiloscopes to draw images with stereo sounds: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["L0KuAx1COEk",1]]},"1Hqm0dYKUx4":{"wy":"Commenters link it as a quick, clear explanation of why twelve notes only approximate simple ratios.","q":{"x":"minutephysics had a nice video about the mathematics that go behind piano tuning too.","i":19309568,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":27696642,"author":"voting749227383","month":"2021-07","story":"A study of musical scales (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why 12 notes approximate simple ratios.","quote":"I like this video for explanation","text":"I can help with a couple of these. 12 notes is a kludge, because powers of 2^(1/12) well approximate simple ratios. These simple ratios (such as 3:2) sound good. I like this video for explanation: [VIDEO] Also, the reason that the letters are in the wrong order is that we're playing the wrong scale. The monks who invented the scale usually sang in A-minor (so we use the first letter for that scale). Nowadays, we use the major scale, which has the same spacing between notes, but in a different order. It just so happens that C-major has the same notes as A-minor, so that's why we count from C. A great channel for this is Michael New, he explains everything very clearly: [LINK] Music, much like language, has a lot of technical debt."},{"id":19309568,"author":"ramanan","month":"2019-03","story":"Piano Tuning","stance":"rec","why":"The mathematics behind piano tuning.","quote":"minutephysics had a nice video about the mathematics that go behind piano tuning too.","text":"minutephysics had a nice video about the mathematics that go behind piano tuning too. \"Why It's Impossible to Tune a Piano\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":10608802,"author":"ramanan","month":"2015-11","story":"Physics of the Piano (2012) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why a piano cannot be perfectly tuned mathematically.","quote":"A related video on the numbers behind piano music, on why it is mathematically impossible to perfectly tune a piano","text":"A related video on the numbers behind piano music, on why it is mathematically impossible to perfectly tune a piano : [VIDEO]"},{"id":48495817,"author":"Groxx","month":"2026-06","story":"Ear Training Practice Exercises","stance":"reference","why":"Example of how the 12-note western scale falls apart.","quote":"have a MinutePhysics take on how the common 12 note western scale falls apart","text":"To +1 the \"no objective foundation\": browse music theory research a bit! There's a ton of caveats and poor-sounding fits and whatnot for literally every approach, and there's endless discussion of it. E.g. have a MinutePhysics take on how the common 12 note western scale falls apart: [VIDEO] And that's before even getting into completely alternate approaches, or how strongly harmonics affect perception: [LINK] or [LINK] (maybe to clarify: there are objective aspects , in that sound is measurable. but there is nothing like a \"grand unified theory\" that covers all music, nor are roughly any of the popular ones internally consistent - it's far, far too varied for that, and physics often doesn't allow the desired consistency, causing more variety)"}],"r":[["oZAc5t2lkvo",1]]},"9QWaZp_2I1k":{"wy":"Recommended as a good high-level view of Humphrey's book and his argument on how consciousness evolved.","q":{"x":"His recent RI lecture was great and a good high level view of the book.","i":38932717,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38932717,"author":"tizio13","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: Best Audiobooks to Listen to in the Car?","stance":"rec","why":"High level view of the book Sentience.","quote":"His recent RI lecture was great and a good high level view of the book.","text":"If you want fun narration and loosely tech based story, I'd recommend the Magic 2.0 series. Scott Myers created a fun world in that series. I'm really hoping book 7 comes out at some point. The narration is so damn funny. Luke Daniels is a master of making all the characters come alive. The Expanse series is also good if you want more of an epic. Jefferson Mays also does a great job of making the characters feel real. Even if he changes how gimbal is pronounced between certain books. There's also loads of great non fiction stuff out there. One of my personal favorites from this past year was Sentience by Nicholas Humphrey. His recent RI lecture was great and a good high level view of the book. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38410651,"author":"laxd","month":"2023-11","story":"What is it like to be a crab?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues sentient experience evolved only in our lineage recently.","quote":"Another interesting source I have found lately is this talk by Nicholas Humphrey (How did consciousness evolve?).","text":"A thing that always comes to mind with these kind of news is the claim that octopi have comparable effects from MDMA as humans. For anyone that have an overview of evolutionary history that should be interesting to say the least. [LINK] Another interesting source I have found lately is this talk by Nicholas Humphrey (How did consciousness evolve?). If I'm interpreting him right, being a crab is nothing. The experience of \"something\" evolved a couple of hundred million years ago at the earliest, in our lineage. Assuming he is right. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37725187,"author":"FollowingTheDao","month":"2023-10","story":"Is Consciousness Part of the Fabric of the Universe?","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on consciousness evolution, including the blue and chocolatey point.","quote":"For more on 'blue' and 'chocolatey', check out this video...","text":"Thank you. I keep bringing it up here and you are the first one who has perceived my point. For more on 'blue' and 'chocolatey', check out this video... How did consciousness evolve - with Nicholas Humphrey [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["45X4VP8CGtk",1]]},"G1m0aX9Lpts":{"wy":"Offered as an example of live-coded music and a fun entry point to Sonic Pi.","q":{"x":"Sam Aaron is the guy behind the project, he does a lot of ambient type stuff","i":14300925,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":34418284,"author":"wglb","month":"2023-01","story":"Common Lisp and Music Composition","stance":"rec","why":"Fun live coding music performance","quote":"this looks like fun","text":"Don't think this is at a haskel conference, but this looks like fun: [VIDEO] . Their web site is [LINK] . An explanation of sonic [LINK]"},{"id":32127048,"author":"ModernMech","month":"2022-07","story":"New numbers on music consumption","stance":"rec","why":"Example of live coding music in front of an audience","quote":"I hope it really catches on!","text":"Have you heard of live coding? Writing code in front of a live audience that plays music? I doubt anything like that has been done before the last couple of decades. I don't know the history, maybe people dabbled with it way back when, but it's an actual thing now. I hope it really catches on! [VIDEO]"},{"id":14300925,"author":"exabrial","month":"2017-05","story":"Get started making music","stance":"rec","why":"Sam Aaron's ambient live coding with Sonic Pi","quote":"Sam Aaron is the guy behind the project, he does a lot of ambient type stuff","text":"Guys if you haven't seen Sonic PI ( [LINK] ), this is also a great tool! You can write beats using a Ruby DSL and it runs them real time. I sat down and did this in an hour: [LINK] Sam Aaron is the guy behind the project, he does a lot of ambient type stuff: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CbHjcwIoTiY",1],["86x-u-tz0MA",1],["LPjzfGChGlE",1],["xixM_cwSLcQ",1],["JiNKlhspdKg",1]]},"ZSPkcpGmflE":{"wy":"Cited in climate and water threads as an encouraging example of large-scale landscape restoration.","q":{"x":"National Geographic did a really good 8-minute video on it","i":21116947,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":32729094,"author":"aarondf","month":"2022-09","story":"Mangroves on a Landfill","stance":"rec","why":"Encouraging landscape restoration stories","quote":"A few I've enjoyed","text":"If this interests you, I've been watching a lot of permaculture and regenerative agriculture videos on YouTube and there is some really encouraging stuff going on out there! I have no idea how to make the restorative stuff go more mainstream, but it's really impressive to watch small communities or determined individuals entirely change a landscape in a few decades. A few I've enjoyed: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":21116947,"author":"atourgates","month":"2019-09","story":"Scientists Identify How Many Trees to Plant and Where to Stop Climate Crisis","stance":"rec","why":"Short film on restoring water to the Selah ranch","quote":"National Geographic did a really good 8-minute video on it","text":"One of my favorite \"case studies\" related to this is the Selah ranch in Texas. The TL;DR: version is that because it was choked with trees and brush, the groundwater had been removed. The owners restored it to primarily native grassland, and in the process restored natural water holes, and streams. National Geographic did a really good 8-minute video on it: [VIDEO] More info on the ranch: [LINK] Trees are great in some environments, but not appropriate for all environments."},{"id":20629230,"author":"johnohara","month":"2019-08","story":"A Quarter of Humanity Faces Looming Water Crises","stance":"rec","why":"Selah Ranch restoration in Texas and aquifers","quote":"bears watching.","text":"This National Geographic piece about David Bamberger and the Selah Ranch Project in Texas bears watching. [VIDEO] Surface water from rivers, lakes, streams, and reservoirs garner attention because they are visible and make the effects of changes and shortages easier to understand. But a much more difficult crisis may already be upon us in the form of aquifer depletion and may require a complete paradigm shift in how we approach conservation and usage."},{"id":18215706,"author":"wlib","month":"2018-10","story":"Trees Could Change the Climate More Than Scientists Thought","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is extremely obvious and it's surprising how long it took for this to be noticed. If you clear a section of a forest, the area becomes as hot as a desert because of the lack of shade. This is a \"microclimate\". The more area you clear, the larger this hot microclimate becomes. This is the reason why there is desertification all over the globe. Whether you believe it or not, it is just as easy to create a desert from a forest as it is to do the reverse [1], it can even be a passive process [2]. Deserts rain occasionally, and when they do, it becomes a flash flood. If you tame the flash flood [3][5], you can change deserts. There are projects that do this and have proven its effectiveness, while also being able to solve the water crisis in arid lands in Africa [6]. Trees are incredibly important not necessarily for their reduction of carbon dioxide, but because of the microclimate each tree creates. They filter harsh light for the undergrowth, they cool the general area, and they transpire around 99% of the water they get, leading to more clouds and then more precipitation in other areas [4]. The reason why projects like this aren't so popular and aren't subsidized is not because they aren't effective, but because it is hard to make money from actually saving the environment. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"}],"r":[["T39QHprz-x8",1],["ZRV3pBuPxEQ",1],["ZD_3_gsgsnk",1],["akVL7QY0S8A",1]]},"ZUG9qYTJMsI":{"wy":"HN holds it up as a great example of a funny, viral company video that frames the razor market well.","q":{"x":"is absolutely amazing for how low budget it is","i":9762500,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":12128087,"author":"aorth","month":"2016-07","story":"Dollar Shave Club and the Disruption of Everything","stance":"rec","why":"Funny intro video framing the razor market","quote":"which is funny and fantastically frames the reality of the razor market.","text":"The article highlights Dollar Shave Club's 2012 introduction video[0], which is funny and fantastically frames the reality of the razor market. I don't know why I missed this a few years ago... [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9762500,"author":"DiabloD3","month":"2015-06","story":"Dollar Shave Club Is Valued at $615M","stance":"rec","why":"Low budget but effective brand video","quote":"is absolutely amazing for how low budget it is","text":"Honestly, I like their branding. It is unique enough to stand apart, but not so far out there that its unapproachable or unnecessarily too hipster, and manly enough without scaring off the metro-sexual or gay crowd (and please, don't take this as offense, it's just that they gravitate to certain brands that are more \"gentlemenly\" than flat out \"manly\"). Their website portrays their brand extremely well, their famous video ( [VIDEO] ) is absolutely amazing for how low budget it is, and, well, how fail it is and they just keep rolling with it. Its genius. I just wish they'd veer into the double edged safety razor market, which in my opinion, needs a bit of disruption too."},{"id":5600095,"author":"dvt","month":"2013-04","story":"Lawdingo: A Funny Ad For a Serious Business","stance":"rec","why":"Example of a ridiculous, viral-style ad done well","quote":"If you want to see what a great example of the above is, consider","text":"Here's some friendly advice from someone that's done ads for one of my start-ups before. 1) If you can't get top-notch talent, just do the ad yourself. Why? You can tell when amateur actors are just \"reading lines.\" They don't seem excited about the product, all they want to do is get through the script. The had has some brilliant ideas (the best friend cheating on your wife bit is quite funny) but the execution kills it. If you don't think you can pull off \"acting\" by yourself, even describing your business (like you do when trying to get into YC, for example) will go a lot farther than amateur actors reading off of a script. Something like \"Hi, I'm Jim and this is what Lawdingo does. I'm excited to introduce you to this new blah blah\" simply shows excitement (bonus points for being the co-/founder -- viewers get a warmer feeling). 2) If you really want to go the funny route (I don't think I would), do something ridiculous. An 80s eras commercial, a song-and-dance; something that has the possibility of going viral. Law advice is a relatively ubiquitous subject. Consider how GEIKO sells insurance. In your case, I'd say you went half-GEIKO. Go full-GEIKO. Be ridiculous if you want to be funny. The ad makes you seem sorta-kinda serious, so I can't help but get mixed feelings. If you want to see what a great example of the above is, consider: [VIDEO] 3) Diction. Diction. Diction. Have some. Or your actors should have some. I remember we paid this one chick god knows how much to "}],"r":[["-f-CFQxaUY4",1],["5SaFTm2bcac",1],["uDr4L6BzpP8",1]]},"aP0Y1uAA-2Y":{"wy":"Cited in floating-point threads as the main introduction to posits and unums as an alternative to IEEE floats.","q":{"x":"Here's a nice introductory presentation","i":17331781,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17331781,"author":"gbrown_","month":"2018-06","story":"Floating-Point Determinism (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Nice introductory presentation on unums and posits.","quote":"Here's a nice introductory presentation","text":"For anyone interested in this sort of thing you'd probably also be interested in John Gustafson's work on unums/posits. Here's a nice introductory presentation [VIDEO]"},{"id":15360635,"author":"dnautics","month":"2017-09","story":"Floating Point Visually Explained","stance":"rec","why":"Posits faster than IEEE in hardware; commenter appears in video.","quote":"You may like","text":"You may like: [VIDEO] It turns out the math is slightly harder but it's faster than IEEE FP in hardware, probably because of less conditionals in the spec. There is better performance in terms of numerical accuracy at the cost of substantially more difficult error analysis. Disclaimer: I'm in the video."},{"id":13634025,"author":"espeed","month":"2017-02","story":"Why does e to pi i equal -1? (2015) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as relevant talk on next-generation arithmetic.","quote":"","text":"Have you seen \"Stanford Seminar: Beyond Floating Point: Next Generation Computer Arithmetic\"? [VIDEO]"},{"id":13741936,"author":"trsohmers","month":"2017-02","story":"The “high-level CPU” challenge (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Gustafson's talk on posits, practical for hardware.","quote":"Check out the talk","text":"Well unums in particular (especially the new type 3 \"posits\" ones) are very interesting and practical for hardware implementation. John Gustafson, the creator of unums, gave the first talk on type 3 unums at Stanford earlier this month, and there are already multiple hardware implementation efforts (one of which I am indirectly overseeing). While many people have recently hyped up low precision floating point for things like machine learning, Posits provide greater dynamic range AND greater accuracy for very few bits (as low as four, but with good ML starting 8 to 10 bits). On top of all of that, it costs less area and power in silicon than IEEE hardware. Check out the talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13743180,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-02","story":"GCC optimized code gives strange floating point results","stance":"rec","why":"Gustafson presents posits; relevant to floating point surprises.","quote":"I'm very surprised nobody has brought up John Gustafson's Unum work.","text":"I'm very surprised nobody has brought up John Gustafson's Unum work. His latest iteration is the posit, which he presented at Stanford earlier this month[0][1] EDIT: apologies to CalChris[2]. What I should have said is discussed Gustafson's Unum work. [0] [VIDEO] (relevant question at 1h22m37s) [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":28189602,"author":"dnautics","month":"2021-08","story":"Boeing to ground Starliner indefinitely until valve issue solved","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as proof of professional Julia use in 2017.","quote":"","text":"2003, was working in a science lab, lab tech came in and said he (or maybe it was his brother) had just bought a house, really cheap, in hawaii. He said it was in lanikai estates. Family is from hawaii and I'm a bit of a geography geek, so I hopped onto mapquest to see where it was... Noticed it was due east of pu'u o'o, and I know the volcano is moving east, so I told him, look you have 10-15 years to sell that house before it winds up under a volcano. Fast forward 15 years and volcano! The volcano just snuck in under my time window, haha. To be fair, I do not know for sure if HIS house went under; there are houses in the neighborhood that were spared. I didn't bother to ask because when I stalked him it said he was an employee of Theranos, and I decided it was probably not a good idea to poke a hornet's nest. Speaking of Theranos, back when I was in my 2nd postdoc (2013) we were talking about how theranos was a fraud and it was going to come crumbling down, Some other crazy short term things I predicted: Sarah Palin VP candidate, Feb 2008, Simone Giertz becoming famous (ca winter 2015). I also predicted the failure of the first starliner launch (documented here in HN). I also have managed to pick some ascendant \"obscure\" PLs just before hockey sticking. Used Julia professionally in 2015 (proof of use in 2017: [VIDEO] ), used Elixir in 2017, been using Zig since 2019. And I haven't really used any other non-mainstream PLs, so I have a pretty good hit record I think. And a co"},{"id":24087547,"author":"dnautics","month":"2020-08","story":"JuliaCon2020: Julia Is Production Ready","stance":"reference","why":"Talks about research and a Julia demo.","quote":"big julia demo 40 minutes in.","text":"There was no paper, sorry, we talk about some of the research here [VIDEO] big julia demo 40 minutes in. But the video doesn't feature the results I talk about (that happened a few months after the video) the specific results are somewhere in a DARPA presentation (but I don't have those slides, and the data are like 5 laptops back for me). The parallelism was, well, Gustafson parallelization, I pushed a randomly-generated 10k x 10k matrix solve to each of the nodes with a custom soft floating point to measure the relative numerical accuracy. Each node did all of one run per core, it was done very fast , but it would have taken months on a pc. As it was a gustafson scaling at its finest, there was zero cross-node communication during the computation. The containerization I used was singularity. I don't really love them much anymore, for various non-technical reasons, but it got the job done."},{"id":20144002,"author":"dnautics","month":"2019-06","story":"Matlab–Python–Julia Cheatsheet","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of a live floating point demo.","quote":"this was a live demo I did of the floating point stuff at Stanford (demo begins ~53 minutes in)","text":"I should probably post evidence; this was a live demo I did of the floating point stuff at Stanford (demo begins ~53 minutes in) [VIDEO]"},{"id":14763462,"author":"_FKS_","month":"2017-07","story":"High Expectations for Low Precision at Cern","stance":"reference","why":"Gustafson's talk on next generation arithmetic.","quote":"Most likely related","text":"Most likely related: John L. Gustafson presenting \"Beyond Floating Point: Next Generation Computer Arithmetic\" [VIDEO] With papers here: - UNUMS: [LINK] - Posits: [LINK]"},{"id":14013971,"author":"Mindless2112","month":"2017-04","story":"16-bit math look-up tables – the unexpected power of scaled-integer math","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on posits as IEEE-754 alternative.","quote":"","text":"Unums are unlikely to gain much usage. Posits[1][2], also by Gustafson, are a more reasonable alternative to IEEE-754 floating point (but will still have a difficult time displacing IEEE-754, if they can at all). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13770943,"author":"dnautics","month":"2017-03","story":"How many floating-point numbers are in the interval [0,1]?","stance":"reference","why":"Includes a machine learning demo of sigmoid numbers.","quote":"At the end of the video, there's a demonstration where I show some very interesting results concerning machine learning.","text":"so I'm the author of one of the first usable unum implementations. We're actually moving to something called sigmoid numbers which are even better. The lecture doesn't cover \"valid mode\" but that's more like the \"unum\". At the end of the video, there's a demonstration where I show some very interesting results concerning machine learning. [VIDEO] You can try out sigmoid numbers in julia here: [LINK] Also, I am working on some verilog implementations, so we may have this in hardware rather soon. A few things about sigmoid numbers: 1) they have fixed sizes, so you don't have to worry about keeping track of things (which was a pain). 2) they have a property I call \"isomorphic structure\" - which means that zero-padding does not affect the value, and truncation gives you the nearest value at a lower bit resolution. 3) they really do perform better than floating points in several applications. There's a higher bit-per-bit entropy for 'most things you want to do', so the information content is richer. The distribution of the numbers is \"more human\" - that is to say at a certain point you stop caring about the details around the number, and more care that it's 'really big' or 'really small'; and the distribution reflects that."},{"id":13624455,"author":"espeed","month":"2017-02","story":"Intel Gets Serious About Neuromorphic, Cognitive Computing Future","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of moving beyond floating point.","quote":"","text":"Are these the Neuromorphic chips [1] Jeff Hawkins of Numenta [2] has been talking about? [1] Neuromorphic Chips [LINK] [2] Numenta papers/videos [LINK] It would also be good to see a major chip manufacturer or cloud provider that makes its own chips (Google/IBM) get serious about graph processing chips [3,4] and moving beyond floating point [5]. [3] Novel Graph Processor Architecture [LINK] [4] Novel Graph Processor Architecture, Prototype System, and Results [LINK] [5] Stanford Seminar: Beyond Floating Point: Next Generation Computer Arithmetic [VIDEO]"},{"id":13687365,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-02","story":"The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra: A Visual Approach (2015) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Gustafson's closure plots comparing number encodings.","quote":"","text":"> Points near the origin have dark colors, with the color assigned to a complex number z approaching black as z approaches 0. Points far from the origin are light, with the color of z approaching white as |z| approaches infinity. Every complex number has a different color in this picture, so a complex number can be uniquely specified by giving its color. So I was wondering if this approach could also be useful in the context of floats and integers. The implementation becomes simpler in many ways (we only have a finite number of possible values to consider). For example, start with a unique mapping of 8-bit integers to a continuous colour ramp. I suppose the easiest option would be to start in the top left, then go from left to right, top to bottom, resulting in a 16x16 image. If we then take a formula that also returns an 8-bit value, map every possible input to an output, and show the results as an image, it should make it easy to see where we have an integer overflow, for example (if we see sudden jumps from very bright to dark or the other way around). Then I realised: I have seen this in the wild! It's used all over the place in the demo-scene. For example: [LINK] [LINK] Aside, this also reminds me of Gustafson's closure plots for comparing his number encoding to standard floating points, although it's not really the same thing: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["IQZ_bQbxSXk",1],["6chLw2aodYQ",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"jyQwgBAaBag":{"wy":"Frequently linked whenever wind-powered vehicles come up, as the clearest visual explanation of how the craft works.","q":{"x":"The visual that made it click for me was at 18:54 in the original video","i":27701541,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32046080,"author":"TimSchumann","month":"2022-07","story":"DDWFTTW Vehicle Analysis (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of why the physics of the vehicle works","quote":"They go into some nice detail about why the physics works out the way it does.","text":"Derek from the YouTube channel Veritasium made two interesting videos about this vehicle. [VIDEO] [LINK] They go into some nice detail about why the physics works out the way it does."},{"id":28117940,"author":"tzs","month":"2021-08","story":"A wind-powered vehicle that can travel twice as fast as the wind itself","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why zigzagging sailboats can beat the wind downwind","quote":"there is a very good explanation in the original Veritasium video","text":"If you believe that sailboats can beat a drifting balloon downwind by zigzagging, then there is a very good explanation in the original Veritasium video that shows that this is essentially the same thing. Here's a link to the section of the video with that explanation [1]. Watch about 2 or 3 minutes starting there and it becomes clear. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28118403,"author":"theli0nheart","month":"2021-08","story":"A wind-powered vehicle that can travel twice as fast as the wind itself","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of why the vehicle works, like a sailboat","quote":"I thought the video below provided a good explanation for why this works.","text":"I thought the video below provided a good explanation for why this works. The physics are similar to those of a single sailboat going faster than the wind. Imagine two sailboats on a cylindrical ocean on diagonal tacks, on opposite poles, and rotating around the core with rods connecting them to the core. Now you have a propeller. :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":27701541,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2021-07","story":"A Physics Prof Bet Me $10k I'm Wrong","stance":"rec","why":"Visual explanation of how the wind-powered vehicle gains energy from air","quote":"The visual that made it click for me was at 18:54 in the original video","text":"The visual that made it click for me was at 18:54 in the original video[0]. It shows the air hitting the propeller losing kinetic energy which becomes the energy ultimately making the vehicle go. I also don't know how you can watch the treadmill video and doubt it after seeing it go up the treadmill unless you believed it was a complete hoax. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27707989,"author":"kinjba11","month":"2021-07","story":"Science YouTuber Wins $10k Bet with Physicist","stance":"rec","why":"Original video that triggered the downwind-faster-than-wind bet","quote":"Veritasium is a great YouTube channel.","text":"Original video triggering the bet for context: [VIDEO] Video discussing the bet and going over criticism: [LINK] Veritasium is a great YouTube channel."},{"id":37819780,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2023-10","story":"World's fastest sailboat: Two wild designs hit the water for testing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See these videos","text":"I doubt this is how sailboats are doing it (if they're doing it) but one way is (I think) to make the airfoil itself cut across the wind while the \"boat\" goes straight downwind. See these videos: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":34572709,"author":"twobitshifter","month":"2023-01","story":"Solar panels on cars make no sense","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wind powered cars are possible and pretty cool. Turbines could use the apparent wind to generate just the same way it’s used by sailboats to go faster than the wind. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31099495,"author":"soheil","month":"2022-04","story":"The swimming of a dead fish (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this Veritasium video were a wind powered car accelerates into the direction of the wind","text":"Reminds me of this Veritasium video were a wind powered car accelerates into the direction of the wind [VIDEO]"},{"id":28116854,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2021-08","story":"A wind-powered vehicle that can travel twice as fast as the wind itself","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the vehicle starting from wind alone","quote":"you can see it starting just from the wind (first attempt is around 5:20)","text":"In the veratasium video [1] you can see it starting just from the wind (first attempt is around 5:20). It works just fine because as you scale up the vehicle you scale up the propeller, so there's plenty of surface for the wind to push against. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28116868,"author":"shagie","month":"2021-08","story":"A wind-powered vehicle that can travel twice as fast as the wind itself","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The original Veritasium video","text":"The original Veritasium video [VIDEO] The video of the bet and the further explanations - [LINK] -- at 13:28 is an example without aerodynamics."},{"id":27589063,"author":"worldsayshi","month":"2021-06","story":"Towing a Tesla at 70 MPH replenishes battery at fast charger rates","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium episode on sailing faster than the wind","quote":"Veritasium has a very relevant interesting episode on this","text":"You can sail faster than the wind when you push against a medium though. Veritasium has a very relevant interesting episode on this: [VIDEO] Although I agree it would be challenging to get any significant speed from it."}],"r":[["yCsgoLc_fzI",2],["rgaTLrZGlk0",1],["0lJKucu6HJc",1],["ELeZAKCN4tY",1],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["RkTvDjhImwo",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["4d0EGIt1SPc",1],["p45T7U5oi9Q",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HF-9Dy6iB_4",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1]]},"EIezuL_doYw":{"wy":"HN shares it as an entertaining, well-argued critique of carbon offsetting in threads on greenwashing.","q":{"x":"i'm taking the chance to share some content by people that present their points of view on this issue much better than i do","i":34725028,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":32179976,"author":"baxuz","month":"2022-07","story":"Launch HN: Bend (YC S22) – Automatically measure your company's carbon footprint","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues carbon offsets are a scam.","quote":"Carbon offsets are a scam","text":"Carbon footprints are a scam: [LINK] Carbon offsets are a scam: [VIDEO] Hearing people talk about these give me the same vibes as techbros talking about stocks and crypto."},{"id":34725028,"author":"rmvt","month":"2023-02","story":"Vestas unveils solution to end landfill disposal for wind turbine blades","stance":"rec","why":"Presents critical views on carbon offsetting better than commenter.","quote":"i'm taking the chance to share some content by people that present their points of view on this issue much better than i do","text":"> Carbon offsetting is needlessly and cynically attacked by the same people who talk about wind power killing birds or not recycling the blades. well i don't really understand how you can lump all those together but ok... they're still not the same. as others have pointed out, it doesn't seem hard (or hard enough) to scam in this business sector - i.e. having the same assets accounting for different offset purchases, or just conflating assets bought for different puroses with offsetting. furthermore, it just deviates us from what should be the true goal: reduce fossil fuel consumption. not to mention how big business just offload this shady business cost to the end consumer. i'm taking the chance to share some content by people that present their points of view on this issue much better than i do: [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , [VIDEO]"},{"id":37454924,"author":"hju22_-3","month":"2023-09","story":"Sucking carbon dioxide out of the sky is moving from science fiction to reality","stance":"reference","why":"Shows possibility of companies gaming carbon schemes; amusing.","quote":"They're amusing to watch, if nothing else, in my opinion","text":"While I'm not saying this whitepaper is wrong, keep in mind that this is done by Carbon Engineering who receive funding from people and organizations like N. Murray Edwards[1], Chevron Corporation, Occidental Petroleum and BHP[2] which either have hands deep in the oil or mining market, among other things. There is financial incentive to provide results that are in-line with what your funders want, and it's nothing new that this kind of thing has been, and still is being done. It's just hard to prove often. So as with all science, consult multiple whitepapers, including ones that don't have direct or borderline not indirect funding from those who have financial incentive to get a result that increases revenue or similar, to get a clearer idea of the probability that the topic isn't incorrect or what have you. As an aside, whether it's right or wrong (since it's a heated topic), I feel these videos demonstrate at least the possibility of companies playing with these kinds of things. They're amusing to watch, if nothing else, in my opinion: [LINK] and [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":32380247,"author":"jwilk","month":"2022-08","story":"Carbon offsetting is just another form of greenwashing","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on whether offsets can solve climate change.","quote":"See also","text":"See also: [VIDEO] (\"Carbon Offsets! Can't we just buy our way out of climate change?\" from the Climate Town channel)"}],"r":[["AW3gaelBypY",1],["YThelfB2fvg",1]]},"PsXFwy6gG_4":{"wy":"HN recalls it as one of the most interesting debates between two contrasting worldviews on progress.","q":{"x":"One of the most interesting debates was between Eric Schmidt and Peter Thiel back in 2012","i":15983583,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":23266955,"author":"SoylentOrange","month":"2020-05","story":"Show HN: Python package to collect normalized news from almost any website","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favourite debate on whether Google can still innovate.","quote":"One of my favourite debates of this format (though not IQ) is Peter Thiel vs Eric Schmidt","text":"I used to watch some of these IQ debates in college, they can be really good. The longer format and strong moderation really lend themselves to interesting discussions, and you can learn a lot. One of my favourite debates of this format (though not IQ) is Peter Thiel vs Eric Schmidt (then executive chairman of Google) arguing about whether Google was still capable of innovation [2012]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15983583,"author":"jamiesonbecker","month":"2017-12","story":"Eric Schmidt to become technical advisor to Alphabet","stance":"rec","why":"One of the most interesting debates, with Schmidt's positions notable.","quote":"One of the most interesting debates was between Eric Schmidt and Peter Thiel back in 2012","text":"One of the most interesting debates was between Eric Schmidt and Peter Thiel back in 2012, especially in the positions that Eric staked out: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20552577,"author":"wesammikhail","month":"2019-07","story":"Work on production line of Boeing 737 Max ‘not adequately funded’","stance":"reference","why":"Discussion illustrating why companies buy back their own stock.","quote":"this \"discussion\" between Theil and Schmidt in my opinion highlights why companies are buying back their stock","text":"It can really be seen as another form of dividend (or payout) depending on how you look at it. And yes it is fairly common when capital is available especially when no major re-investment opportunities present themselves that would utilize the capital in more efficient ways than mere buybacks. Edit: a bit off topic but this \"discussion\" between Theil and Schmidt in my opinion highlights why companies are buying back their stock [VIDEO]"},{"id":12853180,"author":"internaut","month":"2016-11","story":"Political Showdown: Peter Thiel vs. Google’s Eric Schmidt","stance":"reference","why":"Debate between Thiel and Schmidt on capital P progress.","quote":"Thiel and Schmidt have two very different worldviews, they had a debate on capital 'P' progress a few years ago.","text":"Thiel and Schmidt have two very different worldviews, they had a debate on capital 'P' progress a few years ago. [VIDEO] Many people find Thiel's argument hard to believe, especially if they are aspirational middle class types. This is because normal people have a chronocentric bias. Of all our biases, this one is probably the strongest of all. If you find Thiel's position hard to believe, it is worth examining what our grandparents thought about the future. \"The Home of the Future in 1999\" (from the 1960s) [LINK] I am at the point where I consider the optimistic narrative (ex-computation) utterly without supporting evidence. It is all PR and marketing. Here is one example. Consider the discovery of DNA. Important scientifically. And all the resultant research into genetics since. But tell me what relevance any of it has had to the average person? Have we new drugs? More effective treatments? Cheaper medicine? This is a minor heresy among the 'educated' but to me the answer is clearly no. Think about that for a minute or two. Either I am mad, or in half a century there has been no measurable effects. If the second is true then why do we believe CRISPR is going to revolutionize everything outside? The discovery of DNA itself, the Human Genome Project. The cheaper shotgun sequencing. These should have had enormous material effects on society. It does nothing! Food prices should be much lower. They are static or increasing. Timber should be of higher quality. It is actually much"},{"id":12879584,"author":"slimypickle","month":"2016-11","story":"Dalai Lama: Behind Our Anxiety, the Fear of Being Unneeded","stance":"reference","why":"Debate on whether things are getting better, Schmidt versus Thiel.","quote":"Eric Schmidt takes the side a lot of people here take, which is \"everything is getting better\", and Peter Thiel takes the other side","text":"The comments in here remind me of this video: [VIDEO] Eric Schmidt takes the side a lot of people here take, which is \"everything is getting better\", and Peter Thiel takes the other side, that things aren't getting better. I don't think things are getting better at all. You can easily just use your own metrics and then come to the conclusion that everything is getting better. The problem is, your metrics are bullshit so you are coming to the wrong conclusion. I have seen all kinds of metrics used in here like \"don't have to hunt\", \"live longer\", \"safer\", \"can watch netflix\". I don't think any of these things have to do with whether or not people's lives are getting better. You can just take one of those metrics - \"live longer\", and see that in itself, it can't measure \"getting better\", because you can live for 80 years and have a terrible life, or have 60 years of an excellent life. I think people in silicon valley are just completely clueless and are making the wrong bet, which is why they never see things like Trump coming. Things aren't getting better and people know it."}]},"jhicDUgXyNg":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a great live KEXP session with standout guitar work and good sound.","q":{"x":"He's hands down one of the most talented guitarists alive today.","i":22176622,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22176622,"author":"brodouevencode","month":"2020-01","story":"New era for KEXP: How this radio station is doing top-notch live video (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Live KEXP set featuring a standout guitarist","quote":"He's hands down one of the most talented guitarists alive today.","text":"KEXP was the first time I saw the Delvon Lamarr Organ Trio, with Jimmy James on guitar. He's hands down one of the most talented guitarists alive today. Sample vid: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26840456,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2021-04","story":"A top audio engineer explains NPR’s signature sound (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"One of several live sets recommended for great sound","quote":"Just throwing in some semi-random sets I enjoy KEXP","text":"The KEXP stuff also sounds as good if not better than album recordings most of the time. Quite impressive stuff both of them. Of course there's something magical about live sets that often get lost on studio recordings. Great thing we have sources like NPR Tiny Desk and KEXP so we can discover and enjoy awesome music. Just throwing in some semi-random sets I enjoy KEXP [VIDEO] Delvon Lamarr Organ Trio [LINK] Billy Strings & Don Julin [LINK] amiina [LINK] Mokoomba [LINK] Kikagaku Moyo [LINK] José González NPR [LINK] Andrew Bird [LINK] Trombone Shorty [LINK] DakhaBrakha"},{"id":45404382,"author":"alganet","month":"2025-09","story":"Time travel? Or, just clever technology","stance":"reference","why":"Example of drummer cue and delayed reaction in a jam","quote":"You can see a drummer using the end of a bar to cue the flourish used on the organ in this part","text":"Fair enough, you couldn't have something like the vocalist cueing the bassist and the drummer picking it up out of thing air and doing an improvised roll, like it happens here: [LINK] The drummer takes a little bit more than a second to react to it (higher than a lot of stream delays by the way, but I can see how the stacking could mess it up). That is, however, a bunch of experienced jazz musicians improvising at a high level. In most jams, these conversations happen very often on the next bar (1 and 2 and 3 and 4 and you react on the next 1). You can see a drummer using the end of a bar to cue the flourish used on the organ in this part, for example: [VIDEO] It takes multiple seconds for the organist to recognize the cue, that is actually for the next bar, then he joins in. This sort of stuff is actually doable just with just video chat and OBS. Please also note that the product's example workflow is actually worse in that \"reaction jammyness\" regard than what I proposed: > The performer receives the track early, and waits the rest of the delay period to play it This is designed for recording. It sounds more like a studio arrangement in which you have to record your part than a jam session. > The fidelity of the live stream isn't high enough to record Seems like an incomplete product. OBS can already record multi-tracks and monitor tracks, which you can leverage to produce high quality artifact recordings. I use to sync them manually using a DAW, but with all those auto-tim"}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1]]},"ooOL4T-BAg0":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on rap vocabulary and rhyme generation as a clear visual explanation of Eminem's rhyming technique.","q":{"x":"this absolute standout of a YouTube video about the rhyme scheme in Eminem's Lose Yourself.","i":12893702,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12893702,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2016-11","story":"Show HN: DeepRhyme (D-Prime) – Generating dope rhymes with machine learning","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Breakdown of the rhyme scheme in Eminem's Lose Yourself","quote":"this absolute standout of a YouTube video about the rhyme scheme in Eminem's Lose Yourself.","text":"Linked (but a little buried) in the post is this absolute standout of a YouTube video about the rhyme scheme in Eminem's Lose Yourself. [VIDEO] Really drives home both how much more sophisticated hip hop has become and the incredible degree of difficulty in trying to simulate such sophisticated language structures."},{"id":7695452,"author":"Goopplesoft","month":"2014-05","story":"Rappers, Sorted by Size of Vocabulary","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the effort behind Eminem's rhyming and flow.","quote":"Some of that effort explained in this video","text":"Interesting comment about the L, S, and D usage and rhyming. I was particularly surprised by the effort that goes into Eminem's rap that I just contributed to \"good flow\". Some of that effort explained in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34119540,"author":"zdragnar","month":"2022-12","story":"Death to the Death of Poetry","stance":"reference","why":"Simple review of a song's structure and rhymes","quote":"the second a simple review of the song 's structure","text":"I think it is two parts alternative entertainment, one part childhood education. When you have digital video, easy access to long distance travel, broadcast television and radio music, video games and such, the emotional impact of a clever turn of phrase is utterly lost. Families don't sit around and listen to someone sing or tell stories, we're all of entertaining ourselves in our own ways, letting the rest of the world be creative for us. As for the education bit, kids learn the thinnest veneer of what poetry is- slap some rhymes together for homework and move on. I recall seeing a young poet at the white house a few years back that everyone was falling over themselves to praise, and... I didn't get it, at all. It was nothing more than a political screed masquerading as rhyming prose. Even the rhymes felt either forced or at the least uninspired. On the other hand, there is this: Edit: this is the link I was originally meaning to share, but I'll leave the second after. This first one is more academic, the second a simple review of the song 's structure [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["2AecAXinars",1],["fwCz8Ris79g",1],["U_X5uR7VC4M",1],["ewsGmhAjjjI",1],["HmL2l-bcuUQ",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["o4MwTvtyrUQ",1]]},"-K11rY57K7k":{"wy":"Recommended as a good watch on how Go's M:N:P scheduler works and why it was designed that way compared with alternative schemes.","q":{"x":"This one notably also explains the design considerations for golangs M:N:P in comparison to other schemes","i":47359538,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47359538,"author":"c0balt","month":"2026-03","story":"Understanding the Go Runtime: The Scheduler","stance":"rec","why":"Explains design considerations behind Go's M:N:P scheduler versus other schemes","quote":"This one notably also explains the design considerations for golangs M:N:P in comparison to other schemes","text":"[VIDEO] - Dmitry Vyukov — Go scheduler: Implementing language with lightweight concurrency This one notably also explains the design considerations for golangs M:N:P in comparison to other schemes and which specific challenges it tries to address."},{"id":22450285,"author":"RossBencina","month":"2020-02","story":"A Hitchhiker’s Guide to Asynchronous Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on Go scheduler and lightweight concurrency","quote":"This is a good watch","text":"I guess that's because they are \"intros,\" and often multi-core event loops are implemented by the language runtime or some threadpool library. This is a good watch: Dmitry Vyukov — Go scheduler: Implementing language with lightweight concurrency (Oct 14, 2019) [VIDEO]"},{"id":45720056,"author":"lukaslalinsky","month":"2025-10","story":"How I turned Zig into my favorite language to write network programs in","stance":"reference","why":"Explains performance problems of segmented stacks in Go","quote":"but they have nasty performance side-effects, see","text":"Go depends on the fact that it can track all pointers, and when it needs to resize stacks, it can update them. Previous versions of Go used segmented stacks, which are theoretically possible, if Zig really wanted (would need compiler support), but they have nasty performance side-effects, see [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ZhuHCtR3xq8",1]]},"MmG2ah5Df4g":{"wy":"Offered as an accessible visualization for people wanting to understand particles as excitations of fields.","q":{"x":"I mean there are ways to visualize these things","i":37269944,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37269944,"author":"petermcneeley","month":"2023-08","story":"We are not empty: The concept of the atomic void is a mistake","stance":"rec","why":"Visualization of quantum concepts matching article's goal","quote":"I mean there are ways to visualize these things","text":"I mean there are ways to visualize these things [VIDEO] this aligns with goal of the article \"We build new mental images of the quantum world one step at a time, even under the risk of tripping up here and there.\""},{"id":34103543,"author":"vanillaicesquad","month":"2022-12","story":"Why We Might Be Alone","stance":"rec","why":"Visual explanation of particles as expressions of fields","quote":"Si quieres saber mas mira este video","text":"toda particula es la expresion de un campo de fuerza, no tiene localidad en el sentido de que no existe en el espacio (es una propiedad del espacio) Si quieres saber mas mira este video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48395407,"author":"Npovview","month":"2026-06","story":"\"They're made out of weights\"","stance":"reference","why":"Related to idea that the universe is fields of numbers","quote":"Quantum Field Theory visualized","text":"Quantum Field Theory visualized [VIDEO] Here's a more general idea. Our modern physics says that the whole universe is filled with fields and field is composed of numbers. What if we take that literally? When we say an electron is present here, we actually mean that there are more copies of particular number superposed at that place."}],"r":[["qWJpI2adCcs",1],["nM9f0W2KD5s",1]]},"QEJpZjg8GuA":{"wy":"HN cites it as a strong exposition of algorithmic complacency and of the case for active information seeking.","q":{"x":"Technology connections recently made a really good exposition of that issue","i":43410899,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43410899,"author":"sergioisidoro","month":"2025-03","story":"The Lost Art of Research as Leisure","stance":"rec","why":"A really good exposition on being active in information seeking.","quote":"Technology connections recently made a really good exposition of that issue","text":"I think reading is an essential skill, but we also need to get reading out of the pedestal. Because a lot of the time these arguments come across as some sort of literary elitism. Like if you're not ingesting information through reading, it doesn't count. This month I spent many hours listening to lectures on youtube about geopolitics on the Asian continent from the naval war college. Sure I could have read it, but nevertheless I got curious about a topic, engaged in it, and \"followed the rabbit holes\" I think there is a case to be made that we need to be more active in information seeking, rather than just being fed what the algorithm suggests you - on that topic Technology connections recently made a really good exposition of that issue -- [VIDEO] -- But it should not be about the method of ingesting information, but the quality and the intent of it."},{"id":43188874,"author":"lisper","month":"2025-02","story":"AI's Biggest Flaw? The Blinking Cursor Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Technology Connections makes a better argument for the blinking cursor.","quote":"Alec Watons over at Technology Connections has a much better argument for this than I could ever hope to muster","text":"No, the blinking cursor is a feature, not a bug. Alec Watons over at Technology Connections has a much better argument for this than I could ever hope to muster, so I'll just hand it over to him: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44462308,"author":"eesmith","month":"2025-07","story":"The Rise of Whatever","stance":"reference","why":"Watson on GPS routing optimizing only time, illustrating automation complacency.","quote":"Alec Watson of Technology Connections points out that GPS routing defaults to minimizing time","text":"Rarely. I feel lost when I use GPS to get places. Alec Watson of Technology Connections points out that GPS routing defaults to minimizing time, even when that may not the most logical way to get somewhere. His commentary, which starts at [VIDEO] , is an example of his larger argument about the complacency of letting automation do things for you. His example is a Google Maps routing which saves one minute by going a long way to use a fast expressway (plus $1 toll), rather than more direct but slower state routes and surface streets. It optimizes one variable - time - of the many variables which might be important to you - wear&tear, toll costs, and the delight of knowing more about what's going on in the neighborhood. His makes the point that he is not calling for a return to paper maps, but rather to reject automation complacency, which I'll interpret as letting the GPS figure everything out for you. We've all heard stories of people depending on their GPS too much then ending up stuck on a forest road, or in a lake, or other place which requires rescue - what's the equivalent failure mode with a calculator?"},{"id":43280086,"author":"wwweston","month":"2025-03","story":"Forget Twitter threads and write a blog post instead (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Video essay on appeal of algorithmic feeds.","quote":"This possibility was the theme of the (most?) recent video essay on the YouTube Technology Connections channel","text":"> I think that majority prefer \"algorithms\" to serve them information This possibility was the theme of the (most?) recent video essay on the YouTube Technology Connections channel: [VIDEO] There's definitely something to the appeal of algorithmic feeds, I suspect there's a fundamental neurological mechanism* in play which encourages us to scan somewhere for novel appearances / developments, same as seeing what's in the garden or on the market or who is present at the oasis etc etc. But there's probably more to be said for consciously directing attention. (*There's a certain d-word that probably has a genuine neurochemical meaning when not laden by a lot of popular baggage as it's entered colloquial use that I am intentionally not using because at this point I'd suspect it encourages misunderstanding more than helps, but I'm willing to believe it's related.)"},{"id":43302365,"author":"lolinder","month":"2025-03","story":"Kill your Feeds – Stop letting algorithms dictate what you think","stance":"reference","why":"The article basically summarizes this video.","quote":"this is basically a summary of the most recent Technology Connections video","text":"The author sort of (but not really) acknowledges this midway through, but this is basically a summary of the most recent Technology Connections video, Algorithms are breaking how we think : [VIDEO] I'd rather they acknowledge Alec as the inspiration/source for this post at the beginning and explicitly, rather than just mentioning the video in passing midway through, but at least they do link to it!"},{"id":43194739,"author":"simpixelated","month":"2025-02","story":"Making the Internet the Web Again","stance":"reference","why":"Introduces the term algorithmic complacency.","quote":"used the term \"Algorithmic Complacency\" to encompass how many people would rather be fed content determined by their algo of choice than seek it out.","text":"The YouTube channel Technology Connections recently made a video [1] about this and used the term \"Algorithmic Complacency\" to encompass how many people would rather be fed content determined by their algo of choice than seek it out. But for those who do want a return to the \"Web\", I think there are plenty of existing tools for this. RSS feeds allow you to follow independent blogs and websites. BlueSky and Mastadon can be used to curate a list of like minded people who will suggest other cool people or content to check out. YouTube has a Subscriptions tab that is not influenced by the algorithm. That being said, I applaud anyone who wants to make the independent web more accessible and popular. We need alternatives to the infinite scroll fed to us by massive tech companies. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Q1lL-hXO27Q",1],["5_KcQt0z-eE",1],["dEaecUuEqfc",1],["1MGjFKs9bnU",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1]]},"WNWYk4DdT_E":{"wy":"Cited as an expert's account of why gigacasting works and how Tesla manufacturing has changed.","q":{"x":"Sandy Munro is a leading expert in the auto industry, tears down all the vehicles and consults with all the major manufactures.","i":38448265,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38448265,"author":"verdverm","month":"2023-11","story":"Cybertruck Is Already a Production Nightmare for Tesla","stance":"rec","why":"Shows gigacasting's advantage that old auto resisted","quote":"Sandy Munro is a leading expert in the auto industry, tears down all the vehicles and consults with all the major manufactures.","text":"Yet Tesla has tried different things and now we see the larger auto industry singing a new tune. The most visible example of this is the GigaCast. The Old Auto companies never thought this was a viable path until Tesla showed how superior it actually is. [VIDEO] Sandy Munro is a leading expert in the auto industry, tears down all the vehicles and consults with all the major manufactures. He has many great videos on the trends. One thing he consistently points out is how adverse \"old auto\" is to new ideas or change of any kind, that they don't know how to innovate"},{"id":33052916,"author":"sixQuarks","month":"2022-10","story":"My reaction to the Tesla robot after working on robots for a decade","stance":"rec","why":"Munro on gigacasting, crashes and cracking","quote":"link to video where they talk about cracking the gigacast and why it doesn't matter","text":"So it remains to be see if its a genius move from Musk, or if traditional spot welded designs are in fact the better approach. Fair enough. There is a chance it may not work as they planned, but the fact that they are buying more gigapresses tells me they are confident they will get it to work. Munro and associates did a couple of videos on gigcasting. They did talk about what happens in crashes and did not see it as a major concern. Munro is universally recognized as the leading experts in automotive manufacturing. link to video where they talk about cracking the gigacast and why it doesn't matter: [VIDEO] Watch the full episodes here: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":43796986,"author":"timschmidt","month":"2025-04","story":"Whistleblower details how DOGE may have taken sensitive NLRB data","stance":"reference","why":"Cast frames were suggested to Elon by Sandy Munro","quote":"Cast frames were suggested to Elon by Sandy Munro.","text":"> That's a single example > megacastings Wow, you brought your own counterexample! Cast frames were suggested to Elon by Sandy Munro. [VIDEO] To quote Sandy: \"These guys at Tesla, they take everything you say literally, and then they go basically implement! It's amazing! It is truely to me amazing that these guys can do so much in such a short period of time.\" [LINK]"},{"id":42930302,"author":"saturn8601","month":"2025-02","story":"The young, inexperienced engineers aiding DOGE","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for Tesla's cost cutting and R&D","quote":"","text":"Hold on there, Tesla has repeatedly shown an amazing ability to cut costs of their products while improving functionality/quality. Yes Tesla benefits from credits caused by other companies not meeting co2 targets set by the government but that wouldn't have been enough to save them from three near bankruptcies. And yes, they are still quite a ways away from being a leader in overall quality and consistency but yhey are executing extremly well in their R&D compared to other US car companies only to be outdone by the Chinese, certainly not any US company. [1]: [VIDEO] As someone solidly on the left, this has really frustrated me to no end with the typical lefist sources I watch. In 2025 Facts really matter but all I see are facts being omitted to push a narrative (ie. Elon wasn't the founder of Tesla, Tesla/SpaceX is just alive because of the government subsidizes, Elon does not know anything about how to make a car/rocket). They cite his bad behavior or screwups in reforming Twitter but unless you have followed everything that Elon has accomplished/failed at you are lying by omission. This is especially dangerous now because by dismissing him as dumber than he really is, you are setting him up for surprise successes because people let their guard down. This is what I see on /r/fednews at how shocked they are over how fast he is moving at his slash and burn. You would have known this had you followed the whole Twitter saga very closely, the early days of Tesla where they ousted"},{"id":36496721,"author":"huijzer","month":"2023-06","story":"Volvo Cars Adopts Tesla's NACS Port in the United States, Canada and Mexico","stance":"reference","why":"Explains differences in Tesla manufacturing over the years","quote":"here is a video explaining the differences in the Tesla vehicle manufacturing over the years [2].","text":"Sandy Munro currently believes that Tesla is 5 years ahead. Heck, even the Ford CEO has openly said that it’s very hard to catch up to Tesla in terms of software, batteries, and vehicle design [1]. For example, here is a video explaining the differences in the Tesla vehicle manufacturing over the years [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eGffUODWWSE",1],["hJP5GqnTrNo",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["zVhQOhxb1Mc",1]]},"aIHj3qMRqqE":{"wy":"Recommended as a practical way to get 12V or other voltages from USB-C for hobby projects.","q":{"x":"These little boards are ultra handy","i":35851446,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35851446,"author":"hug","month":"2023-05","story":"EU sends Apple stark warning over USB-C charging on new iPhones","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how to power anything at 12V from USB-C using small boards","quote":"These little boards are ultra handy","text":"These little boards are ultra handy: [VIDEO] Rip the barrel plug off of things and replace it with USB-C input, or make your own USB-C 'dongle' to power anything that needs 12v."},{"id":34255224,"author":"rainbowzootsuit","month":"2023-01","story":"All About USB-C: Resistors and Emarkers","stance":"rec","why":"Explains PD trigger boards with selectable voltage modes","quote":"I found this video just today about USB PD \"trigger boards\" or \"decoy modules","text":"[VIDEO] I found this video just today about USB PD \"trigger boards\" or \"decoy modules\" in the ali express/ebay/amazon searches. There seem to be some you can short a resistor on for a locked setting, or others like in the video that have a mode select."},{"id":24269182,"author":"gabrielblack","month":"2020-08","story":"fpx: easy USB‑C power for all your devices","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the ZY12PDN USB-C PD trigger board the commenter uses","quote":"I'm using ZY12PDN USB-C PD boards","text":"I'm using ZY12PDN USB-C PD boards ( [VIDEO] ) About point 2, IMHO, better a jumper to disable programming mode than having to obstruct the sensor."}],"r":[["Wx_kugSemfY",1],["6BhV-o77RqQ",1]]},"t-O-Qdh7VvQ":{"wy":"Linked as a quick, clear primer when black hole energy and efficiency come up.","q":{"x":"For readers who want some quick info","i":23361634,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28877851,"author":"jerf","month":"2021-10","story":"We've spotted a planet surviving its dying star","stance":"rec","why":"Black hole power using similar principles at higher efficiency","quote":"You may also enjoy: which is a minutephysics on black hole power","text":"Well, that very nearly is the method of power generation. It would presumably be paired with some sort of Dyson sphere around the white dwarf. (Remember that a \"Dyson sphere\" isn't necessarily a solid shell, but just some configuration of matter where no matter which direction the energy goes it is intercepted by something.) You may also enjoy: [VIDEO] which is a minutephysics on black hole power using much the same principles but attaining yet higher efficiencies."},{"id":23361634,"author":"willis936","month":"2020-05","story":"Black hole paradoxes reveal a fundamental link between energy and order","stance":"rec","why":"Quick info on Kugelblitzes and getting energy from black holes","quote":"For readers who want some quick info","text":"I don’t see why it’s unbelievable. High mass energy conversion is one of the least crazy things about black holes imo. For readers who want some quick info: [VIDEO] This PBS SpaceTime video doesn’t talk about the practical uses of Kugelblitzes, but it isn’t a big leap from understanding what they are to how to get energy out of them. Hawking radiation rate increases as schwarzschild radius decreases. If you could make a properly sized black hole you could convert a little bit of mass into a lot of energy. Maybe this could be harnessed to solve all electricity demand issues. Maybe it could be used to make a room sized bomb that blows up Earth. Vote now on your phones. [LINK]"},{"id":44565488,"author":"pantalaimon","month":"2025-07","story":"LIGO detects most massive black hole merger to date","stance":"reference","why":"Black holes convert up to 42% of mass into energy","quote":"They actually convert up to 42% of their mass into energy, mostly radiation","text":"> minus a few percent to gravitational waves They actually convert up to 42% of their mass into energy, mostly radiation [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"2z5A-COlDPk":{"wy":"Referenced as a typical example of the channel's hacker-eye view of everyday technology, and for its flasher explanation.","q":{"x":"The TC channel is definitely a hacker's eye view in to all the technologies that we take for granted every day.","i":32712891,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32712891,"author":"bcjordan","month":"2022-09","story":"Interest in heat pumps has increased dramatically in recent years","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why turn signals don't sync at stoplights","quote":"The TC channel is definitely a hacker's eye view in to all the technologies that we take for granted every day.","text":"Came here to post this :D The TC channel is definitely a hacker's eye view in to all the technologies that we take for granted every day. Tons of fascinating tech history covered, too. Other fun ones: - a defense of the simple drip coffee maker [LINK] - why do the turn signals not sync up when you're at a stop light? [VIDEO] - how humidifiers work and ehy the simple swamp cooler style are pretty ideal [LINK] - why dishwasher detergent packs are stupid (use powder/liquid) and why you should use the pre-wash slot and not bother pre rinsing your plates [LINK] - why do light switches click? [LINK]"},{"id":40828453,"author":"Dwedit","month":"2024-06","story":"Apple II graphics: More than you wanted to know","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates thermal and capacitor-based flashers","quote":"There's a video by Technology Connections all about turn signals","text":"There's a video by Technology Connections all about turn signals: [VIDEO] Demonstrates thermal-based flashers as well as capacitor-based flashers. They are clicking because metal is moving and hitting metal."},{"id":29674635,"author":"beeboop","month":"2021-12","story":"Toyota 'reviewing' key fob remote start subscription plan after blowback","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Headlights are way too bright and a big reason is people putting poorly made and poorly aimed aftermarket headlights on.","text":"please please don't: [VIDEO] I refuse to drive at night anymore. Headlights are way too bright and a big reason is people putting poorly made and poorly aimed aftermarket headlights on."}],"r":[["Sp9H0MO-qS8",1],["oHeehYYgl28",1]]},"8Au47gnXs0w":{"wy":"HN admires the maker's engineering and cites it in accessibility discussions, especially for the limits of sensory resolution versus perception.","q":{"x":"'Stuff Made Here' made a 12-point display using two motors.","i":34244964,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":23859093,"author":"sambroner","month":"2020-07","story":"I made a robot to cut my hair with scissors [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"iPad lidar to braille converter that was really impressive.","quote":"This guy is a legend. Awesome videos.","text":"This guy is a legend. Awesome videos. A few of them seem productizable in a way most DIY-er videos don't, albeit in a limited run capacity. He made a iPad lidar to \"braille\" converter that was really impressive, and I'm hopeful someone picks up the work. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34244964,"author":"ryankrage77","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Alternatives to Scratch for a blind child?","stance":"reference","why":"12-point display driven by two motors.","quote":"'Stuff Made Here' made a 12-point display using two motors.","text":"Would it be possible to use fewer motors to control more pins? 'Stuff Made Here' made a 12-point display using two motors. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30959953,"author":"open-paren","month":"2022-04","story":"A naked skydive inspired a way to keep pilots oriented in flight","stance":"reference","why":"Device hit limits of sensory resolution versus ability to perceive it.","quote":"The problem he ran into was sensory resolution vs. ability to perceive that resolution.","text":"There was a Youtuber that made a device like this with the lidar from an iPad and a 3x3 grid of dots to detect obstacles. The problem he ran into was sensory resolution vs. ability to perceive that resolution. (Unsurpisingly) your eyes are unparalleled for picking up visual resolution. [VIDEO]"}]},"E8V8rtdXnLA":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for epistemic humility and how wrong even the smartest people have been.","q":{"x":"Here is a terrific video that sums up this point very well -","i":6331264,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6331264,"author":"WayneDB","month":"2013-09","story":"Didn't Get Enough Sleep? You Might As Well Be Drunk","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows even the smartest people have been dead wrong.","quote":"Here is a terrific video that sums up this point very well -","text":"The first time he was tested, he dropped a small amount of weight and the second time he didn't. Neither test was only done by one scientist and the second test was a much larger fanfare with Indian defense/government researchers present. Bathing was also observed as was gargling (what he spit out was also measured). Even so - these \"red flags\" come from the same scientists that you don't trust. So, they should not support your cynicism in any way. What are we left with? - Not published in a journal. - Incomplete CCTV coverage. Also, it should be stated that according to many cynics, the CCTV coverage is incomplete because Jani moved out of view for moments at a time. He also received visitors and was allowed to take sun baths. Anyway, I'm not really here to debate this case. I am just being an anti-cynic because it's been shown time and again that even the smartest people in the world have been dead wrong about what they think they know. Here is a terrific video that sums up this point very well - [VIDEO]"},{"id":49171979,"author":"anonymousiam","month":"2026-08","story":"Nobel Disease","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on how we can know when we're right.","quote":"How do we know for sure when we're right about something?","text":"You've touched on the whole problem with the \"cancel culture\" of today. Scientific and political beliefs often do not stand the test of time, but is it fair to blame the people of past eras for their misjudgements using the values of today? How do we know for sure when we're right about something? [VIDEO]"},{"id":36140607,"author":"anonymousiam","month":"2023-05","story":"Scientists warned about climate change in 1965. Nothing was done","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue against scientific consensus.","quote":"I am reminded of this presentation","text":"\">There was no scientific consensus in the 1970s that the Earth was headed into an imminent ice age. Indeed, the possibility of anthropogenic warming dominated the peer-reviewed literature even then...When the myth of the 1970s global cooling scare arises in contemporary discussion over climate change, it is most often in the form of citations not to the scientific literature, but to news media coverage.\" Please see my above reply citing over two dozen contemporaneous articles from reputable sources and scientists about global cooling ~50 years ago. Can you cite even a few articles or papers from the same era on the topic of global warming? So much for the consensus argument, but why are you using it anyway? Science has nothing to do with consensus. The Spencer Weart book you cite appears to be a \"history of scientific discoveries that led to the current scientific opinion on climate change.\" I will give it a read, but I am reminded of this presentation: (You Have No Idea How Wrong You Are) [VIDEO]"},{"id":15091366,"author":"hungerstrike","month":"2017-08","story":"Why people believe in conspiracy theories – and how to change their minds","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue humans have little idea how wrong they are.","quote":"humans generally have no idea how wrong they are -","text":"> No, and no one here is suggesting that, and neither is the article suggesting it. Yes, the article certainly does suggest that we need to \"change [the] minds\" of anyone who \"believes\" in conspiracy theories. (And if I'm not outright denying the possibility of a conspiracy the author considers me a \"believer\".) It's right there in the title and in the story of the moon landing. If you read the article, the author links to a study that allegedly proves that people who are open to conspiracy theories aren't being analytical enough and apparently that's somehow a problem for everybody and it needs to be stopped. It's as if the author thinks that they are able to accurately analyze every situation even though they weren't there (on the moon, etc) and that they have accounted for every possibility. He assumes that he has all the information. Clearly this is not the case. People who deal in hard sciences often behave like this. Perhaps the author should try analyzing their own propensity to be incorrect despite applying every analytical power that they possess because if one thing is certainly true, it's that humans generally have no idea how wrong they are - [VIDEO]"},{"id":5347475,"author":"WayneDB","month":"2013-03","story":"Discovery of a microscopic world shook the foundations of theology","stance":"reference","why":"Scientists on the unknown and how wrong we are.","quote":"Here are some more scientists, talking about the unknown","text":"Am I the only person who doesn't think that science and religion are really at odds, except for in the minds of fools and the mouths of manipulators? Every major religion has the idea of \"oneness\" wherein the God/Godhead is portrayed as the ultimate underlying energy (or doer) within every existing thing. Where is the conflict? Atheists like to say \"Why invent this idea of God without evidence?\", but they can't name who \"invented\" religion or prove that it was even invented. We were told about it. Also, every existing thing clearly does exist. So there's your evidence. How helpful is that? Here are some more scientists, talking about the unknown: You Have No Idea How Wrong You Are - [VIDEO] What We Still Don't Know: \"Are We Real?\" - [LINK]"}]},"dlbMuv-jix8":{"wy":"HN commenters hold it up as an unmatched introduction to programming and quote Sussman's line that knowing a spirit's name gives you power over it.","q":{"x":"I don't think it's possible to do any better than this","i":13769779,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13769779,"author":"gingerbread-man","month":"2017-03","story":"Ask HN: What would you teach adults new to programming in a 2 hour class?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best introduction to programming; makes everything clearer and simpler.","quote":"I don't think it's possible to do any better than this","text":"I don't think it's possible to do any better than this: [VIDEO] Admittedly, when I found my way to Sussman's course, I had already been programming for awhile, so the \"magic\" was less of Wow, I can write a computer program that does neat stuff! and more Wow, this makes everything I've been doing so much more clear and simple!"},{"id":13825363,"author":"clusmore","month":"2017-03","story":"Thinking About Recursion","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman covers the Tower of Hanoi problem.","quote":"Gerald Sussman covers the Tower of Hanoi problem in the SICP lectures","text":"Gerald Sussman covers the Tower of Hanoi problem in the SICP lectures [1]. The thing I find fascinating about this solution is that it doesn't even require the rule that you can't place larger discs on smaller discs - it simply is never optimal to do so anyway. And the solution is so simple, I wonder if you could invent a game by first coming up with a solution and then seeing what it actually solves. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13890623,"author":"cgag","month":"2017-03","story":"Acing the technical interview","stance":"reference","why":"Origin of the idea that knowing true names gives power.","quote":"I think the first time I learned the value of knowing the true names of things was here","text":"\"“To know a thing is to name it,” you advise. True names have power. The K language was invented by Ursula K. Le Guin, and is among the oldest and tersest forms of magic.\" Beautiful. I think the first time I learned the value of knowing the true names of things was here: [VIDEO] before I studied it in depth reading Rothfuss."},{"id":13611530,"author":"espeed","month":"2017-02","story":"HyperNormalisation (2016) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman's line about the power of naming things.","quote":"As any sorcerer will tell you, if you know the name of a spirit, you have power over it","text":"\"As any sorcerer will tell you, if you know the name of a spirit, you have power over it\" -- Gerry Sussman, SICP * [VIDEO] Many/most of the general population seem to think \"propaganda\" only happens to other people, somewhere else, under a dictatorship, in Russia or North Korea for example. In contrast, many/most think the TV/media outlets they watch/consume (and most closely identify with) are the real news -- the true/fair/balanced/accurate news -- while the \"other news\" channels are \"extreme\" (MSNBC vs FOX NEWS) or \"fake\" (now ascribed to CNN). It's supposed to be illegal for the USG to use propaganda against its own citizens. Curiously the \"Lobbying Restriction\" in the US tax code for IRS 501(c)(3) describes lobbying as \"carrying on propaganda or otherwise attempting to influence legislation\": [LINK] . Call it propaganda, lies, manipulation, population control, fake news, the matrix, whatever -- I think the significance here is the general population now has a name for it. It resonates. It's gone viral. And now that it's entered the general lexicon and has become a real, legitimate topic of conversation, people will begin to see what's been hiding in plain sight. As the idea propagates through the system and gains strength, the veil will lose power as the curtain fades away. The question to ask is: What happens next?"},{"id":9272395,"author":"cgag","month":"2015-03","story":"Why Racket? Why Lisp? (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman on why naming abstractions gives power.","quote":"if you know the name of a spirit, you have power over it","text":"I don't know about \"possible to express\", but having learned Haskell, I see monoids everywhere. In the SICP lectures, when Sussman teaches the class the word \"predicate\", he mentions that knowing the names of these things is important because \"As any sorcerer will tell you, if you know the name of a spirit, you have power over it\".[1] If you only know C, you're unlikely to think in terms of map/filter/reduce. If you don't know Haskell, you're unlikely to think in terms of functors and monads. You may be vaguely aware of repeated patterns, but knowing you're looking at a monad gives you a lot more power to reason about it. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"rqOUgqsWvbw":{"wy":"Cited in sync-engine threads as the origin story for Zero and for hybrid local and remote queries.","q":{"x":"wholeheartedly recommend this wonderful talk about the thoughts that led to this","i":42453453,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42453453,"author":"trosterud","month":"2024-12","story":"Day Zero – Build Good Web Apps with the Zero Sync Engine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thinking that led to the Zero sync engine","quote":"wholeheartedly recommend this wonderful talk about the thoughts that led to this","text":"Nice! Congratz It's insanely fast. And wholeheartedly recommend this wonderful talk about the thoughts that led to this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43433696,"author":"aboodman","month":"2025-03","story":"Sync Engines Are the Future","stance":"reference","why":"Why general-purpose sync engines were hard: read side and permissions","quote":"For more on this, I talk about it some here","text":"Zero (zerosync.dev) uses transactional conflict resolution, which is what our prior product Replicache and Reflect both used. It is very similar to what multiplayer games have done for decades. It is described here: [LINK] It works really well and we and our customers have found it to be quite general. It allows you to run an arbitrary transaction on the sever side to decide what to do in case of conflicts. It is the software equivalent of git asking the user what to do. Zero asks your code what to do. But it asks it in the form of the question \"please run the function named x with these inputs on the current backend db state\". Which is a much more ergonomic way to ask it than \"please do a 3-way merge between these three states\". Conflict resolution is not the reason why there has not been a general-purpose sync engine. None of our customers have ~ever complained about conflict resolution. The reason there has not been a general-purpose sync engine is actually on the read side: - Previous sync engines really want you to sync all data. This is impractical for most apps. - Previous sync engines do not have practical approaches to permissions. These problems are being solved in next generation of sync engines. For more on this, I talk about it some here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43439005,"author":"ochiba","month":"2025-03","story":"Sync Engines Are the Future","stance":"reference","why":"Hybrid local and remote queries central to Zero","quote":"This is one of the ideas that appears to be central to the genesis of Zero [1]","text":"I think that is where sync engines come in that allow doing arbitrary hybrid queries (across local and remote data) and then keeping the results of those hybrid queries in sync on the client. This is one of the ideas that appears to be central to the genesis of Zero [1] ElectricSQL allows for a similar pattern and PowerSync is also working on this [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":41747045,"author":"aboodman","month":"2024-10","story":"Show HN: One – A new React framework unifying web, native and local-first","stance":"reference","why":"Zero announced at Local-First Conf","quote":"I was even invited to speak at local-first conf, where we announced Zero last May.","text":"Hi Joseph, There is a lot of debate about this in the community. People like pvh, adam wiggins, and johannes have all said they consider Replicache and Zero part of the local-first movement [1] and that they would be sad if we and other things like us weren't in it. I was even invited to speak at local-first conf, where we announced Zero last May. [2] I think in practice delivering on the local-first ideals is ultimately up to the app developer not the library. Like in practice every yjs app talks to its server over some bespoke protocol and is loaded over a URL. If that server goes away your yjs app is a brick. No less a brick than if your zero server goes away. Similarly although everyone likes to claim Linear as a local-first app, if the Linear server goes away your Linear app is a brick. Yeah you can export the data from IDB or whatever but you could do the same for Zero or any of these systems. That all said, for our part, we have recently tried to avoid claiming Zero and Replicache as local-first on our own sites to avoid this controversy [3]. I think we're building something really valuable that a lot of people are excited about. And I think we're making the right tradeoffs for the majority of software that most people use every day. So I don't really care too much if it qualifies as local-first (except insofar as I like hanging out with the local-first folks and would be sad to not get invited to the parties). I forgot to mention this to Nate when he showed me these m"}]},"F0_q-fD_lyU":{"wy":"HN points to it as proof that DIY genetic engineering is feasible and as an example of a rigorous hands-on biology channel.","q":{"x":"he makes videos about DIY genetic engineering and I have a huge amount of respect for his rigor and detail","i":34780662,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":47845799,"author":"samplatt","month":"2026-04","story":"Air Is Full of DNA","stance":"rec","why":"Hands-on DIY biology channel","quote":"The Thought Emporium is a personal favourite.","text":"Not OP, but The Thought Emporium is a personal favourite. Their name belies the hands-on nature of their videos. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40786924,"author":"Bjartr","month":"2024-06","story":"Why Nothing Can Grow on Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows home genetic engineering is feasible","quote":"You can do genetic engineering in your kitchen these days.","text":"You can do genetic engineering in your kitchen these days. It wouldn't be easy to build a mars-worthy microbe, but it's not other people that's going to stop you. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34780662,"author":"Dig1t","month":"2023-02","story":"The Odin – DIY genetic engineering","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for criticism of a lab supplier","quote":"he makes videos about DIY genetic engineering and I have a huge amount of respect for his rigor and detail","text":"The Thought Emporium is one of my favorite YouTubers, he makes videos about DIY genetic engineering and I have a huge amount of respect for his rigor and detail. In one of his videos he talks about sourcing lab supplies and he listed The Odin as the one place you should \"avoid at all costs\". \"Not a single kit they sent me worked\", was pretty damning. He lists all the reasons he doesn't like using them including the fact that they have sent him totally wrong supplies, and overcharge for most of their stuff. Source: [VIDEO] He lists better alternatives that are both cheaper and more reliable."}]},"LEENEFaVUzU":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for the scale of future human population and long-term thinking.","q":{"x":"Kurzgesagt has some interesting videos on human population","i":34223505,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34223505,"author":"mLuby","month":"2023-01","story":"Should we care about people who need never exist?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting videos on human population","quote":"Kurzgesagt has some interesting videos on human population","text":"It's survivorship bias—where bias means skewed from the full distribution of choices people and societies made, not bias as in discrimination. Most of the families and societies that didn't prioritize population growth (by reproduction or immigration) aren't around to advocate for that approach's merits, whereas those that did are. Fun fact: for each human alive today there've been 15 deceased humans ever. As zombie apocalypses go, 15:1 doesn't sound too bad. Kurzgesagt has some interesting videos on human population if that's your style: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38754661,"author":"Wurdan","month":"2023-12","story":"Should we tell people it's too late to save civilization?","stance":"reference","why":"A line about each generation expecting the apocalypse","quote":"","text":"A line in a Kurzgesagt video ( [VIDEO] ) a while back caught my attention. “Every generation assumes they’re important enough to witness the apocalypse.” I don’t think that’s the only factor in people wishing for collapse, but I think it’s one of them."},{"id":31940794,"author":"lumenwrites","month":"2022-07","story":"Project Ideas","stance":"reference","why":"Argues future humans vastly outnumber everyone alive now","quote":"If humanity plays its cards right, there will be trillions and trillions of humans living in the future, all over the galaxy.","text":"Take a look at the recent Kurzgesagt video: [VIDEO] If humanity plays its cards right, there will be trillions and trillions of humans living in the future, all over the galaxy. Everyone who lives right now, or has ever lived, is barely noticeable compared to that. Take a look at the recent Less Wrong post: [LINK] I don't see any flaws in this argument, we all are on a fast-track to getting killed by AI, and the smartest people on the planet don't have a plan on how to fix it. \"Build an AI that burns all GPUs to prevent all the future AIs, and hopefully doesn't kill us all in the process\" is the best idea they've got. If you believe that we're about to kill trillions and trillions of potential future human beings, these priorities make sense."},{"id":32104264,"author":"randomhodler84","month":"2022-07","story":"EU legislation eIDAS article 45.2 may force inclusion of insecure QWAC root CAs","stance":"reference","why":"Prompted reflection that we are among the first few billion humans","quote":"we will be the source of fascination for many generations to come","text":"After seeing The last human - Kurzgesagt [VIDEO] and realizing that for all Of human history we have though it was the end - and actually it’s more likely that as the first few billion humans we will be the source of fascination for many generations to come. Our digital record will be preserved and relentlessly studied. Maybe our thread here will be a footnote in a hologram in a few millennia."}],"r":[["QsBT5EQt348",1],["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1]]},"MhQ70O1MiXc":{"wy":"HN points to it as an example of Notch's famous live-coding sessions, which drew many early Minecraft fans.","q":{"x":"Watching `notch` live-code is also very popular.","i":3611235,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3611235,"author":"thebigshane","month":"2012-02","story":"Hackathons are nonsense","stance":"rec","why":"Notch live-coding Minicraft","quote":"Watching `notch` live-code is also very popular.","text":"Watching `notch` live-code is also very popular. [1]: [LINK] Making Metagun [2]: [VIDEO] Making MiniCraft [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":30719014,"author":"deaddodo","month":"2022-03","story":"Hot-code reloading on macOS/arm64 with Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Notch testing logic changes in his Java environment","quote":"this is exactly how he used his Java environment","text":"If you watch some of Notch’s old game jam live streams; this is exactly how he used his Java environment and is probably why he was so invested in it. You can see some examples here when he just tests logic changes: [VIDEO] (Though, he restarts often since he’s editing preloaded assets also)"},{"id":21502685,"author":"PeterStuer","month":"2019-11","story":"Nostalgia for Minecraft","stance":"reference","why":"Notch's live coding drew early Minecraft buyers","quote":"started with geek dads watching Notch live coding and buying the game for their kids","text":"As I recall Minecraft's snowball started with geek dads watching Notch live coding and buying the game for their kids. [VIDEO]"}]},"Pp9Yax8UNoM":{"wy":"Cited as a striking, counterintuitive example of extreme thermal insulation and hard materials science.","q":{"x":"have a look at this video demonstrating insulating tiles as used on the space shuttle","i":33965154,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33965154,"author":"waste_monk","month":"2022-12","story":"US Air Force launches first operational hypersonic missile","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates space shuttle insulating tiles and modern materials science.","quote":"have a look at this video demonstrating insulating tiles as used on the space shuttle","text":"I would imagine they have an easier time of it since they don't have to worry about reusing the missile :) [1] More seriously, have a look at this video demonstrating insulating tiles as used on the space shuttle (which is IIRC some kind of aerogel): [VIDEO] . Modern materials science is astounding, and while probably not aerogel (as I understand it is quite fragile), I'm sure whatever system the missile uses is more than sufficient for it's expected operational life post-launch. [1] similar to this classic story about memory leaks [LINK]"},{"id":35209175,"author":"DubiousPusher","month":"2023-03","story":"Tungsten Gold Plated Bar","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as another example of experience-defying physical behavior.","quote":"This video gives me a similar vibe.","text":"I think it's so interesting for people because so much of our concept of reality and how the world works is down to our experience. Then you encounter something contrary to that experience and it kind of forces you to realize, evem for just a minute that your understanding is largely a connecting of the dots of your own limited sampling of a much larger thing. This video gives me a similar vibe. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11268251,"author":"mozumder","month":"2016-03","story":"Hard Tech is Back","stance":"reference","why":"Example of science-driven hard tech: shuttle tile held at 1500 degrees.","quote":"the Space Shuttle material that you can hand hold at 1500 degrees","text":"To me, \"Hard Tech\" is more science oriented, like the Space Shuttle material that you can hand hold at 1500 degrees: [VIDEO] Stuff that's non-obvious but amazing and makes you think sci-fi... DARPA does a lot of this kind of stuff. Definitely non-trivial problems, most likely hardware, maybe materials science or something in the other science fields. Maybe new math, or solvers that take days to run on thousands of CPUs. Stuff that might take thousands of man-hours to develop. Not sure how YC would be involved here, as this is expensive to develop, but DARPA has a funding model."},{"id":7389140,"author":"bsamuels","month":"2014-03","story":"How a Light Saber Works","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an extremely heat-resistant material for the argument.","quote":"example of extremely high thermal resistance material","text":"Not necessarily, maybe the thermal properties of the lightsaber are modified to give them high thermal resistance that is lowered contextually (like when youre slashing up some guy) example of extremely high thermal resistance material: [VIDEO]"}]},"ToKcmnrE5oY":{"wy":"Cited constantly as a quip when a person or organization seems to be doing something morally dubious.","q":{"x":"this sketch from Mitchell and Webb stayed with me for years.","i":44631116,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44631116,"author":"kjellsbells","month":"2025-07","story":"Peep Show is the most realistic portrayal of evil I have seen (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Comedy sketch about villains who don't see themselves as bad","quote":"this sketch from Mitchell and Webb stayed with me for years.","text":"On the topic of evil, this sketch from Mitchell and Webb stayed with me for years. Are we the baddies? [VIDEO] Every villain is the hero in their own story, after all."},{"id":48136215,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2026-05","story":"Meta's New Reality: Record High Profits. Record Low Morale","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"did I wonder \"Are we the baddies?","text":"I guess it's a sign of the times. I was just reading the old speech by John Barlow, in another post[0]. Sort of dovetails with this. I spent the majority of my career at a camera manufacturer. I probably made half of what I could have made, anywhere else, and there were lots of issues, caused by bureaucratic overhead, heavy-handed QA, and cultural misunderstandings. But not once, during almost 27 years, did I wonder \"Are we the baddies?\"[1]. My first job was at a defense contractor, where we manufactured surveillance gear, and sold it to militaries and spy agencies around the world. One of the reasons that I left that job, was because we definitely were the baddies. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47880657,"author":"thih9","month":"2026-04","story":"Palantir Employees Are Starting to Wonder If They're the Bad Guys","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The title is likely a reference to a sketch","text":"The title is likely a reference to a sketch: [LINK] [VIDEO] Although it could be unintentional - the phrase is mainstream now and not hard to produce independently either."},{"id":45470250,"author":"bjt","month":"2025-10","story":"Asked to do something illegal at work? Here's what these software engineers did","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"that great \"are we the baddies?\" sketch from Mitchell and Webb.","text":"> You might want to think about why Petersen wants you to think you’re the Nazi. What change is he trying to effect in our culture, and how does that belief support his desire? Rhetoric always aims to effect some change in the attitude of the listener, and never without some benefit of the speaker. What benefit do you think he's trying to get from it? I'm honestly trying to figure out the nefarious angle and coming up blank. It seems to me like a very similar sentiment to that great \"are we the baddies?\" sketch from Mitchell and Webb. [1] I see both as an exercise in moral humility. See the Milgram experiment, or the Asch experiment. Most people do cave to pressure from authorities and the group. Everybody believes they're they exception. Statistically, most of them are wrong. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37358852,"author":"cf141q5325","month":"2023-09","story":"ISPs Should Not Police Online Speech – No Matter How Awful It Is","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">I think we disagree on the totalitarian aspect of the echo chamber. That distinction you hope for does not exist. We arent talking about your frame of digital neighbors but an end to self hosting. How ever nice your story sounds isnt more important then its predictable consequences. Authoritarian means with righteous sounding justifications safeguarded by only good intentions and an assumption of competence arent safeguards against totalitarianism, its its description. You cant create an authoritarian entity where you just keep the mean people away from the wheel. Thats how almost all totalitarians describe their perspective, including when some of my older relatives can be believed my great grand parents with NSDAP membership cards. You are making the exact same argument. By insisting on how competent, well intentioned and justified you are, you verifying this assumption. You acknowledge that you are unable to recognize that that isnt enough. Which means less and less people are going to tell you this. Because its really dangerous, on an individual and a societal level. [LINK] When the acoustic breaks are gone the kinetic ones will need to be engaged. Thats not a bug, they have to, there is genocide and existential risk waiting at the bottom of the slope and you dont have the ability, let alone intention, to break yourself. Which is really bad as its a very safe indicator that stuff is soon to become horrific. With anyone who tried the acoustic breaks unable to engage the k"},{"id":37627977,"author":"cf141q5325","month":"2023-09","story":"Project Gutenberg has implemented one of the worst AI fears of striking actors","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Mitchell and Webb made a great bit about checking regularly if you and your friends are maybe the baddies.","text":"Yes. Unfortunately none of this is easy. Even if you are sure you are not in a deteriorating cult (pleasant one or not) there is still a ton of blind spots not stemming from social environment but your ego for the lack of a better term. Cognitive biases are a real danger and both advertisement and cognitive warfare target those. This is very much an asymmetric struggle. While you might be a sensible person when you are paying attention to the right aspects, most of the time you arent. Or to quote the Provisional IRA of all people >Today we were unlucky, but remember we only have to be lucky once. You will have to be lucky always. I dont see a way around regularly checking if you have gotten unlucky and carried away. Its really easy to just be opposed to a caricature, figuring out and accepting that you have become one as well as a reaction is quite a lot harder. Mitchell and Webb made a great bit about checking regularly if you and your friends are maybe the baddies. [VIDEO] If people around you take offense to you doing that, thats a very valuable indicator. If this leaves you with a collapsed reality model, dont worry, thats normal. Stuff is just complicated. Fortunately less bad is often a very reachable bar."},{"id":18098128,"author":"zepto","month":"2018-09","story":"Facebook Network Breach Impacts Up to 50M Users","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"For more philosophical background on this idea","text":"By your reasoning it would be a fallacy to call any large project unethical. I think that is clearly a fallacy. Nobody is arguing that every individual within Facebook is an unethical person. The argument is that the overall project is unethical and the ethical people within it should take that possibility seriously. For more philosophical background on this idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8585521,"author":"DanBC","month":"2014-11","story":"World War II Is Full of Plot Holes (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See Mitchel and Web for a better example.","text":"It would have been funner if it had been more truthy. What actually happened fits very well with that bit of fun narrative. Using a bunch of incorrect stuff detracts from the funness. my example: the storm troopers didn't wear black. The SS did. But they didn't just wear black, they had runic emblems and skulls and shit. This minor detail is important because remembering details about genocide is probably a good thing. See Mitchel and Web for a better example. [VIDEO]"},{"id":49022671,"author":"graemep","month":"2026-07","story":"Protecting our FLOSS commons from LLMs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Its very rare for people do deliberately do bad things. Almost everyone thinks they are doing the right thing. My point (which I think should be apparent in my original comment) is that we should look at doing things \"for their own good\" has a very bad history. Now do you get it? [VIDEO]"},{"id":48511170,"author":"netsharc","month":"2026-06","story":"EV demand up 50% in France and Germany since Iran war","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory \"Are we the baddies?","text":"Obligatory \"Are we the baddies?\" [VIDEO] for those who don't know this meme (the Dawkins definition of meme)"},{"id":47173866,"author":"probably_wrong","month":"2026-02","story":"Statement from Dario Amodei on Our Discussions with the Department of War","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This should be a \"have you noticed that the caps on our hats have skulls on it?\" moment","text":"I have read the whole thing but I nonetheless want to focus on the second paragraph: > Anthropic has therefore worked proactively to deploy our models to the Department of War This should be a \"have you noticed that the caps on our hats have skulls on it?\" moment [1]. Even if one argues that the sentence should not be read literally (that is, that it's not literal war we're talking about), the only reason for calling it \"Department of War\" and \"warfighters\" instead of \"Department of Defense\" and \"soldiers\" is to gain Trump's favor, a man who dodged the draft, called soldiers \"losers\", and has been threatening to invade an ally for quite some time. There is no such a thing as a half-deal with the devil. If Anthropic wants to make money out of AI misclassifying civilians as military targets (or, as it has happened, by identifying which one residential building should be collapsed on top of a single military target, civilians be damned) good for them, but to argue that this is only okay as long as said civilians are brown is not the moral stance they think it is. Disclaimer: I'm not a US citizen. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46795594,"author":"florkbork","month":"2026-01","story":"Kash Patel says FBI is investigating Signal chats of Minnesotans tracking ICE","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"they might actually be the \"baddies","text":"I think it's important to assess the quality of the comments - they aren't bringing facts, just stating opinions; doing so quickly and agreeing with each other. You can test this out - pick a few names on the comments that disagree, ctrl+f, and you'll quickly find one individual with 29 comments at the time of writing all over the thread; with a handful of others with 1-4 responses. This is not actually what the majority of people think and feel. IE; from recent polling > 55%+ of Americans have “very little” confidence in ICE, while 16 percent only have \"some\". That's ~71% of ordinary US folks; and I would wager many international folks are very clear eyed about the situation. But why don't you see a ratio of 7/10 of top level comments critical? It's reasonable to assume that about half of those people are just keeping to themselves or part of the political middle that feel something is a \"bit wrong\"; but not quite enough to go yell into the internet about it. For the others, arguing is tiring and doesn't seem to change much. Watching the situation induces feelings of dread, despair or helplessness. On the opposing side, that 29% of people are faced with the fact that they might actually be the \"baddies\" ( [VIDEO] ), and a good number of them are flooding conversations to prove they are in fact \"not\"... because admitting otherwise would mean they are actually doing something quite morally or ethically wrong by their own or their community standards. Since that would be unthin"},{"id":46371554,"author":"netsharc","month":"2025-12","story":"US bars 5 Europeans it says pressured tech firms to censor American viewpoints","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory: \"Are we the baddies?","text":"Obligatory: \"Are we the baddies?\" [VIDEO] Looking forward to the commenters arguing that this regime aren't the baddies..."},{"id":44647447,"author":"netsharc","month":"2025-07","story":"The United States withdraws from UNESCO","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Pointing out this satirical sketch might be a cliche, but","text":"Pointing out this satirical sketch might be a cliche, but: [VIDEO] (Mitchell and Webb: Are we the Baddies?)"},{"id":41408125,"author":"duggan","month":"2024-08","story":"Shine with Gleam","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'd probably wonder \"are we the baddies?\"[1].","text":"I think if I were reading a statement like \"no nazi bullshit\" and thought it applied to me, I'd probably wonder \"are we the baddies?\"[1]. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40907130,"author":"robertlagrant","month":"2024-07","story":"Standard cells: Looking at individual gates in the Pentium processor","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"you must know something's a bit off[0].","text":"Why would one grad student have the same power as 10000 employees of a company? Surely when you're finishing with ominous repetition you must know something's a bit off[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37010411,"author":"iamflimflam1","month":"2023-08","story":"Most promoted and blocked domains among Kagi Search users","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"they just be getting that “are we the bad guys vibe","text":"You have to feel for anyone who works at pinterest - they just be getting that “are we the bad guys vibe” [VIDEO]"},{"id":8141826,"author":"celticninja","month":"2014-08","story":"Big Data Firm Says It Can Link Snowden Data To Changed Terrorist Behavior","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your definition of freedom differs from theirs. They belive in the freedom to follow their religion as they belive it should be followed. Their supporters believe in the same freedom. Your definition of freedom (and indeed mine) is very different. But you would be foolish to think that they thought of themselves as the bad guys and that they had no support amongst the local populace. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8017173,"author":"Terr_","month":"2014-07","story":"The Tech Industry vs. San Francisco","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Are we the Baddies?","text":"\"Are we the Baddies?\" ( [VIDEO] ) Probably not, but you may be working in a closer profession to them."}]},"VJ-p9qOhBv4":{"wy":"Commenters use it in earthquake threads as an anecdote that animals may sense tremors seconds before humans, and occasionally as a joke about cat alarms.","q":{"x":"you can clearly see the cats get alerted to something ~10 seconds before the shaking starts","i":39943573,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39943573,"author":"floxy","month":"2024-04","story":"M 4.8 – 2024 Whitehouse Station, New Jersey Earthquake","stance":"rec","why":"Cats visibly alerted about ten seconds before the shaking.","quote":"you can clearly see the cats get alerted to something ~10 seconds before the shaking starts","text":"Speaking of cats and earthquakes, I just watched: [VIDEO] ...again. This is a Cat Cafe in Japan, and you can clearly see the cats get alerted to something ~10 seconds before the shaking starts. Do we have an idea of what they are reacting to?"},{"id":20358178,"author":"tzs","month":"2019-07","story":"Strongest earthquake in years rattles Southern California","stance":"reference","why":"Cats wake and look around seconds before shaking starts.","quote":"Here's a video of a bunch of cats in a cat cafe in Japan whose sleep is interrupted by an earthquake.","text":"Cats can probably feel or hear the p waves, which humans usually do not notice, and which travel faster than the more noticeable (to humans) s waves. Here's a video of a bunch of cats in a cat cafe in Japan whose sleep is interrupted by an earthquake. They all wake up and start looking around about 9 seconds before the shaking starts, probably due to sensing the p wave. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24539244,"author":"hexbinencoded","month":"2020-09","story":"Earthquake detection and early alerts, now on your Android phone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Also, cat alarms","text":"For fixed locations, there are cheap P-wave detection earthquake alarms based on mechanical pendulums that will give seconds of warning. Example: [LINK] Also, cat alarms: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NQFqDKRAROI",1]]},"fnUc_W3xE1w":{"wy":"HN language learners cite it as the source of the comprehensible input idea and apply it to vocabulary tools and other learning.","q":{"x":"I've been pretty much sold on Stephen Krashen's theory of second language acquisition[1][2], which in practice focuses on comprehensible input.","i":24618704,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24618704,"author":"narwally","month":"2020-09","story":"Show HN: Vocabulary 90 – vocabulary learning automation","stance":"rec","why":"Krashen's theory of second language acquisition via comprehensible input","quote":"I've been pretty much sold on Stephen Krashen's theory of second language acquisition[1][2], which in practice focuses on comprehensible input.","text":"Anki is fantastic for its flexibility, but for language learning I've found I reach a point of diminishing returns after a certain point. From personal experience, I've been pretty much sold on Stephen Krashen's theory of second language acquisition[1][2], which in practice focuses on comprehensible input. lingq.com is a pretty good way to learn a second language this way. I use it by uploading an ebook in my target language. The app keeps track of the words I've learned, and highlights the words and phrases I'm still learning or haven't encountered. I can click on the word/phrase as I'm reading to see a definition or translation in the sidebar, and then mark the level at which I've learned it just like in Anki. It's pretty slow going at the start, and the texts you have to use are very basic, but pretty quickly I was reading entire YA novels with only a handful of unknown words on each page. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31756336,"author":"ethanwillis","month":"2022-06","story":"Launch HN: Mintlify (YC W22) – Maintainable documentation for software teams","stance":"reference","why":"Krashen's comprehensible input idea applied to documentation","quote":"","text":"I like the answers in the last part. I literally had an interview today where I quoted a professor of mine from about a decade ago. He was very upset with me about some incorrect types in my javadoc comments and told me \"The only thing worse than no documentation is wrong documentation!\" :) For your answer on good documentation, that's a good general answer. As you say there are lots of layers to it. Some ideas just from my current viewpoint: - From my forays into foreign language learning I picked up the linguist's Stephen Krashen's research after learning about it from my language exchange partner. One of the main themes of his research is the idea of \"Comprehensible Input\"(CI). CI is the idea that you acquire knowledge via exposure to new language that is at a difficulty level where you understand most of what's being said. Then you acquire these new smaller pieces of knowledge via context. I would say this is most closely related to what you mean by structure of the documentation. Obviously with some overlap with the content itself. - There's also a stronger version of CI that adds an additional piece to this, the content must be of interest to the acquirer/learner. This property is I think the closest to what I think you mean in regards to content of documentation. In my opinion reaching the first goal, Comprehensible Input, where knowledge is able to be acquired efficiently is step 1. Resources of this type can be created much more easily as there is less subjectivity a"},{"id":30128091,"author":"dymk","month":"2022-01","story":"Latin as she is spoke: How classicists tricked themselves (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Source of recent online discussion of comprehensible input","quote":"99% of the recent online discussion about this is based off of this youtube video [1] of Stephen Krashen","text":"It's not a new idea being discussed in language learning circles, and 99% of the recent online discussion about this is based off of this youtube video [1] of Stephen Krashen (a linguist) talking about comprehensible input in the 80s. If I had to guess, most of the recent discussion has started again in the Japanese language learning community, as Japanese is a notoriously difficult language to acquire for anglophones. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["l0GA901oGe4",1]]},"gRelVFm7iJE":{"wy":"HN cites it as a view of Gates evading questions and sparring with a top lawyer.","q":{"x":"He just seems like a bit of a smug nerd who's clearly enjoying going toe-to-toe with one of the country's top lawyers.","i":32946934,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":25782249,"author":"hammock","month":"2021-01","story":"Bill and Melinda Gates: America’s Top Farmland Owner","stance":"rec","why":"Highlights of Gates' antitrust deposition","quote":"I did see this video recently and found it interesting.","text":"I don't expect you to change your mind because confirmation bias is a powerful force. I did see this video recently and found it interesting. Have you seen it? \"Bill Gates - Microsoft Antitrust Deposition - Highlights\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32946934,"author":"q7xvh97o2pDhNrh","month":"2022-09","story":"Google loses EU appeal and is fined a record $4B","stance":"reference","why":"Deposition highlights showing a smug nerd going toe-to-toe with a lawyer","quote":"He just seems like a bit of a smug nerd who's clearly enjoying going toe-to-toe with one of the country's top lawyers.","text":"I found a highlights video: [VIDEO] (I see the full 11 hour deposition is also on YouTube, but I think I'll save that for another day.) So far, being 10 minutes in, I see a little bit of what you mean, but I haven't gotten to anything \"unhinged\" yet. He just seems like a bit of a smug nerd who's clearly enjoying going toe-to-toe with one of the country's top lawyers. Do you remember anything that jumped out for you from the deposition? I'd love to see more of what you're getting at."},{"id":24457083,"author":"DavidSJ","month":"2020-09","story":"Revisiting the spectacular failure that was the Bill Gates deposition","stance":"reference","why":"Deposition excerpt of Gates evading questions about an email","quote":"A: It's an attribute of the e-mail.","text":"Q: Okay. Let me ask you to look at Trial Exhibit 560. This is a message from you to Mr. Ballmer and Mr. Chase with a copy to Mr. Maritz and some other people also given copies dated August 15, 1997 at 4:07 p.m. on the subject of IBM and Netscape; correct? A: Uh-huh Q: BY MR. BOIES: And you type in here Importance: High.\" A: No. Q: No? A: No, I didn't type that. Q: Who typed in \"High\"? A: A computer. Q: A computer. Why did the computer type in \"High\"? A: It's an attribute of the e-mail. Q: And who set the attribute of the e-mail? A: Usually the sender sends that attribute. Q: Who is the sender here, Mr. Gates? A: In this case it appears I'm the sender. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hC3VTgIPoGU",1]]},"h6MfM8EFkGg":{"wy":"Recommended as the deep, expert RF analysis behind Starlink's hardware.","q":{"x":"Check out this video by The Signal Path (a Bell Labs expert!)","i":27755384,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27755384,"author":"ChrisGammell","month":"2021-07","story":"Dumping and extracting the SpaceX Starlink user terminal firmware","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown explaining the Starlink dish's phased array","quote":"Check out this video by The Signal Path (a Bell Labs expert!)","text":"It's a phased array, it uses those many little chips to do \"beam steering\". Check out this video by The Signal Path (a Bell Labs expert!) doing a teardown and explaning parts of it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46545427,"author":"maxall4","month":"2026-01","story":"Iran Goes Into IPv6 Blackout","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown showing complex FPGAs and components in Starlink dish","quote":"See this teardown","text":"Starlink receivers are actually very complicated. They make use of a bunch of high-end FPGAs and a bunch of other expensive and uncommon components. See this teardown: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":26761400,"author":"StringyBob","month":"2021-04","story":"Setting up Starlink","stance":"reference","why":"Detailed RF analysis of the Starlink dish","quote":"And more detailed RF analysis at","text":"There’s a pretty crazy amount of electronics in the dish. They are likely selling it at well below cost at present. Teardown showing PCB at: [LINK] And more detailed RF analysis at [VIDEO]"}]},"kWOAHIpmLAI":{"wy":"Cited in Rails performance and Rack design discussions for Patterson's analysis of stack depth and garbage collection.","q":{"x":"The interesting stuff happens around the 20 min. mark.","i":2808193,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2808193,"author":"maercsrats","month":"2011-07","story":"Rails 3.1.0 to be released on 22nd August","stance":"rec","why":"Patterson explains why Rails 3.1 is slower and how a shallower Rack pipeline fixes it.","quote":"The interesting stuff happens around the 20 min. mark.","text":"There is a talk by Aaron Patterson from RailsConf talking about why rails 3.1 is slower than rails 3 and rails 2.3. Basically the rack pipeline is getting deeper. Because rack is so simplistic every request requires you going through that pipeline and back again. His solution is to break up the pipeline into 3 separate types: generators, filters (for response data), and lifecycle hooks. Doing this brought the speed back up to rails 2.3 speeds and a bit faster in some instances. But those were early numbers. You can see his talk here: [VIDEO] The interesting stuff happens around the 20 min. mark. Overall, there are a lot of people who want to make ruby better and faster. Rubinius, Jruby and MacRuby are all examples. There are also people who want to make Rails faster. They are examining the slower parts (active record, rack, etc) and working to make it better."},{"id":4117037,"author":"jonsmock","month":"2012-06","story":"Why your web framework should not adopt Rack API","stance":"reference","why":"Patterson discusses Rack API design at 26:45.","quote":"Aaron Patterson (tenderlove) talks about this in his RailsConf talk, starting at 26:45.","text":"Nice article, Jose! For those interested, Aaron Patterson (tenderlove) talks about this in his RailsConf talk, starting at 26:45. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3381834,"author":"getsat","month":"2011-12","story":"Rails master is now 4.0.0.beta; Ruby 1.8.7 support dropped","stance":"reference","why":"Patterson on reducing middleware stack depth to speed up Rails apps.","quote":"Here's the video where he talks about it (towards the beginning)","text":"As the number of Rack middleware pieces in a base Rails app grows, it gets slower. MRI and YARV spend a lot more time doing GC as the stack depth grows. Aaron Patterson mentioned that he was working on a modification to the Rack API where each piece of middleware did not call the next piece, reducing the stack depth and drastically improving the runtime performance of Rails apps. Here's the video where he talks about it (towards the beginning): [VIDEO]"},{"id":3012613,"author":"getsat","month":"2011-09","story":"CoderTalk - Episode 5. Has Rails Lost It's Rails? Slow Rails Test, etc.","stance":"reference","why":"Patterson ties Rails 3 slowness to MRI garbage collection on deep stacks.","quote":"he came to the conclusion that Rails 3's slowness is because MRI sucks at GC on a deep stack depth","text":"In Aaron Patterson's recent \"Double Dream Hands: So Intense!\" presentation[0], he came to the conclusion that Rails 3's slowness is because MRI sucks at GC on a deep stack depth. The average stack depth of Rails 3 applications is ENORMOUS. He mentions in the video that he rewrote the Rack API to pass a mutated var to the next piece of middleware in the Rack chain instead of calling the next middleware and passing an env var to it. The gains were pretty impressive. Hopefully, they make this change to the Rack API for Rails 3.2. That change + the 1.9.3 fix for requiring files[1] should result in much faster overall Rails performance. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":2778943,"author":"mdasen","month":"2011-07","story":"Ask HN: Where is the Django community?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't want to be defending the OP's position, but. . . settings.py is a mess. Even Simon's written a post bashing it ( [LINK] ). I cringe a little starting up a project and having to dive into the settings.py file. In some ways, Django tries to be flexible, but it's clumsy. For example, you can have your templates in your app directories. So, you have /path/to/project/posts/templates and /path/to/project/people/templates. However, you can't just put your templates in there because Django doesn't namespace them. Like, if I do {% extends \"people/base.html\" %} it will look in both those template directories for a people folder. It won't be like, \"oh, I should look in /path/to/project/people/templates/base.html\". I have to put the template in /path/to/project/people/templates/people/base.html. That's clumsy. urls.py is another flexible/clumsy thing (IMO). Django wants me to write a regexp for the simple cases. It's kludgy to do r'^people/(?P<id>\\d+)/?$' just to have a URL with an ID in it. And other frameworks have been able to offer the power of regexps for the case where you want them while making the common cases a lot easier. Django caches certain things and if you try to change it at runtime, well, it doesn't change. Django's reusable apps aren't that reusable. They were a lot more reusable than Rails' apps, but the Rails team has worked on that a lot - especially with 3.1 and how each app can have its own assets (images/css/js) all namespaced and neat and tidy. There isn'"}]},"nNMNglk_CvE":{"wy":"It is the standard citation in IPv6 threads that large mobile carriers already run phones with only IPv6 addresses.","q":{"x":"Here's a dude from T-Mobile explaining how they have (in 2017) over ten million end-customer devices without any IPv4 addresses assigned","i":28192695,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":14501823,"author":"MrRadar","month":"2017-06","story":"Five years of IPv6: whither the next five?","stance":"rec","why":"T-Mobile's IPv6 transition progress at the 2017 Rocky Mountain IPv6 Summit.","quote":"at the 2017 Rocky Mountain IPv6 Summit that's about where T-Mobile US","text":"It's interesting how his chart deflects somewhere around 80%, since at the 2017 Rocky Mountain IPv6 Summit that's about where T-Mobile US (who probably have the highest penetration of IPv6-capable endpoints of any ISP) noted their transition started slowing drastically.[0] The main reasons are that the remaining devices are old (e.g. 3G phones), have limited capabilities/needs (e.g. embedded IoT devices which just need to phone home once in a while), or are using odd configurations (e.g. for some reason T-Mobile doesn't currently support IPv6 for tethering). Another good talk from that conference is about how Carrier Grade NATs are (amazingly) even worse in practice than they sound in theory[1]. During the Q&A portion one of the audience members points out that as technologies like CGN become required to provide IPv4 service it will cause the cost of supporting IPv4 to rise over time and naturally push ISPs to kill IPv4 as the use of v4 drops while the costs to support it do not. I can also recommend a short talk by Cloudflare about their experience serving IPv6 traffic[2] and another on the history and future of IPv6[3]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":46033428,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2025-11","story":"ISPs more likely to throttle netizens who connect through CG-NAT: Cloudflare","stance":"reference","why":"Rocky Mountain IPv6 Taskforce 2017 talk on T-Mobile IPv6-only handsets.","quote":"And Rocky Mountain IPv6 Taskforce in 2017","text":"> Isn’t essentially the entire US on CG-NAT for IPv4 on mobile data? T-Mobile, for one, has had their handsets IPv6-only for a few years, so if your Android/iOS does a DNS lookup and gets an AAAA record back, it will skip CG-NAT. T-Mobile presenting at NANOG in 2018 on IPv6: * [LINK] And Rocky Mountain IPv6 Taskforce in 2017: * [VIDEO] Further data: > Chances are if you use the Internet on your smartphone, you are connecting via IPv6. According to the Internet Society’s 2018 State of IPv6 Deployment,[1] 80% of smartphones in the US on the major cellular network operators use IPv6 and major mobile networks are driving IPv6 adoption with Verizon Wireless at 84%, Sprint at 70%, T-Mobile USA at 93%, and AT&T Wireless at 57%. Plus, some mobile networks are taking the step to run IPv6-only to simplify network operations and reduce costs. * [LINK] Though some folks aren't happy with the implementation: > But from my own experience, neither T-Mobile nor AT&T allows inbound traffic to the phone's IPv6 address. This negates some of the advantages of having a globally routable IPv6 address. * [LINK]"},{"id":44792287,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2025-08","story":"I tried living on IPv6 for a day","stance":"reference","why":"Shows mobile telcos with millions of IPv6-only users.","quote":"there are mobile telco networks with tens (hundreds?) of millions of people getting only IPv6 addresses on their devices.","text":"Trying to re-engine a 747 in mid-air is a challenging operation. > So IPv6 is about 30 years old, and the testimony being shared is the chair of the group spending years of research and millions of dollars, finally launching ipv6 corporate lans in 2023. And how many years of research and millions of dollars was spent in the 1990s on IPv4? People used to use workstations as routers (e.g., see SunOS routeadm(1M)) and DNS caching servers before a lot of money was poured into ASICs for routing (and switching). There are all sorts of dumbass things with IPv4: how much time has been wasted on engineering solutions around NAT (e.g., STUN/TURN/ICE)? But because IPv4 just happens to be the default we accept them as 'normal' and anything that is different is treated as 'abnormal'. > You're not selling me on it's viability. I'm not sure how it's not viable given there are mobile telco networks with tens (hundreds?) of millions of people getting only IPv6 addresses on their devices. * [VIDEO] * [LINK] There are some folks who (a) were lucky enough to get in early on the IPv4 address land rush, or (b) are rich enough to be able to purchase IPv4 addresses, but there are also (c) plenty of folks who are left with scraps for IPv4 connectivity. The fact that you happen to fall into (a) or (b) does not mean you get to dismiss the folks in (c) who need IPv6, as otherwise they'd have no connectivity at all."},{"id":42317163,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2024-12","story":"No NAT November: My month without IPv4","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence IPv6 is ready: T-Mobile handsets are IPv6-only.","quote":"T-Mobile US is IPv6-only on handsets, as are many other carriers","text":"> At the rate it's going, IPv6 should be ready […] It's ready now. T-Mobile US is IPv6-only on handsets, as are many other carriers: * [VIDEO] Microsoft went towards IPv6-only in corporate networks so that their IPv4 address could be moved to Azure to generate revenue: * [LINK] See also Google and their offices: * [LINK] Facebook/Meta is IPv6-only in their DCs: * [LINK] According to Google, the US and UK is half IPv6 (France and Germany are >75%): * [LINK]"},{"id":39997394,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-04","story":"Do You Need IPv4 Anymore?","stance":"reference","why":"2017 presentation on T-Mobile US going IPv6-only.","quote":"a 2017 presentation on T-Mobile US going IPv6-only","text":"> Ha! I have an iPhone 15 and when I switch to mobile I get an IPv4-only address! My mobile provider is Verizon. APNIC 34 (2017) presentation on Verizon and IPv6: * [LINK] Also, a 2017 presentation on T-Mobile US going IPv6-only: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40008999,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-04","story":"Do You Need IPv4 Anymore?","stance":"reference","why":"One of several examples of organizations deliberately adopting IPv6.","quote":"T-Mobile US","text":"> I have yet to meet an infrastructure engineer or even hear of one who switched to IPv6 on purpose because they thought it was better. T-Mobile US: * [VIDEO] Wells Fargo: * [LINK] Microsoft * [LINK] * [LINK] Google: * [LINK] LinkedIn: * [LINK] Facebook is famously IPv6-only internally (and as of 2016, 60% of user traffic was over IPv6): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":40044058,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-04","story":"Why No IPv6?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of large organization using IPv6-only.","quote":"Tell that to T-Mobile US","text":"Tell that to T-Mobile US: * [VIDEO] Wells Fargo: * [LINK] Microsoft: * [LINK] * [LINK] Google: * [LINK] LinkedIn (pre-Microsoft): * [LINK]"},{"id":37568868,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-09","story":"AWS IPv4 Estate Now Worth $4.5B","stance":"reference","why":"T-Mobile's 2017 going-IPv6-only presentation as counterexample to hardware excuses.","quote":"T-Mobile was gave a presentation on going IPv6-only in 2017","text":"> AFAIK, IPv6 adoption is held back by hardware. What hardware, especially ASICs, do not support wire speed IPv6 and have not for a decade or two? T-Mobile was gave a presentation on going IPv6-only in 2017: > For the past 10 years T-Mobile has worked towards creating an IPv6 environment and we are now getting very close to our goal. Stephan presents learning on how to successfully enable IPv6-only using DNS64 with or without 464XLAT. He will do a live demo of the different IP interfaces on an Android handset. Finally, he will discuss and give some best practices on how to handle DNS, applications, and websites that are having issues with DNS64. * [VIDEO] So they started in 2007. Whatever ISP you're with has probably had at least 2-3 tech refreshes in which IPv6 hardware has been available. For the CPE, Free in France had IPv6 in 2007: * [LINK]"},{"id":35719936,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-04","story":"Thinking about our passive exposure to IPv6 issues","stance":"reference","why":"T-Mobile presentation on IPv6-only with translation to reach IPv4.","quote":"Presentations from T-Mobile at NANOG and Rocky Mountain IPv6 Taskforce","text":"> T-Mobile and Verizon both use CGNAT (but do issue individual ipv6 addresses to end user devices). More specifically, T-Mobile US only assigns IPv6 addresses to devices, and to reach the IPv4 world you have to go through a translation mechanism. Presentations from T-Mobile at NANOG and Rocky Mountain IPv6 Taskforce: * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":33901556,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2022-12","story":"Tell HN: IPv6-only still pretty much unusable","stance":"reference","why":"Rebuts claim that all mobile clients are behind CG-NAT.","quote":"T-Mobile US mobile clients are IPv6-only and connect via IPv6 to IPv6 sites","text":"> All mobile clients are behind CG-NAT. Demonstrably false. T-Mobile US mobile clients are IPv6-only and connect via IPv6 to IPv6 sites: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] NAT is only used to connect to IPv4-only hosts via DNS64 (with or without 464XLAT). As of 2022Q2, T-Mobile US has 110 million customers: * [LINK] One-third of the US population is connecting via IPv6-only on a day-to-day, hour-to-hour basis every time their smartphone reaches out over the radio."},{"id":32202595,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-07","story":"Two containers with same number detected in Chittagong port","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for cell networks giving phones only IPv6 addresses.","quote":"Lots of cell networks only give IPv6 addresses to phones.","text":"> […] ipv4 is still the majority of internet traffic. Not if you're on mobile. Lots of cell networks only give IPv6 addresses to phones. E.g., T-Mobile in the US: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":31752988,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-06","story":"Is IPv6 faster than IPv4?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence T-Mobile USA devices are IPv6-only.","quote":"If you are on T-Mobile USA you are IPv6-only on your mobile device","text":"> Anyone running v6 only and bridging to v4 on the edge? If you are on T-Mobile USA you are IPv6-only on your mobile device: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] Lots of folks are probably IPv6-only without realizing it."},{"id":30220686,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-02","story":"The Formation of IPv4 Address Markets (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"T-Mobile's IPv6-only rollout with DNS64 and 464XLAT, with live Android demo.","quote":"Stephan presents learning on how to successfully enable IPv6-only using DNS64 with or without 464XLAT.","text":"\"T-Mobile’s path to IPv6 Only\": > For the past 10 years [US] T-Mobile has worked towards creating an IPv6 environment and we are now getting very close to our goal. Stephan presents learning on how to successfully enable IPv6-only using DNS64 with or without 464XLAT. He will do a live demo of the different IP interfaces on an Android handset. Finally, he will discuss and give some best practices on how to handle DNS, applications, and websites that are having issues with DNS64. * [VIDEO] Apple mandated that all app in the iOS/mobile App Store had to support working on IPv6-only networks: * [LINK] Perhaps they should to the same thing for the macOS App Store."},{"id":30223961,"author":"zacwest","month":"2022-02","story":"The Formation of IPv4 Address Markets (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim about native IPv6-only on T-Mobile iPhones.","quote":"iPhones on T-Mobile do not use 464XLAT, they use native IPv6 only.","text":"iPhones on T-Mobile do not use 464XLAT, they use native IPv6 only. Some system frameworks like URLSession can do the NAT64 translation of hard-coded IPv4 addresses but it still goes over IPv6. In the video linked above: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30127060,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-01","story":"Reasons for servers to support IPv6","stance":"reference","why":"Shows phones already use IPv6 daily.","quote":"For example see the video \"T-Mobile’s path to IPv6 Only\"","text":"> What's really interesting is how many of these \"we fear change\" IT people don't realize they're already using IPv6 on their phones every day, with a majority of the sites they visit. For example see the video \"T-Mobile’s path to IPv6 Only\": * [VIDEO]"},{"id":29503483,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-12","story":"IPv4 Waiting List","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence T-Mobile US phones are IPv6-only.","quote":"If you have a mobile device on US T-Mobile you only get a IPv6 address on it","text":"> I wonder when (or if) we will see new IPv6 only ISPs. If you have a mobile device on US T-Mobile you only get a IPv6 address on it: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK] Any access to IPv4 is through a proxy."},{"id":28192695,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-08","story":"India is leading IPv6 migration with 61.67% adoption","stance":"reference","why":"T-Mobile engineer explains IPv6-only customer devices using DNS64 and 464XLAT.","quote":"Here's a dude from T-Mobile explaining how they have (in 2017) over ten million end-customer devices without any IPv4 addresses assigned","text":"I'm not sure what you're referring to. Here's a dude from T-Mobile explaining how they have (in 2017) over ten million end-customer devices without any IPv4 addresses assigned: * [VIDEO] For reaching services that are IPv6 only they use(d) DNS64 (with and without 464XLAT)."},{"id":28328930,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-08","story":"IPv4 vs. IPv6 FAQ","stance":"reference","why":"T-Mobile presentation on IPv6-only mobile networking with CG-NAT.","quote":"Here's a guy from T-Mobile presenting on it","text":"> This should help address these types of issues so more devs can get onto iOS more easily without having to meet Apple's level of requirements. Well if an app developer does not ensure that their code works on a device that only has an IPv6 address they will be in for a big surprise: lots of mobile telcos only give out IPv6 addresses. Connecting to the IPv4 world is done via CG-NAT with the first few hops being IPv6-only. Here's a guy from T-Mobile presenting on it: * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["r3snVCRo_bI",1]]},"sR8M4zARBXY":{"wy":"Brought up in threads on synthetic food and flavor as a fun example of building a dish from chemicals.","q":{"x":"There's a great recent Thought Emporium video where he synthesizes chicken noodle soup from pure chemicals","i":38631497,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38631497,"author":"rdlw","month":"2023-12","story":"OpenCola","stance":"rec","why":"Synthesizing chicken noodle soup from pure chemicals","quote":"There's a great recent Thought Emporium video where he synthesizes chicken noodle soup from pure chemicals","text":"There's a great recent Thought Emporium video where he synthesizes chicken noodle soup from pure chemicals: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38850911,"author":"cf1241290841","month":"2024-01","story":"Few adults are interested in trying \"lab-grown\" meat","stance":"reference","why":"Artificial chicken flavor soup recipe","quote":"See thought emporium for \"chicken flavored soup\" on that.","text":"I wonder more and more why we even go down that path. \"Like meat\" is hitting the consistency more and more and you can create the flavor artificially. See thought emporium for \"chicken flavored soup\" on that. Recipe for diy [VIDEO]"},{"id":37849857,"author":"lbotos","month":"2023-10","story":"Ammonium chloride tastes like nothing else. It may be the sixth basic taste","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"wat. Did you watch the same video?","text":"wat. Did you watch the same video? [VIDEO] Here's a simple test you can try yourself: put some food in your mouth and hold your nose. What do you taste? Now do it again with your nose not blocked. Try this for various foods. I got this from Science and Cooking: [LINK] The first chapter talks about how aroma is a component of flavor and you can use this test to identify if your ingredients are giving you taste (tongue) or aroma molecules (nose)"}],"r":[["rgaTLrZGlk0",1],["PtKhbbcc1Rc",1],["xOCurBYI_gY",1],["t_rzYnXEQlE",1]]},"t-_VPRCtiUg":{"wy":"Cited as an intuitive illustration of synchronization, particularly the metronome demonstration.","q":{"x":"it is worth watching the entire video","i":28352357,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28352357,"author":"sphericalgames","month":"2021-08","story":"Train Wheels Are Cones","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the problem; worth watching entirely","quote":"it is worth watching the entire video","text":"This video explains the problem - it is worth watching the entire video. If you are pressed for time jump to 15:00 mark [VIDEO]"},{"id":28708122,"author":"LeifCarrotson","month":"2021-09","story":"When listeners pay attention to stories, their heart rates rise and fall in sync","stance":"reference","why":"Intuitive example of metronome synchronization","quote":"For an intuitive example, see this Veritasium video, starting at around 4:50","text":"There's some synchronization effects in metronomes and in industrial equipment. If you have several things that can oscillate, perhaps not at precisely the same rate but close, and the motions reinforce each other when they do achieve the same rate, you can get surprising synchronization. For an intuitive example, see this Veritasium video, starting at around 4:50: [VIDEO] . This happens in crowds of people, for an example, the London Millenium pedestrian bridge wobble: [LINK] . I was initially curious how this could happen with a heart rate; I could imagine some shared feedback mechanism for walking, or breathing, but a shared feedback for heart rate was surprising. Perhaps if they were touching, and there was some electrical or pressure-sensitive pulse? Disappointing that it's merely the delta between exciting and dull parts of the stories."},{"id":27013070,"author":"juhatl","month":"2021-05","story":"Longest pedestrian suspension bridge opens in Portugal","stance":"reference","why":"Interesting info on synchronization and bridges","quote":"There's more interesting information on synchronization and bridges on Veritasium's video","text":"Apparently an incident took place in 1831 where a bridge collapsed under soldiers marching, but the Wikipedia article [1] notes that \"The conclusion of the investigation was that the vibration caused by the marching precipitated the bolt's failure, but it would have failed eventually anyway\". There's more interesting information on synchronization and bridges on Veritasium's video, The Secret of Synchronization [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"03iwAY4KlIU":{"wy":"Cited as an example of a distinctive local dialect, with unfamiliar words and phrases.","q":{"x":"the vid gives examples of words I've never heard","i":2047052,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":8672629,"author":"bane","month":"2014-11","story":"Where Are the Hardest Places to Live in the U.S.?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the unique local Appalachian dialect.","quote":"Local dialect","text":"There is, lots of ski resorts, hiking, that sort of thing. The kicker is that those same places will actually hire in lots of Europeans to work the facilities, so not only are the employees not local, they aren't even from the same country! It's a shame, there's a kernel of some great culture there, a unique dialect, great music, entertaining local history and color, some beautiful folk art. Local dialect [VIDEO] The traditional music is a descendant of Celtic folk music, but unique enough to be interesting. Here's a fantastic documentary on the music [LINK] All it would really take would be for some kind of Appalachian Music to become wildly popular and I could see the region getting popular music centers people would come to visit. Their main possible export market is all cultural."},{"id":6317067,"author":"jfarmer","month":"2013-09","story":"That Time I Made A Website","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's a few things worth responding to in your comment, some technical and non-technical, but both important for beginners to keep in mind. I assume you know most of what I'm going to say, so I'm pretending a student asked this. So, what's the problem with the <center> tag? First, at the purely technical level, <center> was deprecated in HTML4.01 and is not part of HTML5 at all. As far as I know browsers will still render it fine, but it's technically not valid HTML5. Second, <center> was deprecated along with other tags like <font>, <blink>, <big>, and <strike> because of a popular movement in the late 90s to encapsulate the logical and semantic structure of the document in HTML and the display structure in CSS. So, there's a case to me made for not using it as a Best Practice™, irrespective of the technical details about what browsers do or don't do, whether it's deprecated or not, etc. Third, other people take your code as a reflection of what was going on inside your head when you wrote it. Your HTML and other code exists in a cultural milieu whether you like it or not. Potential employers will certainly read these sorts of things into your code, so if you care about that then you should care about what they'll think when they see you using the <center> tag. In sociolinguistics there's an idea of the \"prestige\" of a dialect. For example, in the US, someone speaking Received Pronunciation ( [LINK] ) will be perceived as more educated and worldly while something speaking "},{"id":2047052,"author":"Anechoic","month":"2010-12","story":"After correcting for demographics, US schools fare better than EU/Asian schools","stance":"reference","why":"Examples of unfamiliar words and combinations in Appalachian English.","quote":"the vid gives examples of words I've never heard","text":"Well those weren't exhaustive examples, but I'd still disagree - for the longest time, I though \"hoagie\" was a polish food (I grew up in \"grinder\" territory) and I was mystified the first time someone asked me to pick up an \"orange coke\" at the drug store. Another example: [VIDEO] it's all English, and you can often get a general meaning from context, but the vid gives examples of words I've never heard, or more familiar English words used in combinations that obscure the meaning. So of course you can take someone from the video and drop them in NYC and they'll be able to function, but there will likely be things they miss and things NYCers will miss talking to them. Now in a school environment, that can make a huge distance."}]},"0qbrLiGY4Cg":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it in critiques of Apple's hardware choices and base-model storage and memory, as an example of a design flaw.","m":[{"id":47350425,"author":"hermanzegerman","month":"2026-03","story":"Big Data on the Cheapest MacBook","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Also there are countless reports of bricked M1 8GB MacBook Airs","text":"Not if you're powerusing it like in the Article and relying heavily on Swap. Also there are countless reports of bricked M1 8GB MacBook Airs that are bricked because the SSD used up it's write cycles [VIDEO]"},{"id":46382576,"author":"trinsic2","month":"2025-12","story":"I'm returning my Framework 16","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Louis Rossman talked about a design flaw in Apple Macbooks","text":"I'm pretty sure part of the reason of integrating everything on the board has some nefarious reasons, at least on Laptop's. Louis Rossman talked about a design flaw in Apple Macbooks where if the SSD fails, in my cases, your system will fail to power up because the mainboard is designed to fail when the SSD fails.(If I am interpreting that correctly)[0]. Remember this flaw is in the Macbooks where the SSD's are soldiered into the board. IMHO there are ways to design integrated hardware in such a way where failures minimize damage and I think many companies decide its not in there best interest to design hardware to prevent that. IMHO this is done in bad faith. [0}: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41361241,"author":"replete","month":"2024-08","story":"Apple to upgrade base Macs to 16GB RAM, starting from Apple M4 models","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple SSDs engineered in the worst way possible","text":"I hope one day they can fix the engineering problems so I can buy a machine and keep it for more than two years without fear of some cheap components exploding. A purchased computer should work for a decade at least. If this sounds over the top, look at what the repair shop guys are saying... Edit: Thanks for the downvotes, its not popular but an unfortunate reality. These issues are related to 2016-present Apple laptops. My 2012 MBA is running just fine, but I cannot trust these soldered systems.. there's other content around about the 13v being dumped into the SSDs when another component fails. There are engineering issues. If you want to understand what I'm talking about: Apple SSDs engineered in the worst way possible: [VIDEO] Exploding USB-C MacBook Ports Explained: [LINK]"},{"id":41377175,"author":"replete","month":"2024-08","story":"Apple to upgrade base Macs to 16GB RAM, starting from Apple M4 models","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There absolutely are issues, whether you accept it or not","text":"There absolutely are issues, whether you accept it or not, and it is nothing to do with 'not trusting TPM'. Apple SSDs engineered in the worst way possible: [VIDEO] Exploding USB-C MacBook Ports Explained: [LINK]"}]},"1_FXVjf46T8":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as evidence that Dragon's built-in launch escape system works and that a failed launch need not kill the crew.","q":{"x":"SpaceX demo'ed their launch abort system","i":11645162,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":18193005,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2018-10","story":"Astronauts escape malfunctioning Soyuz rocket","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dragon's built-in escape system successfully tested.","quote":"It's built into the capsule, and has been successfully tested.","text":"SpaceX's crewed Dragon is the escape system. It's built into the capsule, and has been successfully tested. [VIDEO] The BFR, to my knowledge, lacks one. Not sure if Musk has commented on that; I'd imagine his likely argument is that airliners don't either."},{"id":17009489,"author":"rwh86","month":"2018-05","story":"NASA advisers say SpaceX rocket technology could put lives at risk","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant footage of the pad abort test.","quote":"I think the more relevant videos are","text":"I think the more relevant videos are: Pad abort test: [VIDEO] Pad abort test (POV): [LINK] Stream of the same test: [LINK]"},{"id":12407046,"author":"rory096","month":"2016-09","story":"A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket has exploded at Cape Canaveral","stance":"reference","why":"Test of Dragon 2 launch escape system.","quote":"Here's a video of the LES test for Dragon 2","text":"Actually they specifically plan to load astronauts before fueling begins. If they're in the capsule the launch escape system (LES)[1] can propel them away from any on-pad disaster; not so if they're still walking across the gantry. That said, NASA has raised concerns about loading crew pre-fueling as part of the NASA-SpaceX Commercial Crew development contract (CCDev). [1] Here's a video of the LES test for Dragon 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12408027,"author":"rst","month":"2016-09","story":"A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket has exploded at Cape Canaveral","stance":"reference","why":"Shows pad deluge system and abort thrusters firing.","quote":"the pad deluge system is more visible than usual in this video of the Dragon pad-abort test","text":"The point of those systems was to limit damage from vibration and rocket exhaust on a normal launch, not to allow the pad to withstand an explosion. (And SpaceX does have them as well; the pad deluge system is more visible than usual in this video of the Dragon pad-abort test, which was conducted at the same launch pad they use for Falcon 9s, though without the rocket itself or any of its mobile support equipment: [VIDEO] -- the large streams of water kick in a few seconds before the Dragon abort thrusters kick off.)"},{"id":12439774,"author":"GenerocUsername","month":"2016-09","story":"SpaceX is using Boeing's 1920s strategy to get to Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence a failed launch need not mean death.","quote":"A failed launch does not necessarily imply death.","text":"A failed launch does not necessarily imply death. [VIDEO] However, your point stands. Space travel will never catch on if it combines \"Extreme Sport\" mortality rates with C-level costs."},{"id":11645162,"author":"mikeyouse","month":"2016-05","story":"SpaceX lands rocket at sea second time after satellite launch","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX demo of launch abort system protecting crew.","quote":"SpaceX demo'ed their launch abort system","text":"Since they don't have to deal with the insulation issues like the shuttle did, the most dangerous phase of flight for a crew is during launch. Last Spring, SpaceX demo'ed their launch abort system which in theory should be able to protect the astronauts against any launch problems; [VIDEO]"},{"id":10990564,"author":"ansible","month":"2016-01","story":"SpaceX successfully tests parachute for bringing astronauts back [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstration of Dragon's thruster-based launch escape.","quote":"They demonstrated this with their pad abort test","text":"They also need the parachutes because if there is an emergency during launch, the thrusters will be used to separate the capsule from the launch vehicle. They demonstrated this with their pad abort test: [VIDEO] Perhaps later this year we will also see an in-flight abort test, which will be very exciting."},{"id":10604131,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2015-11","story":"NASA Orders SpaceX Crew Mission to International Space Station","stance":"reference","why":"Pad abort test builds confidence in SuperDraco thrusters.","quote":"after the unmanned pad abort test [1]","text":"I'm assuming NASA is pretty confident in SpaceX's SuperDraco thrusters after the unmanned pad abort test [1]. With regards to the second comment, I can find out if you're interested. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"3SlOXowwC4c":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that ammunition cooking off in a fire is much less dangerous than people assume.","q":{"x":"Ammo cooking off in a fire sounds dramatic but isn't particularly dangerous [0]","i":15447053,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":25063725,"author":"bacondude3","month":"2020-11","story":"Disaster prepping is mainstream again","stance":"reference","why":"Ammo is not dangerous in a fire","quote":"Ammo is not dangerous in a fire","text":"Again, wrong. Ammo is not dangerous in a fire: [VIDEO] As for humidity, I think you would be surprised. Much of Appalachia and the East Coast have high humidity levels (typically 70-90% during summer IIRC) and I've never found ammo damaged by humidity there. I've shot 25 year old ammo that was put in an ammo can and dumped in a hiding spot near a pond (so, very high humidity) and had no issues shooting with it. Even if the ammo is stored in the manufacturer's cardboard box, I wouldn't worry about humidity damage unless average levels are over 85 or 90%. Water damage is a different matter, but I don't have any experience with that."},{"id":20897534,"author":"billh","month":"2019-09","story":"TP-82 Cosmonaut survival pistol","stance":"reference","why":"Unchambered rounds in a fire are not a big deal","quote":"Even if it does go off, as long as the rounds are not chambered it's really not that big of a deal.","text":"Even if it does go off, as long as the rounds are not chambered it's really not that big of a deal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15447053,"author":"Merad","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: My house might burn down in the next 24 hours. Suggestions?","stance":"reference","why":"Ammo cooking off in a fire is not very dangerous","quote":"Ammo cooking off in a fire sounds dramatic but isn't particularly dangerous [0]","text":"Ammo cooking off in a fire sounds dramatic but isn't particularly dangerous [0], unless of course a loaded gun is burning. In the OP's position it would be really low on my list of priorities. [0]: [VIDEO] (skip to about 13 min)"},{"id":8730143,"author":"MrLeap","month":"2014-12","story":"Flying with Machine Guns?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows fired cartridges outside a chamber lack penetrating energy","quote":"it demonstrates that a rifle cartridge fired without a chamber doesn't have the energy to penetrate cardboard","text":"Most bullet cases are made out of brass. I'm no rocket scientist, but brass cases would make a horrible rocket engine material. The only reason cases don't catastrophically fail when you fire a gun is because of the bolt/chamber providing support. Brass IS pretty good about stretching quite a bit before a catastrophic failure. This property is what allows case fire-forming ( [LINK] ). It's also a pretty awesome safety feature. Cases are so bad by themselves about containing the force of expanding gas, that ammunition fires aren't nearly as dangerous as you might expect them to be. ( [VIDEO] ) It's a long video, but if I recall correctly, it demonstrates that a rifle cartridge fired without a chamber doesn't have the energy to penetrate cardboard at like an 11 inch range."}]},"5c6AADI7Pb4":{"wy":"Referenced as a concrete example of how weak Google Maps listing verification enables phone fraud and impersonation.","q":{"x":"he was able to impersonate the US Secret Service and recorded a bunch of phone calls","i":20241185,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":40244901,"author":"Scion9066","month":"2024-05","story":"You receive a call on your phone. The caller says they're from your bank","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about using fake Google Maps listings to intercept law enforcement calls.","quote":"here's a talk from someone who used fake Google Maps listings to intercept phone calls between law enforcement","text":"It's not just ads or banks, here's a talk from someone who used fake Google Maps listings to intercept phone calls between law enforcement: Wiretapping the Secret Service Can Be Easy and Fun | Bryan Seely | TEDxKirkland [VIDEO]"},{"id":20241185,"author":"relic","month":"2019-06","story":"Google Maps is filled with false business addresses pretending to be nearby","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker exploited fake Maps listings to intercept Secret Service calls.","quote":"he was able to impersonate the US Secret Service and recorded a bunch of phone calls","text":"I happened on a YouTube video of a TedX talk where a guy is really into this Google Maps scam. He got to the point where he was able to impersonate the US Secret Service and recorded a bunch of phone calls. The Secret Service told google to fix their maps issue, but he said they just disabled some features and reactived them without change a few weeks later. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18528828,"author":"adiian","month":"2018-11","story":"Scammers are changing the contact details for banks on Google Maps","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on wiretapping FBI and Secret Service via Google Maps listing verification gaps.","quote":"In 2014 Bryan Seely wiretapped FBI and Secret Service exploiting the Google maps (lack of) verification process.","text":"In 2014 Bryan Seely wiretapped FBI and Secret Service exploiting the Google maps (lack of) verification process. According to his tedtalk, Google was not to eager to fix the problem and it seems things didn't change much since then. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18545844,"author":"electrograv","month":"2018-11","story":"We are Google employees – Google must drop Dragonfly","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that Google does little about fraudulent directory listings.","quote":"here is evidence that Google does absolutely nothing about fraudulent directory listings and ads","text":"P.S. In fact, here is evidence that Google does absolutely nothing about fraudulent directory listings and ads, like the one you seek to think will be solved by Google moving into China: [VIDEO]"}]},"9x4_dozWkq0":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it ironically, as evidence of Microsoft's earlier privacy marketing against Google that sits awkwardly with its later telemetry.","m":[{"id":21929673,"author":"Buge","month":"2020-01","story":"How to stay private when using Android","stance":"reference","why":"Microsoft ad criticizing Google's email scanning, cited as evidence","quote":"And a Microsoft ad criticizing Google for it","text":"See news articles about it from the time, and a petition telling Google to stop: [LINK] [LINK] And a Microsoft ad criticizing Google for it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20556905,"author":"starsinspace","month":"2019-07","story":"Report on Microsoft Office and Windows software: still privacy risks remaining","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"they had this video, targeting Google's privacy problems","text":"Microsoft is really shooting themselves in the foot with their terrible approach to privacy ever since Win10. Given that they earn money by selling actual products, they could stand right next to Apple as a defender of privacy if they wanted to (unlike Google, which has a business model which depends on data collection)... but they choose not to. It's not so long ago, in 2011, they had this video, targeting Google's privacy problems: [VIDEO] Now they're no better than Google. What happened?"},{"id":17001975,"author":"rasz","month":"2018-05","story":"How much does Apple know about me? The answer surprised me","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"From Scroogled to \"telemetry\" collecting user keyboard input in 4 years.","text":"Microsoft was there in ~2011-2014. Check these out [VIDEO] [LINK] From Scroogled to \"telemetry\" collecting user keyboard input in 4 years."},{"id":7453610,"author":"Kerrick","month":"2014-03","story":"Microsoft defends its right to read your email","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's terribly ironic","text":"It's terribly ironic, considering the video they \"leaked\" a couple years ago lambasting Gmail for reading your email. [VIDEO]"}]},"AEtbFm_CjE0":{"wy":"Shared as nightmare fuel in threads on diving and underwater work to show how deadly pressure differentials are.","q":{"x":"Pressure differentials are a constant threat in commercial diving operations.","i":17058755,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":40404520,"author":"misswaterfairy","month":"2024-05","story":"Sucked feet first down the tube (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Warns of pressure differential danger","quote":"Delta-P is not to be messed with.","text":"This guy is incredibly lucky. I hope that council worker was taken to task over it. Delta-P is not to be messed with. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17058755,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2018-05","story":"The Weird, Dangerous, Isolated Life of the Saturation Diver","stance":"reference","why":"Example of pressure differential accidents in diving","quote":"Pressure differentials are a constant threat in commercial diving operations.","text":"Similar accidents happen in the water. Pressure differentials are a constant threat in commercial diving operations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14045342,"author":"josefx","month":"2017-04","story":"Nuclear plant shutdowns tied to coal pollution, decreased birth weights","stance":"reference","why":"Video on large forces from pressure difference on divers","quote":"It is the pressure difference that results on a large force on any unlucky diver.","text":"Here an actuall video on the topic: [VIDEO] . It is the pressure difference that results on a large force on any unlucky diver."},{"id":12970344,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2016-11","story":"Mystery over Dutch WW2 shipwrecks vanished from Java Sea bed","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates danger of pressure differences underwater","quote":"Help yourself to some Delta P nightmare fuel","text":"I've helped float a 70ft steel wreck off of the Caribbean Sea floor using second hand airbags that cost no more than a few thousand dollars (EDIT: You'll want to spend a bit more if this isn't just for fun). Physics is awesome, and the only cost is your time and the materials. Also, if you were only interested in the most valuable materials on the vessel that are accessible without entering a confined space, you'd just need a scuba rig (very low barrier to entry). I would definitely not attempt extensive disassembly underwater. Far more dangerous than floating the vessel and breaking it on shore, dry dock, low tide. EDIT: I thought I'd add why its not safe underwater. Help yourself to some Delta P nightmare fuel: [VIDEO] (also confined spaces, unstable underwater structures, etc)"}]},"Gflpf0DrCgw":{"wy":"Cited as an example of poor-quality knockoff products sold on Amazon, not recommended as a watch.","m":[{"id":33738832,"author":"astura","month":"2022-11","story":"Almost everything on Amazon is becoming an ad","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"is a poorly made Chinese knockoff that can be raked.","text":"Plenty of complete shit get labeled as \"Amazon Choice.\" For example, \"Amazon Choice\" for \"high security padlocks\" is a poorly made Chinese knockoff that can be raked. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24005692,"author":"astura","month":"2020-07","story":"How Best Buy saved itself (2019)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"is actually a Chinese knockoff [2] but you wouldn't know that unless you are familiar with the original.","text":"Would you know if you purchased a fake yeti tumbler[1], for example, if you didn't have a real one to compare to side-by-side? [1] [LINK] The \"Amazon's Choice\" for \"high security lock\" is actually a Chinese knockoff [2] but you wouldn't know that unless you are familiar with the original. [2] [VIDEO] Even people dying doesn't seem to get products removed [LINK] >A 23-year-old man in Missouri purchased a motorcycle helmet from Amazon that was at the time listed as certified as meeting US Department of Transportation safety standards. Later that year, however, he was killed in a crash while riding. A federal investigation later found that the helmet did not meet DOT standards and was recalled. The WSJ, however, found the product still for sale, with an active listing promising compliance, until the WSJ contacted Amazon to inquire about it."},{"id":20500408,"author":"mey","month":"2019-07","story":"Amazon Warns Customers: Those Supplements Might Be Fake","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"LockPickingLawyer (Youtube) has a series of videos on the poor quality of","text":"LockPickingLawyer (Youtube) has a series of videos on the poor quality of \"Amazon Choice\" for various locks. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] (Branded Amazon Basics) [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":19257985,"author":"toufiqbarhamov","month":"2019-02","story":"FTC Brings First Case Challenging Fake Paid Reviews on an Indie Retail Website","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Par for the Amazon course, cheap Chinese knockoffs.","text":"Nothing about Amazon’s abuse of their customers surprises me anymore. Their “Amazon’s Recommemdation” is a joke too, and they’re associating their brand with garbage. [VIDEO] Par for the Amazon course, cheap Chinese knockoffs. If they had anything other than a highly permissive refund system, they’d already be drowning in lawsuits. They must bleed refund money though, and I thought Bezos was all about right margins?"}]},"IX_d_vMKswE":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a funny but precise take on deterrence strategy and politics.","q":{"x":"This sketch is a humorous but precise assessment of this strategic issue","i":7998926,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":49336656,"author":"einpoklum","month":"2026-08","story":"Anthropic's 'Watermark' Text Adulteration in Claude Is a Perversion of Writing","stance":"reference","why":"Sketch about last resort in nuclear deterrence","quote":"Reminds me a bit of that 'Yes Prime Minister' sketch about nuclear deterrant","text":"It's very weird looking in from the outside. I mean, sure, the US is the dominant power and everything, so things like Iraq and Iran could be construed as just collateral damage from their imperial maneuvers. But it has just piled on, more and more, and even when they are hit right in the face with the massive bombing of NordStream, still practically nobody tries to draw any sort of line. Reminds me a bit of that 'Yes Prime Minister' sketch about nuclear deterrant, when the skeptical conversant asks the PM: \"So what is the last resort, Picadilli?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":48097175,"author":"upboundspiral","month":"2026-05","story":"Iran mulls taking control of all 7 cables passing through Strait of Hormuz","stance":"reference","why":"Episode discusses tackling unemployment through defense spending","quote":"is eerily close to what they discussed in an episode of Yes Prime Minister in the 80s.","text":"Your solution (of tackling unemployment by tacking it onto national defense), is eerily close to what they discussed in an episode of Yes Prime Minister in the 80s. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":8589546,"author":"RegW","month":"2014-11","story":"SpaceX Will Announce Micro-Satellites for Low Cost Internet Within Three Months","stance":"reference","why":"Explains deterrence logic better, relevant to states tampering with satellites","quote":"A better explanation in \"Yes, Prime Minister\"","text":"A better explanation in \"Yes, Prime Minister\": [VIDEO] ... and perhaps relevant to the discussions of states tampering with other people's satellites."},{"id":7998926,"author":"rollo","month":"2014-07","story":"Germany turns up pressure in suspected US spy case","stance":"reference","why":"Sketch as humorous but precise assessment of nuclear strategy","quote":"This sketch is a humorous but precise assessment of this strategic issue","text":"It's precisely the other way arround. This sketch is a humorous but precise assessment of this strategic issue: [VIDEO] Relying on nuclear deterrent against modern hybrid warfare means you will lose."},{"id":6701537,"author":"icebraining","month":"2013-11","story":"Can the Defense Budget Shrink Without Risking National Security?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Yes, Prime Minister has a funny scene about the nuclear deterrent","text":"So what would you do if a country with nuclear capabilities started attacking the US with conventional forces? Yes, Prime Minister has a funny scene about the nuclear deterrent: [VIDEO]"}]},"KpPE85Jogjw":{"wy":"HN posts it as a joke whenever a company pushes intrusive advertising or metaverse monetization.","m":[{"id":49074962,"author":"sandreas","month":"2026-07","story":"BMWs shows in-car ads for Spiderman","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think the only way vendors will learn that this is not acceptable is people not buying their stuff any more. In my opinion Windows has pushed \"slop\" too far and is now experiencing lowering market share and I really hope this happens to all the companies trying a B055man69 move[1] on their customers. 1: Nolan Sorrento, Ready Player One, [VIDEO]"},{"id":44815690,"author":"kayge","month":"2025-08","story":"Genie 3: A new frontier for world models","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"we estimate we can sell up to 80% of an individual's visual field before inducing seizures!","text":"Hopefully they'll skip this[0] part: \"...once we can rollback some of Halliday's ad restrictions, we estimate we can sell up to 80% of an individual's visual field before inducing seizures!\" [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28676395,"author":"Datenstrom","month":"2021-09","story":"Facebook invests $50M to build the 'metaverse' in responsible manner","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Actual footage from the Facebook board meeting","text":"Actual footage from the Facebook board meeting discussing responsibly building the 'metaverse'. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27923488,"author":"jareklupinski","month":"2021-07","story":"Even if you’re paying, you’re still the product","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"i guess 81% is the end :P","text":"\"We estimate we can sell up to 80% of an individual's visual field before inducing seizures.\" [VIDEO] i guess 81% is the end :P"},{"id":21090279,"author":"franky47","month":"2019-09","story":"Facebook Horizon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminds me of Nolan Sorrento's desire to increase advertising space in the Oasis","text":"The fact that Facebook is behind it reminds me of Nolan Sorrento's desire to increase advertising space in the Oasis [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"ONZcjs1Pjmk":{"wy":"Cited as the canonical demonstration of pulling out invisible signals like pulse from video, with commenters imagining other applications.","q":{"x":"they amplified color changes. So the tiny flushes caused by the heartbeat were clearly visible","i":9754377,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":16200221,"author":"lowdest","month":"2018-01","story":"User succumbs to a seizure in virtual reality while other players can only watch","stance":"reference","why":"Technique could reduce strobing amplitude","quote":"A similar technique to reduce the amplitude of strobing seems plausible.","text":">What can we hack together that can be used to filter seizures? I think a video filter could do this. See this video on Eulerian Video Amplification from SIGGRAPH 2012: [VIDEO] A similar technique to reduce the amplitude of strobing seems plausible."},{"id":14493464,"author":"titojankowski","month":"2017-06","story":"No one gives a fuck about Climate Change","stance":"reference","why":"Magnifies tiny changes, possible climate application","quote":"Reminds me of those visualizations designed for magnifying tiny changes","text":"Reminds me of those visualizations designed for magnifying tiny changes, Eulerian Video Magnification: [VIDEO] Perhaps a potential application?"},{"id":9754377,"author":"raverbashing","month":"2015-06","story":"A New Algorithm Reveals the Hidden World of Imperceptible Motion","stance":"reference","why":"Amplified color changes make heartbeat flushes visible","quote":"they amplified color changes. So the tiny flushes caused by the heartbeat were clearly visible","text":"I remember a similar technique (maybe it's from the same people) where they amplified color changes. So the tiny flushes caused by the heartbeat were clearly visible [VIDEO] (from 1m30s)"},{"id":5745384,"author":"pgeorgi","month":"2013-05","story":"Xbox, A New Generation Revealed (livesteam)","stance":"reference","why":"MIT paper video amplifying features like blood flow","quote":"video to MIT paper submitted to SIGGRAPH about emphasizing video features, blood flow among them","text":"\"read your heartrate\" reminded me of [VIDEO] (video to MIT paper submitted to SIGGRAPH about emphasizing video features, blood flow among them)"},{"id":4432912,"author":"pstuart","month":"2012-08","story":"Your Brain Can Be Hacked","stance":"reference","why":"Detecting pulse from video","quote":"detecting a persons pulse rate from video","text":"Between Paul Ekman's work in behavioral science and detecting a persons pulse rate from video ( [VIDEO] ), it seems like one could have a pretty decent \"lie detector\" running on a smart phone."}]},"PSxihhBzCjk":{"wy":"Used as a joke or shorthand reference when HN discusses the next big technology or career trend.","m":[{"id":22864648,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: How to rediscover the joy of programming?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I deleted my rather cynical post, about how we can \"age out\" of frontline programming, and will replace it with this rather sunnier treatise: Open source work. I was a manager for over 25 years. I'm a really good manager. I could probably be an ideal CTO. But I don't love it. I love writing code. As my management track progressed, I found my technical work deprecating. It did teach me to be a lot more \"strategic,\" and \"architectural\" in my approach, but I found that I was sitting down and banging out code less and less. To make things worse, I kept reading about how the tools and techniques were progressing to levels far beyond what I had learned. I didn't have a \"shower clause\" in my employment contract (where the employer insists that they own the ideas that you come up with in the shower before work), so I was free to write software in an extracurricular manner, as long as I didn't use employer time and/or resources to do so. So I started to do open source. In the beginning (about 25 years ago), there wasn't a good infrastructure for shared configuration management, so most of the work I did was published \"after the fact\" (otherwise known as \"deliverable\"). I would make the source available, \"on the side.\" Nowadays, with things like GitHub, GitLab and BitBucket, you can actually run a distributed collaborative open-source (or closed-source) project, with the process open. It also works great for single-engineer projects. The result, these days, is that I have a GIGANTIC po"},{"id":37082798,"author":"refulgentis","month":"2023-08","story":"Llama 2 on ONNX runs locally","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"They still do. HN is way behind on ONNX and I'd go so far as to say it's the \"Plastics.\"[1] of 2023. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7587111,"author":"nymph","month":"2014-04","story":"Bend It, Charge It, Dunk It: Graphene, the Material of Tomorrow","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Benjamin, I want to say one word to you. Just one word … ” \"Yes … What is is, Mr. McGuire?” \"Plastics.” [VIDEO]"}]},"QCIo8e12sBM":{"wy":"Cited as an early expert critique of Model 3 fit, finish and manufacturing quality, and for its note on hard-to-find emergency door releases.","q":{"x":"reported really poor fit and finish quality - rattling doors, poorly fitted gaps around the trunk","i":16548634,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":29547684,"author":"fionaellie","month":"2021-12","story":"Toyota owners have to pay $8/month to keep using their key fob for remote start","stance":"reference","why":"Shows hard-to-find emergency release; none in back seat.","quote":"it's quite hard to find, and if you're in the back seat, there's no emergency mechanism at all.","text":"From this video, I learned that it's quite hard to find, and if you're in the back seat, there's no emergency mechanism at all. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16548634,"author":"michaelt","month":"2018-03","story":"Early owners of Tesla's Model 3 are reporting quality problems","stance":"reference","why":"Review reporting poor fit and finish on Model 3.","quote":"reported really poor fit and finish quality - rattling doors, poorly fitted gaps around the trunk","text":"That's consistent with the review by Sandy Munro [1] who reported really poor fit and finish quality - rattling doors, poorly fitted gaps around the trunk, and so on. Computer gamers know the perils of pre-ordering - you've got no recourse if the product has reliability, quality or completeness problems. I hope Tesla enthusiasts who pre-ordered realised the gamble they were taking. [1] [VIDEO] and [LINK]"},{"id":16869060,"author":"Jasper_","month":"2018-04","story":"Tesla switching to 24/7 shifts to push for more model 3s by June","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The Model 3's panel gaps are quite well-known among car owners.","text":"> Most of the design tolerances of the Model 3 are already better than any other car in the world. Really? The Model 3's panel gaps are quite well-known among car owners. One example from Munro and Associates: [VIDEO] How can Elon say this with a straight face?"},{"id":16548655,"author":"influx","month":"2018-03","story":"Early owners of Tesla's Model 3 are reporting quality problems","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Convinced me that Tesla has some major issues in manufacturing.","text":"This teardown previously on HN: [VIDEO] Convinced me that Tesla has some major issues in manufacturing. To get into a Model 3 with most of the features people want is closer to 45K, at which point you could get a pretty damn good car from another manufacturer. I'm cheering for Tesla, but come on, fit and finish should be on par with the best, not something I should have to overlook."}]},"QtgVxGkrX1Y":{"wy":"Used as primary footage when commenters need to check exactly what was said, in threads on COVID misinformation and trusting authorities.","q":{"x":"You can literally watch him come up with the idea all on his own in real time.","i":28853656,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28853656,"author":"parrellel","month":"2021-10","story":"Why “Trusting the Science” Is Complicated","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of Trump improvising the disinfectant idea","quote":"You can literally watch him come up with the idea all on his own in real time.","text":"[VIDEO] I mean, we can all wonder what was going through the man's head, but there you have it. You can literally watch him come up with the idea all on his own in real time."},{"id":24502057,"author":"slavak","month":"2020-09","story":"American Airlines Bans Wearing Too Much Personal Protection Equipment on Board","stance":"reference","why":"Primary footage of the briefing and Trump's remarks in context","quote":"All of this is on video","text":"No, it objectively isn't. The context of Trump's comments was a presentation by Bill Bryan from the Department of Homeland security on how to fight COVID-19, in which he explicitly discussed that a) The virus dies quickest in the presence of direct sunlight. and b) Readily available household disinfectants are effective at killing the virus. He specifically mentions that bleach and isopropyl alcohol had been tested. This is the immediate context in which Trump's comments about putting light inside the body and injecting disinfectant were made. There is absolutely no doubt what he meant here. Stop trying to gaslight people. All of this is on video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22969544,"author":"fiblye","month":"2020-04","story":"Ultraviolet Irradiation of Blood: “The Cure That Time Forgot”? (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of Trump's remarks about injecting disinfectants","quote":"the president did say it's worth trying out injecting disinfectants.","text":"Immediately following someone stating that disinfectants like alcohol and bleach can destroy the virus, the president did say it's worth trying out injecting disinfectants. [1] He didn't specifically say \"mainlining bleach\", but he was referencing injecting one of the aforementioned disinfectants. It's kind of hard to say he didn't. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22977059,"author":"strategarius","month":"2020-04","story":"YouTube bans coronavirus-related content that directly contradicts WHO advice","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of the president suggesting injecting disinfectant","quote":"Should YouTube ban the interview with the President of the United States","text":"Should YouTube ban the interview with the President of the United States, where he suggests injecting desinfectant or hand sanitizer to treat COVID-19? [VIDEO]"}]},"UZ1XLqc5IUg":{"wy":"Cited critically as evidence that early Autopilot 2 was unsafe and overpromised.","m":[{"id":15575200,"author":"bsaul","month":"2017-10","story":"Waymo Will Test Self-Driving Cars in Snowy Detroit","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"not driving properly on regular roads under the sun","text":"Your comment made me wonder whether i hadn't miss anything : are car actually able to drive themselves in clear condition at the moment ? I've seen the demos of the start ups in the valley, the local experiments, but i'm still waiting for a single real-world deployment, even limited to local driving, such as city to city taxi driving, or item delivery. On the other hand, i did hear of the trouble with tesla's autopilot \"2\" not driving properly on regular roads under the sun ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":14600390,"author":"haberman","month":"2017-06","story":"Chris Lattner: “Turns out that Tesla isn't a good fit for me”","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla strikes me as over-promising and under-delivering here.","text":"I just saw their latest marketing literature on their website. They promise: > All Tesla vehicles produced in our factory, including Model 3, have the hardware needed for full self-driving capability at a safety level substantially greater than that of a human driver. [LINK] It seems somewhat irresponsible to promise that you have enough hardware to achieve something that nobody has ever achieved before, with any hardware. Tesla has plenty of impressive-looking videos on their website of autopilot working well, but if you Google for \"Tesla autopilot fail\" you can also find plenty of stuff like this, which is just surprisingly bad: [VIDEO] Tesla strikes me as over-promising and under-delivering here. I wouldn't want to work in a division like that."},{"id":14024948,"author":"owenversteeg","month":"2017-04","story":"The customer is always wrong: Tesla lets out self-driving car data when it suits","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the car literally crossed to the wrong side of the road in nine seconds!","text":"Elsewhere on HN, someone posted this video: [VIDEO] This was my comment reply: Wow. That guy is going nineteen miles per hour on a simple road in daylight and the car literally crossed to the wrong side of the road in nine seconds! That's an absolute disaster."},{"id":14025928,"author":"revelation","month":"2017-04","story":"Ford tops the list of automated driving leaders in new study","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"if they are willing to release this dangerous crap","text":"This is the leaders performance on actual production hardware outside of fancy (postprocessed) videos with their \"cameras only\" approach: [VIDEO] At this point I don't even care if they theorethically are the best, if they are willing to release this dangerous crap they are sooner going to go up in a big publicity fire than get actual autonomy to customers."},{"id":14196243,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-04","story":"Alphabet's Self-Driving Cars to Get Their First Real Riders","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That's terrible performance.","text":"That's terrible performance. The problem seems to be that Tesla's system is extremely dependent on a nice clear white line at the outer edge of the road. On this road, near the traffic islands, the roadside grass sometimes overgrows the road edge and obscures the white line. The white line has also been scuffed by cars near the edge of the road. [1] The Tesla runs off the road in that situation. There are two kinds of self-driving. One came up from the DARPA Grand Challenge, which was off-road. That kind first looks at the terrain and obstacles, and figures out where it can physically drive safely. There's LIDAR profiling of terrain. Then it looks at road markings and figures out where it's supposed to go. If the road markings lead into an obstacle or drop-off, it will stop or go around the obstacle. That's Waymo. The second kind came up from lane-keeping and auto-brake systems. Those are very dependent on highway markings, and only work right on freeways. That's Tesla's original system. It's hard to tell about the others. Volvo has an extensive sensor suite. Otto seems to be mostly a lane-keeping system for freeways. Uber hasn't released much detail. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13880608,"author":"revelation","month":"2017-03","story":"Tesla to raise $1B","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"AP2 is truly dangerous.","text":"AP2 is truly dangerous. It's incomprehensible they would release something like this: [VIDEO] I guess it's a good reality check for autonomous cars when you take away the LIDAR toys. One rather important point is that the car often doesn't realize it is screwing up, because quelle surprise, realizing you don't have a correctly detected lane can be just as difficult as detecting one in the first place."}]},"VMqcLUqYqrs":{"wy":"Cited as a quick reference to that Newsroom monologue when a thread touches on American decline.","m":[{"id":23864419,"author":"SECProto","month":"2020-07","story":"TSMC plans to halt chip supplies to Huawei in 2 months","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Or just watch the opening from the Newsroom.","text":"Don't make a bomb joke in a US airport either! They weren't comparing the US to China, they were explaining the flaws with American democracy/judiciary/media. The US is not a shining beacon to be held up in those categories compared to eg New Zealand/Germany (proportional representation), Denmark (judicial independence - the US doesn't crack the top ten[1]), or most of the rest of the West (see how far you have to scroll in press freedom rankings to find the US [2] - they have very weak media ownership rules, and while the media is free from a lot of govt limitations, it's free to be biased and partisan as hell) Or just watch the opening from the Newsroom. [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28619107,"author":"manuel_w","month":"2021-09","story":"When McDonalds Came to Denmark","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It's the most powerful nation on earth and runs laps around everyone else combined on pretty much every metric. Guess you're talking about number of incarcerated citizens per capita, number of adults who believe angels are real, and defense spending? :-) [VIDEO]"},{"id":15221154,"author":"Markoff","month":"2017-09","story":"Make Mark Zuckerberg Testify","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"leading the free world? you really mean US is some leader of free world? in what filed exactly? living standard, healthcare, education, internet, etc? because I fail to see pretty much any positive field in which US would be world leader. [VIDEO]"}]},"_LjN3UclYzU":{"wy":"Used as a joke whenever a thread touches on missile guidance or Kalman filters.","m":[{"id":46066536,"author":"kragen","month":"2025-11","story":"Coq: The World's Best Macro Assembler? (2013) [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"has a different attempt to formalize Kalman filters which I think we can all agree was not a successful formalization","text":"I'm curious about how you'd do that, too. I haven't tried doing anything like that, but I'd think you'd start by trying to formalize the usual optimality proof for the Kalman filter, transfer it to actual program logic on the assumption that the program manipulates real numbers, try to get the proof to work on floating-point numbers, and finally extract the program from the proof to run it. [VIDEO] has a different attempt to formalize Kalman filters which I think we can all agree was not a successful formalization."},{"id":41187769,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2024-08","story":"Robot Dog with Gun Turret for Hunting Aerial Drones Being Tested by Army","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the missile knows where it is at all times, it knows this because it knows where it isn't","text":"predicting trajectory of a projectile is what computers were first made to do, at least if you count ENIAC as the first, and the Apollo Guidance Computer not long after (the missile knows where it is at all times, it knows this because it knows where it isn't... [0]) as it happens the projectiles have minds of their own now so to predict trajectory will require theory-of-mind shooting down fast moving drones might just be the path to self-awareness in the machine world, \"if I were a drone, what action would I take in that situation...\" [0] [LINK] [0.b remix] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24310545,"author":"superkuh","month":"2020-08","story":"Fox Two: The Story of the Aim-9 Sidewinder (2014)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"a great song about missile guidance systems and knowing where you are by how much you aren't (there)","text":">The crowning touch, however, was wiring the seeker to aim not where the target was, but where it would be. The missile knows where it is, [VIDEO] , a great song about missile guidance systems and knowing where you are by how much you aren't (there)."}]},"aNi17YLnZpg":{"wy":"Cited as a cautionary example whenever collision-avoidance or self-driving safety systems come up.","m":[{"id":16688658,"author":"chillingeffect","month":"2018-03","story":"Uber disabled Volvo SUV's safety system before fatality","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Especially because a Volvo plowed into a pedestrian almost a year ago: [LINK] IIRC it was discovered this happened because the owner hadn't purchased the \"Pedestrian Avoidance Package\". And shortly before that, a Volvo plowed into a parked truck (in a demo): [VIDEO] This was \"was the result of an earlier flat battery that temporarily disabled the system due to low voltage\" [0] The pattern here is not only due to the collision systems themselves, but to the system around the collision avoidance system. [0] [LINK]"},{"id":2793969,"author":"alexitosrv","month":"2011-07","story":"Toyota automatically stops car before it can hit pedestrian","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Does anybody else remember this demostration of a Volvo featuring a similar characteristic and utterly failing? [VIDEO] These technologies need to gain the trust of the drivers before they can become mainstream."},{"id":1778213,"author":"eli_s","month":"2010-10","story":"Google Cars Drive Themselves, in Traffic","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"It will take only a few of these: [VIDEO] (volvo crash avoidance failure) to shake people's confidence in these systems."}]},"d5wfMNNr3ak":{"wy":"Listed as one of several resources for high-carb, low-fat diets in nutrition debates, not heavily endorsed.","m":[{"id":12475320,"author":"eatfruitnoteggs","month":"2016-09","story":"Medical guidelines don’t include a diet low in carbohydrates","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I commend your effort, but don't even try bringing up Veganism on Hacker News. I've tried to do the same and low-carb dogma ideologues lost their minds to the point logical and evidence-based dialogue became impossible. My own theory is, there is too much emotional attachment to eating meat, dairy and eggs in Americans to consider Veganism seriously. They will perform incredible mental gymnastics to justify their violent ideology promoted and ingrained by USDA and Animal Ag. Regarding the NY Times article itself - it is the usual incoherent nonsense, citing how \"USDA began recommending a high-carb, low-fat diet\" as if to imply Americans then started eating whole plant foods, instead of added sugars and refined carbohydrate. Ignored also are the many studies pointing out low-carb diets are not satiating and promote weight gain and death (1, 2, 3, 4, 5), which is obvious to anyone who has had an unbiased look into Atkins \"nightmare\" diets [11]. Who wrote the article? None other than a \"director\" at an organization paid for by Laura and John D. Arnold, which have substantial ties[6] to animal agriculture industry (Another poster in this thread linked to a website trying to discredit Dr. Greger which the Arnold Foundation also paid for.) The low-carb ideologues will vehemently deny the current scientific consensus that we should derive a majority of our calories from carbohydrate (7, 8), citing flawed, biased or outright retracted research, akin to deniers of anthropogenic climat"},{"id":12281011,"author":"lucidguppy","month":"2016-08","story":"Machine Learning and Ketosis","stance":"reference","why":"One of several resources on high-carb vegan diets","quote":"More resources","text":"Carbohydrates do not make you fat. I am eating a high carb vegan diet and have lost 63 pounds since last October. My weight loss: [LINK] I do not restrict the amount of food I eat. I snack and have big meals. Carbs do not make you fat. Eating high caloric density foods makes you fat. More resources: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":7224367,"author":"Spoygg","month":"2014-02","story":"John Yudkin: the man who tried to warn us about sugar","stance":"reference","why":"One of several references for a high-carb, low-fat diet","quote":"","text":"Thanks for honest answer :) It's interesting how there is so much evidence for whatever approach you take on foods :D I guess one can only try and see how anything works for him. Well, there is no consensus about common name and there are more than one approaches to this kind of eating (high carb, low fat, low protein) I will list you some references so you can see what I'm talking about. In no special order: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"}]},"fMeH7wqOwXA":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of maintainer burden and why companies benefit from upstreaming drivers, as an explanation straight from a lead kernel maintainer.","q":{"x":"Greg Kroah-Hartman explains it","i":38494502,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38494502,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2023-12","story":"A Nouveau graphics driver update","stance":"reference","why":"Kroah-Hartman explains why upstream drivers win","quote":"Greg Kroah-Hartman explains it","text":"Because it leads to straight up better products and companies that don't upstream the drivers get outcompeted by the ones that do. Greg Kroah-Hartman explains it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37680476,"author":"jamespwilliams","month":"2023-09","story":"Linux Kernel Maintainers, why are they so grumpy? [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Video version of the slides talk","quote":"for a video version of this talk","text":"[VIDEO] for a video version of this talk One funny bit about the talk, which isn’t mentioned in the slides, is that he actually gave the talk at Linaro (the very same company who were called out in the slides as a case study of submitting a particularly bad set of patches)"},{"id":35254404,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2023-03","story":"Intel Thunder Bay Is Officially Canceled, Linux Driver Code to Be Removed","stance":"reference","why":"Why upstreaming drivers benefits companies","quote":"That's how good corporations contribute.","text":"Because then consumers get hardware that works out of the box on Linux. That's how good corporations contribute. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28732392,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2021-10","story":"Facebook Struggles to Quell Uproar over Instagram’s Effect on Teens","stance":"reference","why":"Kroah-Hartman on amateur versus professional contributors","quote":"I'm reminded of Greg Kroah-Hartman's talk about kernel contributors.","text":"> it's not the publishing but the use which is proscribed - same applies for engineering Exactly. I think there should be an exception for free and open source software though. We have lots of free software designed to be used by people. I don't think these programmers should be held liable. Many of them are amateurs and volunteers. The chilling effect would simply be too great. I'm reminded of Greg Kroah-Hartman's talk about kernel contributors. [VIDEO] > My goal, of me, as a developer and to make Linux succeed, is to help [amateurs, unknown individuals] out. > If you're hired to do this stuff, you're on your own and you better know what you're doing. I think he's right. At the time, roughly 20% of the kernel was done by individuals. That work is extremely valuable yet it is different from professional work. It should be treated differently. with a lot more compassion and understanding. On the other hand, it is fine to expect professionals to be disciplined. > A social-media site operated by an amateur in the bedroom being paid nothing and using only open-source software would still be liable under any sensible and realistic regulation. Yeah, I agree. That's not just software anymore, that's a service. > e.g. create a non-profit, and pay the non-profit which pays the programmer. So long as the programmer is one step removed from you it's fine. It doesn't matter how indirect it is. The important fact is the programmer is getting paid for their work. That makes it professional"},{"id":27631742,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2021-06","story":"“Please don't waste maintainers' time on your KPI grabbing patches”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> maybe it's time to have sub-maintainers to whom such tasks could be delegated They already have subsystem maintainers. The result was an even faster process. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27631901,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2021-06","story":"“Please don't waste maintainers' time on your KPI grabbing patches”","stance":"reference","why":"Kernel expectations for paid versus volunteer contributors","quote":"If you're hired to do this stuff, you're on your own, you better know what you're doing.","text":"[VIDEO] > If you're hired to do this stuff, you're on your own, you better know what you're doing. Huawei pays professional developers to contribute to the kernel. Of course they should be held to a higher standard. They should be working on something substantial, not fixing minor problems. Surely there are far more important things to work on. Failing to prioritize important issues coupled with incentives for kernel contribution means they are putting in minimum effort for maximum personal gain at the expense of maintainers."},{"id":17110979,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2018-05","story":"Libui: a portable GUI library for C","stance":"reference","why":"Maintainer time is scarce","quote":"They're scarce resources.","text":">implies that the requester's time is less valuable than the project maintainer's It is. In large projects, there are relatively few maintainers responsible for evaluating the requests, reports and code from lots of people. They're scarce resources. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14974655,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2017-08","story":"Why GitHub Can't Host the Linux Kernel Community","stance":"reference","why":"Kroah-Hartman on kernel contributions","quote":"There was in fact a talk given by Greg Kroah-Hartman, one Linux's lead maintainers, about development for the Linux kernel","text":"\"They don't need or even want your help\" There was in fact a talk given by Greg Kroah-Hartman, one Linux's lead maintainers, about development for the Linux kernel which he titled \"I Don't Want Your Code\" . [VIDEO]"}]},"jKS3MGriZcs":{"wy":"Mentioned critically, as evidence that the show's jokes are weak and lean on canned laughter.","m":[{"id":46262111,"author":"netsharc","month":"2025-12","story":"Online Piracy Can Boost Box Office Revenue, Study Suggests","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Might as well add pause for laughter like the Big Bang Theory.","text":"But American comedy movies are shit nowadays, it's just a bunch of comedians and Will Ferrell doing one-liners. Might as well add pause for laughter like the Big Bang Theory. [VIDEO] As for drama, I'd like to watch Sentimental Value, but I imagine my eyes will be wet in many scenes, I'd rather do that at home than in a theater."},{"id":36361123,"author":"SV_BubbleTime","month":"2023-06","story":"The note Reddit sent to moderators threatening them if they don’t reopen","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It highlights that the scripts are usually pretty bad.","text":"It doesn’t work. The pauses are built into the script. It’s worse, it’s far worse. It highlights that the scripts are usually pretty bad. Classic example of a completely unfunny basic show [VIDEO]"},{"id":6865588,"author":"dl8","month":"2013-12","story":"Peter Higgs: I wouldn't be productive enough for today's academic system","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it's generally not funny","text":"The main thing I don't like about BBT is that it's generally not funny. Look how the show holds without its laugh track: [VIDEO]"}]},"kHI7RTKhlz0":{"wy":"Linked whenever lines of code or developer productivity metrics are debated.","q":{"x":"Former Microsoft CEO Steve Ballmer on useless performance metrics","i":25225814,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":46856733,"author":"Findecanor","month":"2026-02","story":"Claude Code is suddenly everywhere inside Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"Ballmer on IBM's KLOC counting culture.","quote":"Steve Ballmer on IBM and KLoC's","text":"If so, it hasn't always been that way. Steve Ballmer on IBM and KLoC's: [VIDEO] (I think it is from \"Triumph of the Nerds\" (1996), but I can't find the time code)"},{"id":25225814,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-11","story":"Microsoft's Creepy New 'Productivity Score' Gamifies Workplace Surveillance","stance":"reference","why":"Ballmer explains IBM's KLOC obsession and why lines of code are useless as a metric.","quote":"Former Microsoft CEO Steve Ballmer on useless performance metrics","text":"Former Microsoft CEO Steve Ballmer on useless performance metrics[1][2]: >> In IBM there's a religion in software that says you have to count K-LOCs, and a K-LOC is a thousand line of code. How big a project is it? Oh, it's sort of a 10K-LOC project. This is a 20K-LOCer. And this is 5OK-LOCs. And IBM wanted to sort of make it the religion about how we got paid. How much money we made off OS 2, how much they did. How many K-LOCs did you do? And we kept trying to convince them - hey, if we have - a developer's got a good idea and he can get something done in 4K-LOCs instead of 20K-LOCs, should we make less money? Because he's made something smaller and faster, less KLOC. K-LOCs, K-LOCs, that's the methodology. Ugh anyway, that always makes my back just crinkle up at the thought of the whole thing. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16158861,"author":"protomyth","month":"2018-01","story":"Why do I do this to myself? Bob's first predictions for 2018 – I, Cringely","stance":"reference","why":"Ballmer on KLOCs from Triumph of the Nerds.","quote":"the part with Steve Balmer talking about KLOCs is funny and true","text":"Triumph of the Nerds: The Rise of Accidental Empires is his and is pretty good. I must admit that the part with Steve Balmer talking about KLOCs is funny and true [VIDEO]"},{"id":12374236,"author":"protomyth","month":"2016-08","story":"G.E., the 124-Year-Old Software Start-Up","stance":"reference","why":"Ballmer on measuring programmers by lines of code.","quote":"Say what you want about Steve Balmer but he had the right attitude towards that","text":"> he didn't feel like they wrote enough lines of code. Say what you want about Steve Balmer but he had the right attitude towards that [VIDEO] As to the rest, I get the feeling that a lot of players in the market that now need software have the exact same tales being told about them. It is going to be a wonderment to me if I will be about to get through a day in 10 years without a IoT failure."},{"id":12378798,"author":"ben_jones","month":"2016-08","story":"Why bad scientific code beats code following “best practices” (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Ballmer on KLOCs as a metric.","quote":"LOC value is certainly debatable...","text":"Steve Ballmer on \"KLOCs\" [1]. Not saying you're taking that extreme but LOC value is certainly debatable... [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"kO5EXjFKE7U":{"wy":"Used as a reference timeline in arguments over whether information was suppressed, with commenters noting a possible pro-China bias.","m":[{"id":22795225,"author":"megous","month":"2020-04","story":"Pentagon Knew of Possible Coronavirus Threat for Years","stance":"reference","why":"Chinese-leaning timeline of verifiable events, possibly biased","quote":"The guy may be a bit biased towards China","text":"Chinese timeline: [VIDEO] The guy may be a bit biased towards China, but all of that should be verifiable information, so if there's bias it will come through via omissions, or cherry-picking, or playing up insignificant events."},{"id":22606584,"author":"blackoil","month":"2020-03","story":"Chinese scientists destroyed proof of virus in December?","stance":"reference","why":"Timeline suggests info about virus was not suppressed","quote":"If the timeline in","text":"If the timeline in [VIDEO] are true, doesn't seem like the information was suppressed. WHO knew about a new virus/disease by 31st Dec/1st Jan and the market in question was closed. What they didn't know was if these were one off cases from eating bad meat or is it spreading human to human. Also, Li Wenliang circulated SARS info on WeChat on 30th/31st and public broadcast in Wuhan/Hubei was on 31st. Possible the source is biased and info. is missing but doesn't look any info is false."},{"id":22407736,"author":"FooBarWidget","month":"2020-02","story":"Coronavirus and the blindness of authoritarianism","stance":"reference","why":"Coronavirus timeline used to counter claims of suppression","quote":"Here is the coronavirus timeline","text":"But did they really sweep things under the rug or is that just what people casually like to say without fact checking? Here is the coronavirus timeline: [VIDEO]"}]},"kwnWfHq2EfQ":{"wy":"HN cites it only for specific moments, such as the live Spanner resize demo, the Jamboard demo and the Titan announcement.","m":[{"id":15094400,"author":"bluegate010","month":"2017-08","story":"Titan in depth: Security in plaintext","stance":"reference","why":"Titan announcement explaining the earring swag","quote":"See the Titan announce video","text":"We've actually got Titan earring swag we'll hopefully start distributing at upcoming recruiting / customer events, so images may start cropping up in short order. Why earrings? See the Titan announce video[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14405021,"author":"chickenbane","month":"2017-05","story":"Jamboard is now available","stance":"reference","why":"Jamboard demo at Next","quote":"you can see them demo it at Next","text":"The Jamboard does way more than that, you can see them demo it at Next: [VIDEO] If you've been in countless business meetings whiteboarding, Jamboard is definitely not outrageous."},{"id":13882533,"author":"vgt","month":"2017-03","story":"Show HN: FaunaDB, a strongly consistent, globally distributed cloud database","stance":"reference","why":"Live demo of resizing Spanner from 70 to 99 nodes","quote":"here's the 15-second live demo of resizing Spanner from 70 to 99 nodes at","text":"If we're on the topic comparing Spanner, here's the 15-second live demo of resizing Spanner from 70 to 99 nodes at [0]. The act itself is quite unremarkable, but the complexity abstracted away is awesome. Both Spanner and Datastore do quite well in the cloud for \"huge scale\" as fully managed services. And with any deployment on-premise, one certainly must manage their own capacity \"by hand\". (work at Google Cloud) [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"mty0RwkPmE8":{"wy":"HN mentions it chiefly for a specific moment where Nolen credits Chris Granger and Light Table, and in a few hiring threads.","m":[{"id":16971940,"author":"g_h","month":"2018-05","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (May 2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"PayGarden | Full-stack | SF | Full Time | ONSITE (preferred, sponsor visa) + REMOTE (some positions) What: With PayGarden merchants can accept major brand gift cards as payment - imagine Stripe, but instead of credit cards, your customers can pay with Starbucks, Walmart, Amazon (& 100 more) gift cards. See our partnership with Watsi [0] turning major brand cards into life-changing healthcare for people, or Private Internet Access [1] making VPN payment anonymous. How: Our systems handle millions of dollars, built primarily with Clojure/Script & a dash of Rust. Us: If you've ever done any work with Clojure, you've likely seen someone from our tiny, incredibly high-leverage team of Xooglers & YC alumni. We've: - contributed to the cljs compiler [2], - built cool open-source Clojure/Scripts apps [3], - given [4] dozens [5] of talks [6] on [7] fun and cutting [8] edge development - run the #sfcljs & ReasonDojo meetups You: Love working with others, taking ownership over business concerns while jumping into other parts of the stack to help out. An internal drive to continuously improve, while remaining humble. jobs@paygarden.com with \"HN\" in the subject, & link us to your favorite project! [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] - OneGraph [5] [LINK] - Unikernels in the real world [6] [LINK] - Truly full-stack apps in OCaml [7] [LINK] - Generating & Running 1,000,000 Selenium tests in 60 minutes [8] [LINK] - Polymorphic Codebases"},{"id":16740693,"author":"sgrove","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (April 2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"PayGarden | Full-stack | SF | Full Time | ONSITE (preferred, sponsor visa) + REMOTE (for some positions) What : With PayGarden merchants can accept big-brand gift cards as payment - imagine Stripe, but instead of credit cards, your customers can pay with Starbucks, Walmart, Amazon (& ~100 more) gift cards. See our partnership with Watsi [0] - turning major brand cards into life-changing healthcare for people, or Private Internet Access [1] - making VPN payment anonymous. How : Our systems handle millions of dollars, built primarily with Clojure/Script, ReasonML, & a dash of Rust. Us : If you've ever done any work with Clojure or Reason, you've likely seen someone from our tiny, incredibly high-leverage team of Xooglers & YC alumni. We've: - contributed to the cljs compiler [2], - built cool open-source Clojure/Scripts apps [3], - given [4] dozens [5] of talks [6] on [7] fun and cutting [8] edge development - run the #sfcljs & ReasonDojo meetups You : Love working with others, taking ownership over business concerns while jumping into other parts of the stack to help out. An internal drive to continuously improve, while remaining humble. jobs@paygarden.com with \"HN\" in the subject, & link us to your favorite project! [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] - OneGraph [5] [LINK] - Unikernels in the real world [6] [LINK] - Truly full-stack apps in OCaml [7] [LINK] - Generating & Running 1,000,000 Selenium tests in 60 minutes [8] [LINK] - Polymorphic Codebases"},{"id":12823724,"author":"zubairq","month":"2016-10","story":"Eve: Programming designed for humans","stance":"reference","why":"Nolen credits Chris Granger and Light Table","quote":"David Nolen gives a great shoutout to Chris Granger and Eve's predecesor, Light Table at minute 17:00","text":"Actually Chris Granger was heavily involved with Clojure. David Nolen gives a great shoutout to Chris Granger and Eve's predecesor, Light Table at minute 17:00 a couple of weeks ago at the Clojure TRE conference: [VIDEO]"}]},"nt00QzKuNVY":{"wy":"Cited as proof that machines solved the cube in under a second years ago, often as the earlier record before newer robots.","q":{"x":"In 2018, this was solved by some guys (I believe one worked for Boston Dynamics) in 0.38s.","i":40594267,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40593785,"author":"razodactyl","month":"2024-06","story":"Mitsubishi robot solves Rubik's Cube in 0.305s","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier 0.38s solve video","quote":"","text":"> This is a 2015 story. Why is it dated 2024? Edit: Ignore me, I was confused with the .38/s video from 6 years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40594267,"author":"mkhalil","month":"2024-06","story":"Mitsubishi robot solves Rubik's Cube in 0.305s","stance":"reference","why":"2018 0.38s solve with hardware and software details","quote":"In 2018, this was solved by some guys (I believe one worked for Boston Dynamics) in 0.38s.","text":"Sorta related: (if this video triggers some brain neurons from the past) In 2018, this was solved by some guys (I believe one worked for Boston Dynamics) in 0.38s. Video: [VIDEO] Hardware Info: [LINK] Software Info: [LINK]"},{"id":38741920,"author":"jader201","month":"2023-12","story":"Cyberrunner – robot playing Labyrinth board game","stance":"reference","why":"Robots have beaten human cube records","quote":"AI robots have been destroying human records for solving a Rubik’s Cube for a while.","text":"Not sure if this qualifies as a “game”, but AI robots have been destroying human records for solving a Rubik’s Cube for a while. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18062491,"author":"CorpOverreach","month":"2018-09","story":"Self-solving Rubik's Cube","stance":"reference","why":"Machine solves cube in under a second","quote":"There are machines that can solve them in < 1s.","text":"Exactly this! The solving algorithm is no big deal at this point. Experienced cubers can solve them in under 10 seconds. There are machines that can solve them in < 1s. [1] Again, the innovation there is the accuracy and precision of the machinery involved. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"tom6_ceTu9s":{"wy":"HN uses it as shorthand for the vapidness of TED talks and for content that says very little in a lot of words.","m":[{"id":44902215,"author":"nicbou","month":"2025-08","story":"The biggest loss of the modern internet, and how we let it happen","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It sounds like this [1]","text":"Don't change anything on account of my personal taste. Here's a reddit post about the \"LinkedIn writing style\": [0]. It reads like a series of cliffhangers that never lead anywhere. It sounds like this [1] and feels like this [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] Again, that's really just my opinion and you should not take this too seriously."},{"id":43245502,"author":"adityaathalye","month":"2025-03","story":"Thought Leader (2016) [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is uncannily Thought-Leading","text":"Ducks Go Quack, Chickens Say Cluck - Onion Talks - Ep. 3 [VIDEO] YouTube algorithm is right. This is uncannily Thought-Leading, like the original post, but better, because it's shorter by half, and still receives a standing ovation."},{"id":31814125,"author":"preseinger","month":"2022-06","story":"Writing one sentence per line","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"All of his articles are like HTML versions of the Ducks Go Quack TED Talk","text":"One sentence per line makes prose feel sanctimonious, even self-aggrandizing. I find this dude's writing un-readable for exactly this reason. All of his articles are like HTML versions of the Ducks Go Quack TED Talk [0]. I just roll my eyes. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27009529,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2021-05","story":"GitHub repository for Sedgewick's Algorithms is taken down","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"There are some great TED[x] talks, like \"Ducks Go Quack, Chickens Say Cluck\": [VIDEO] And \"How to sound smart in your TEDx Talk\": [LINK]"},{"id":9574211,"author":"jerf","month":"2015-05","story":"The Sound of TED: A Case for Distaste","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it's even more a dead-on satire home run from the Onion","text":"\"TED’s is the language and tone of the pitch. It’s a style that comes from corporate conference rooms, where product ideas are pitched to potential investors.\" Someone in HN linked this in a previous discussion about TED, but especially with that quote in the piece it's even more a dead-on satire home run from the Onion: [VIDEO] Part of a series though my impression was that it's the same basic joke over and over... still, for at least one iteration, it's dead-on, and this is probably the most pure illustration of just the form in that series, with no content, for the author's point."},{"id":9208335,"author":"carsongross","month":"2015-03","story":"The Church of TED","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I will say, this TED talk improved my life immeasurably, if only for two minutes and fifty eight seconds: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8817461,"author":"davekeck","month":"2014-12","story":"TED Is a God Damn Cult (2013)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the linked (and hilarious) Onion Talks video is satire","text":"For reference, the linked (and hilarious) Onion Talks video is satire: [VIDEO] But the blog post itself isn't. Or at least I'm not sure how quotes like \"TED Talks are designed more to create an experience of feeling enlightened than to actually enlighten\" could be considered satire."},{"id":5490754,"author":"_delirium","month":"2013-04","story":"O'Reilly on  Morozov","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion parody floating around [1] caricatures some of the rhetorical style pretty well.","text":"I agree TED is a bit of a nexus for it. In my circles I see a sort of backlash (or maybe, better, buyer's remorse) directed at TED being fairly broad, hitting many people who wouldn't read more than a few sentences of Morozov. But then Morozov thrusts a dagger into that wound for a subset of people. For people who never liked any of that stuff and just think society is going downhill with all this technological nonsense, Morozov has less appeal, because you've already got Nicholas Carr and many other people for that angle. You mention ideas vs. packaging/motives, but I think I've gotten more skeptical about the style of idea as well. Skepticism of the marketing/book/speaking-tour angle probably plays a role in that, but the ideas increasingly seem sort of fluffy. I like to think TED was better back when I was more positive on it, but I've re-watched a few older talks I recall liking, and they seem to have aged poorly, at least for me. I don't have anything against popularizing science, but I can't find much in the TED archives anymore that reminds me of what I think of as good popularized science, up to the gold standard of a Carl Sagan. It more often reminds me of motivational speakers and self-help gurus, the kinds of ideas you see on a CNN segment or something: new science says [amazing revelation that will change your life] . The Onion parody floating around [1] caricatures some of the rhetorical style pretty well. The style somehow seemed fresh at the time, but looking b"},{"id":5409484,"author":"rm999","month":"2013-03","story":"When TED Lost Control of Its Crowd","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion does a really good series called \"Onion Talks\" that satirizes the vapidness of TED talks.","text":"The Onion does a really good series called \"Onion Talks\" that satirizes the vapidness of TED talks. They also capture the audiences' \"inspired\" reactions pretty well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4889791,"author":"rauljara","month":"2012-12","story":"All the TED Talks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This one is my favorite so far","text":"The Onion recently began parodying TED talks. This one is my favorite so far: [VIDEO] . The Onion parodies are definitely not entirely fair, but they do a good job of pointing out that a lot of TED talks are definitely more on the aspirational side of things. I've definitely seen a few TED talks where the speakers' abilities basically covered up the fact that they weren't actually saying anything. I was kind of heartened to see the onion parodies, because I had wondered if I was the only one whose bs detectors were going off. This isn't to say you should be dismissive of TED talks out of hand, or that they're all crap, or anything. I actually think that if they didn't let a few bs-ers in it would be a sign that they weren't taking enough risks. But it is important to keep in mind that some talks are little more than hand waving. And you definitely shouldn't automatically assume it's brilliant just because it has the TED brand behind it."}]}});
